DELETE FROM unit_detail WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos';
DELETE FROM chapter WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>This is a work of scholarship, and at its close a work of translation. Reza Shah-Kazemi published it in 2006, reading the Shii and Sufi traditions of Islam together and drawing on sources that reach back to the seventh century. Its subject is the spirituality of Ali ibn Abi Talib — cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet Muhammad, fourth caliph, and first of the Shii Imams — and its argument is that in this one figure the inward substance of the Islamic tradition finds an integral expression.</p>
<p>The book moves inward. It opens with Ali''s nearness to the Prophet and the household around him, then turns to what he understood by intellect as distinct from mere reason, and to the certainty that intellect yields. From there it follows his conception of justice — sacred rather than merely legal — through the ordering of society, the claims of the poor, and the sincerity that must underlie every act of rule. Its final movement is devotional: prayer, the remembrance of God, and the extinction of the self in the divine Name.</p>
<p>The closing chapters set commentary aside and let Ali speak for himself. The first sermon of the Nahj al-Balagha and his letter to the governor Malik al-Ashtar are given in full translation, so that the reader may weigh the man against his own words.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'introduction to the book';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>In relation to the Prophet, Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>) was both a living presence and a promise. The epigraph names it: &quot;I am the city of knowledge and Ali is its gate; so whoever desires knowledge, let him enter the gate&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَا مَدِينَةُ الْعِلْمِ وَعَلِيٌّ بَابُهَا، فَمَنْ أَرَادَ الْعِلْمَ فَلْيَأْتِ الْبَابَ</span>).</p>
<p>To speak of Ali ibn Abi Talib — cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet Muhammad, fourth caliph of Islam, and first in the line of Shii Imams — is to speak of the very heart of Islamic spiritual life. In this central figure of Islam''s earliest days, both of its two great sources come together: the revelation of the Quran and the inspired Sunna of the Prophet. Sunna here does not mean simply copying the Prophet''s outward actions — a narrow view that is all too common today — but rather the inner spiritual substance of prophetic perfection, the perfection to which the Quran itself points:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Qalam: 4</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِنَّكَ لَعَلَىٰ خُلُقٍ عَظِيمٍۢ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Verily, thou art of a tremendous nature&quot; (Al-Qalam: 4).</p></blockquote>
<p>The Prophet''s words and deeds give voice to that spiritual substance, yet they do not exhaust it. To take this substance inwardly into oneself, rather than merely copy its outward forms, is the aim of every Muslim drawn to the spiritual life. Between the believer''s soul and the prophetic nature there is a deep kinship, a mysterious closeness that reaches across time and space, as the following verse expresses:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Ahzab: 6</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلنَّبِىُّ أَوْلَىٰ بِٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ مِنْ أَنفُسِهِمْ ۖ وَأَزْوَجُهُۥٓ أُمَّهَتُهُمْ ۗ وَأُو۟لُوا۟ ٱلْأَرْحَامِ بَعْضُهُمْ أَوْلَىٰ بِبَعْضٍۢ فِى كِتَبِ ٱللَّهِ مِنَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَٱلْمُهَجِرِينَ إِلَّآ أَن تَفْعَلُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰٓ أَوْلِيَآئِكُم مَّعْرُوفًۭا ۚ كَانَ ذَلِكَ فِى ٱلْكِتَبِ مَسْطُورًۭا</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;The Prophet is closer to the believers than they are to themselves, and his wives are their mothers; and by the ordinance of God, blood relatives are more entitled to one another than other believers and emigrants, unless you would show kindness to your close associates. This is decreed in the Record&quot; (Al-Ahzab: 6).</p></blockquote>
<p>The Prophet thus shows outwardly the very perfection that the spiritually sensitive believer senses, in some measure, at the core of his or her own soul.</p>
<p>It is no surprise, then, to find the Prophet saying that Ali &quot;is as my own soul&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عليٌّ مِنِّي بِمَنْزِلَةِ نَفْسِي</span></p></blockquote>
<p>This confirms not only the extraordinarily close bond between Ali and the Prophet, but also the kinship — and in the end the oneness — between every sanctified soul and the prophetic nature. More mysterious still, the Prophet said to Ali, &quot;You are from me, and I am from you&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أنْتَ مِنِّی وَ أَنَا مِنْکَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>As for how fully Ali took into himself the substance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوَادّ</span>) of the Quran, there is this saying of the Prophet:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ عَلِيًّا مَعَ الْقُرْآنِ وَالْقُرْآنُ مَعَ عَلِيٍّ، لَنْ يَفْتَرِقَا حَتَّى يَرِدَا عَلَيَّ الْحَوْضَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Ali is with the Quran and the Quran is with Ali. They will not separate from each other until they return to me at the [paradisal] pool (al-hawd).&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>Ali thus took fully within himself both the spirit of the Quran and the soul of the Prophet; and it is exactly this taking-within of the two sources of the Islamic revelation that forms the spiritual path of Islam.</p>
<p>The chief way to study and reflect upon the spirituality of Ali is to engage with the body of teachings handed down under his name. Not every one of the thousands of sayings attributed to him can be assigned to him with confidence; yet the sheer size of this body of work shows that he must have given voice to a great many profound teachings. No other companion of the Prophet left anything close to it. The same holds for the sciences. His founding role in a whole range of them may be open to debate — among them jurisprudence (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِقْهٌ</span>), theology (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَلَامٌ</span>), Quranic exegesis (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَفْسِيرٌ</span>), rhetoric (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلَاغَةٌ</span>), grammar (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَحْوٌ</span>), and calligraphy (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَطٌّ</span>), the mystical knowledge of Sufism, and such hidden sciences as numerology (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَفْرٌ</span>) and alchemy (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْكِيمِيَاءُ</span>) — but the later masters in every one of these fields look back to him as the first impulse behind their discipline, which itself testifies to how far and how deep the influence of both his formal teaching and his personal radiance reached.</p>
<p>Because the Islamic intellectual sciences as a whole are so closely bound to Ali, the Prophet''s description of him as the &quot;gate&quot; to prophetic wisdom, quoted in the epigraph, reads in two ways at once: as an account of what Ali was to the Prophet in their own lifetime, and as a prophecy of the part he would play as those sciences later unfolded. Each of these sciences is understood as one more outward, formal expression of the inward, essential spirit of the Islamic revelation — a spirit that Ali drew together within himself and faithfully passed on. The main historical outlines of his life come first.</p>
<p>Ali was a &quot;follower&quot; of the Prophet in the deepest sense of that word. He lived at the Prophet''s side, in his very shadow, and took in spiritually all that shone out from him. The closeness between them is caught in the words of one of the Imam''s own sermons:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَمَّا كُنْتُ وَلِيدًا ضَمَّنِي إِلَى صَدْرِهِ، فَكُنْتُ أتَّبِعُهُ اتّبَاعَ الفَصِيلِ أُمَّهُ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;When I was but a child he took me under his wing. I would follow him as a baby camel follows its mother.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَرْفَعُ لِي فِي كُلِّ يَوْمٍ عَلَمًا مِنْ أَخْلاقِهِ الْكَرِيمَةِ وَيَأمُرُنِي بِالاِقْتِدَاءِ بِهِ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Every day he would hold up for me a sign of his noble character and command me to follow it.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَكَانَ يَتَعَاهَدُ غَارَ حِرَاءٍ فِي كُلِّ سَنَةٍ، فَأَرَاهُ وَلَا يَرَاهُ غَيْرِي</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Each year he would go into seclusion at the mountain of Hira. I saw him there, and no one else did.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَمْ يَجْمَعْ بَيْتٌ وَاحِدٌ يَوْمَئِذٍ فِي الإِسْلامِ غَيْرَ رَسُولِ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَخَدِيجَةَ وَأَنَا ثَالِثُهُمَا</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;At that time no single household was gathered together in the religion of Islam, save that of the Messenger of God, Khadija, and myself as the third among them.&quot;</p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرَى نُورَ الْوَحْيِ وَالرِّسَالَةِ، وَأَشُمُّ رِيحَ النُّبُوَّةِ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;I saw the light of the revelation and the message, and I smelt the fragrance of prophecy...&quot;</p></blockquote>
<p>This sketch concerns Ali as the faithful follower who carried on the prophetic pattern. Its focus is the early period of his life, from his birth (c. 599 CE) to the death of the Prophet (11/632). Two later periods fall outside it: the second, from the Prophet''s death to Ali''s own accession to the caliphate (35/656); and the third, his brief caliphate, torn by tragic civil wars and ended by his assassination in 40/661. These periods raise questions tangled in historical and source-critical difficulty, and to enter those disputes would pull away from the main aim of this book — to present the spiritual ethos of this extraordinary figure in a way that shows how widely it speaks, both within Islam and beyond it. That ethos rises above the boundaries of formal religion, just as it rises above the political quarrels underlying the outward Shii-Sunni divergence — quarrels that only draw attention away from the essential spiritual message of Ali.</p>
<p>What this book brings forward instead is the uniting power of his spiritual teachings — teachings whose great importance and depth Muslims of every school affirm alike. What follows is a brief survey of that earlier period of Ali''s life spent with the Prophet, along with the ways the Prophet described him in well-attested sayings; this is the necessary background for understanding his role as the gate to prophetic wisdom. The second part of this opening chapter then takes up some key principles of his spiritual ethos, with special attention to the place of the intellect within it.</p>
<p>By the account of all Shii authorities and of several Sunni sources, Ali held the singular distinction of being born inside the Kaba in Mecca. His mother was Fatima bt. Asad; his father, Abu Talib, son of Abd al-Muttalib, was a leading member of the Hashimite clan. Abu Talib had raised the young orphan Muhammad, son of his brother Abd Allah, and would later become his chief protector once Islam was preached openly in Mecca. Some years before the mission began, in a time of drought, Muhammad lightened Abu Talib''s burden by taking his young son Ali — then about five years old — into his own household. From that time on, Ali was like a son to him, and the two were seldom apart.</p>
<p>Ali is held to be the first male to embrace Islam, though he was only nine or ten at the time. Abu Bakr was the first grown man to enter the new religion, and Khadija, the Prophet''s wife, was the first woman. That the young boy answered the prophetic message at once, and held to it without wavering ever after, carries great weight. About three years later, when revelation instructed the Prophet to &quot;warn thy nearest kin&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ash-Shu''ara: 214</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَنذِرْ عَشِيرَتَكَ ٱلْأَقْرَبِينَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>(Ash-Shuara: 214), he invited the leading members of his clan to a feast and then addressed them: &quot;O sons of Abd al-Muttalib, I know of no Arab who hath come to his people with a nobler message than mine. I bring you the best of this world and the next. God hath commanded me to call you unto Him. Which of you, then, will help me in this, and be my brother, mine executor and my successor among you?&quot; All remained silent, except for the youthful Ali, who spoke up: &quot;O Prophet of God, I will be thy helper in this.&quot; The Prophet then placed his hand on Alis neck and said, &quot;This is my brother, mine executor and my successor among you. Hearken unto him and obey him.&quot; Though the clan''s leaders met this with mockery at the time, the declaration was taken far more seriously as the years passed and the extraordinary character, nobility, and intelligence of the Prophet''s young cousin grew ever more dazzlingly plain.</p>
<p>This early period also holds the part Ali played in the migration (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هِجْرَة</span>) from Mecca to Medina (622 CE), the event that marks the start of the Muslim calendar. The Prophet''s enemies had plotted to kill him; knowing this, Ali risked his own life by taking the Prophet''s place, sleeping in his bed on the night he left. Once in Medina, the Prophet took Ali as his &quot;brother&quot; in the pact of brotherhood he made between the &quot;emigrants&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُهَاجِرُونَ</span>) — the Muslims who had come with him on the hijra from Mecca — and the &quot;helpers&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَنْصَارُ</span>), the Medinan tribes who had embraced the new faith.</p>
<p>During the Medinan years, Ali made his mark on the battlefield, as a scribe of the Quranic revelation as it came down, and as a leading companion of the Prophet. In the battles against the overwhelming forces of the Meccan Quraysh and their allies, his courage and heroism were second to none. He stood in the front rank of nearly every major engagement fought under the Prophet''s banner and was often made standard-bearer. He was never beaten in the single-combat duel with which battles customarily opened; indeed, he was never beaten in any battle he fought at all. Both his unbreakable courage and his matchless skill as a warrior became legendary. It is said that at the battle of Uhud a heavenly voice was heard to proclaim, &quot;There is no chivalric knight but Ali, no sword but Dhul-Faqar (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا فَتَى إِلَّا عَلِيّ، وَلَا سَيْفَ إِلَّا ذُو الْفَقَارِ</span>),&quot; the last being the name of the sword the Prophet had given him. The battle that did most to fix Alis name as the foremost — indeed the unconquerable — warrior of Islam was Khaybar, in 7/629. The Muslims had been able to make no headway against the heavily fortified defenses of their foes, which seemed impossible to breach. The Prophet then declared that he would hand the army''s banner to one who &quot;loves God and His Messenger and is loved by God and His Messenger,&quot; and that through him victory would be granted. He sent for Ali, who was away on account of an ailment of the eyes. The Prophet healed him by placing saliva on his eyes, and Ali went on to lead the Muslims to victory. In the thick of the fighting, having lost his own shield, he is said to have torn a gate from its hinges and used it as a shield — a gate that, after the battle, eight men could barely lift.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'in the prophet''s shadow';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>)s standing as a warrior is only one of many things that explain his unique place in the Islamic tradition. Far more important is how close he was, in every respect, to the Prophet, and the wisdom and sanctity that flowed from that closeness. Ali received the immense honor of marrying the Prophet''s daughter Fatima. She and her mother Khadija, the Prophet''s first wife, are regarded as the most saintly women in Islam. One of the epithets the tradition bestows on Fatima is Majma al-Nurayn, Confluence of the Two Lights'', the two lights of nubuwwa (prophecy) and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَايَة</span> (sanctity). After the Prophet himself, Ali was thus the head of what became known as the Ahl al-Bayt, the People of the House, that is, the Prophet''s family.</p>
<p>The Ahl al-Bayt are addressed in an important Quranic verse as having been purified of all defilement:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Ahzab: 33</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقَرْنَ فِى بُيُوتِكُنَّ وَلَا تَبَرَّجْنَ تَبَرُّجَ ٱلْجَهِلِيَّةِ ٱلْأُولَىٰۖ وَأَقِمْنَ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَآتِينَ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ وَأَطِعْنَ ٱللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُۚۥٓ إِنَّمَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيُذْهِبَ عَنكُمُ ٱلرِّجْسَ أَهْلَ ٱلْبَيْتِ وَيُطَهِّرَكُمْ تَطْهِيرًۭا</span></p><p class="tr">God only wisheth to remove from you all impurity, O People of the House, and to purify you with a complete purification (Al-Ahzab: 33).</p></blockquote>
<p>According to several of the most authoritative early sources, the Prophet called for Fatima, Ali, and their two sons Hasan and Husayn, and declared, This is my Ahl al-Bayt. On one well-attested occasion the Prophet gathered these members of his family beneath his cloak and recited the words of this verse. This happened when a Christian delegation from Najran was engaged in religious debate with the Prophet, and a revelation came calling for a challenge of mutual imprecation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاهَلَة</span>) against the Christians. The Prophet brought forward the four persons named above for this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُبَاهَلَة</span>, but the Christians declined the challenge.</p>
<p>The importance of the Ahl al-Bayt is further expressed in a verse in which the Prophet is instructed:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ash-Shura: 23</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَلِكَ ٱلَّذِى يُبَشِّرُ ٱللَّهُ عِبَادَهُ ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّلِحَتِ ۗ قُل لَّآ أَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ أَجْرًا إِلَّا ٱلْمَوَدَّةَ فِى ٱلْقُرْبَىٰ ۗ وَمَن يَقْتَرِفْ حَسَنَةًۭ نَّزِدْ لَهُۥ فِيهَا حُسْنًا ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ شَكُورٌ</span></p><p class="tr">Say: I ask you for no reward, save love of the near of kin (Ash-Shura: 23).</p></blockquote>
<p>Several of the most significant Quranic commentators identify the near of kin in this verse as the Prophet''s Ahl al-Bayt. The revelation thus explicitly demands love of the Prophet''s family. This does not exclude the verse''s wider meaning, which takes in love for all those bound to a person by close kinship. How deeply all Muslims are to revere the Prophet''s family is shown further by another practice: blessings are to be called down upon his al, or progeny, as part of the blessings called down upon him. The Quran instructs the believers:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Ahzab: 56</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ وَمَلَٓئِكَتَهُۥ يُصَلُّونَ عَلَى ٱلنَّبِىِّۚ يَٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟ صَلُّوا۟ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلِّمُوا۟ تَسْلِيمًا</span></p><p class="tr">Truly, God and His angels bless the Prophet. O ye who believe, bless him and greet him with peace (Al-Ahzab: 56).</p></blockquote>
<p>When this verse was revealed, the Prophet was asked how one should perform this blessing, and he replied that blessings were to be invoked upon him and his al as follows: O God, bless Muhammad and the progeny of Muhammad, as Thou hast blessed Abraham and the progeny of Abraham. Truly, Thou art the Praised, the Glorious ... This blessing is recited during the canonical prayer. Its importance, together with the obligation to love the Prophet''s family, is beautifully expressed in the following lines by Imam al-Shafii, founder of one of the four schools of Sunni jurisprudence:</p>
<p>Loving you, O family of the household of the Prophet of God, is an obligation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَرْضٌ</span>) from God in the Quran which He revealed; Sufficient to show the grandeur of your dignity is that one who blesses you not, no prayer has he.</p>
<p>There is also a prophetic saying that underscores the spiritual significance of the Ahl al-Bayt:</p>
<p>Truly, I am leaving behind among you the two weighty things (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلثَّقَلَيْنِ</span>): the Book of God and my Ahl al-Bayt. They will not be parted from each other until they return to me at the [paradisal pool] <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَوْضَ</span>.</p>
<p>Beyond these sayings, which concern Ali as the most important member of the Prophet''s Ahl al-Bayt, there are sayings of the Prophet that concern Ali specifically, and these deserve to be cited in full. Only on the basis of these sayings can one begin to appreciate the immensely important role of Ali in the spiritual dimension of the Islamic faith. These traditions (ahadith, sing. hadith) are presented here as a list with minimal commentary, following the manner in which they appear in most standard hadith collections. Anyone who takes prophetic guidance seriously cannot but take Ali seriously, and it is largely for this reason that countless Muslims across the centuries have meditated on these sayings, drawing from them diverse interpretations and incalculable inspiration. In a certain sense, these sayings form the prism through which the tradition views Ali, and they deepen one''s spiritual receptivity to his teachings. The crucial importance of Ali in the spiritual firmament of nascent Islam emerges more clearly in the light of these prophetic descriptions. They should therefore be regarded as a significant part of the prophetic background to Ali s seminal contribution to Islamic spiritual and intellectual life, whether in a Shii, Sunni, or Sufi context.</p>
<p>The traditions given below can be found in any standard Shii hadith collection. To show that they — together with their implications and the devotion they have inspired — are not confined to Shii Islam, they are cited here from two of the most authoritative Sunni traditionists. The first is Ahmad al-Nasai, author of the Sunan, one of the six collections deemed canonical in the Sunni corpus (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْكُتُبُ السِّتَّةُ</span>). The second is al-Hakim al-Nisaburi, author of al-Mustadrak alal-sahihayn (Supplement to the Two Sound Collections). This work gathers those prophetic traditions that meet the exacting criteria of authenticity established by Bukhari and Muslim, authors of the sahihayn, but were not included in their own collections. These sayings speak of Ali s distinctions, virtues, and merits, all conveyed by the Arabic word fadail (sing. fadila). They explain why Ahmad b. Hanbal, another leading Sunni traditionist and founder of one of the four Sunni schools of law, made the following statement: No companion of the Prophet has had such fadail ascribed to him as those which have been ascribed to Ali ibn Abi Talib.</p>
<p>The sayings are these:</p>
<p>Truly, Ali is from me and I am from him (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ عَلِيًّا مِنِّي وَأَنَا مِنْهُ</span>), and he is the wali (patron/spiritual master) of every believer after me.</p>
<p>Ali is with the Quran and the Quran is with Ali, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ عَلِيًّا مَعَ الْقُرْآنِ وَالْقُرْآنُ مَعَ عَلِيٍّ، لَنْ يَفْتَرِقَا حَتَّى يَرِدَا عَلَيَّ الْحَوْضَ</span>. They will not separate from each other until they return to me at the [paradisal pool] (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَوْضَ</span>).</p>
<p>Three things were revealed to me regarding Ali: he is the leader of the Muslims, the guide of the pious, and chief of the radiantly devout (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدُ الْمُسْلِمِينَ، إِمَامُ الْمُتَّقِينَ، وَقَائِدُ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ</span>).</p>
<p>Gazing upon Ali is an act of worship (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّظَرُ إِلَى عَلِيٍّ عِبَادَةٌ</span>).</p>
<p>May God have mercy on Ali. O God, make the truth revolve around Ali wherever he turns (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدِرِ الْحَقَّ مَعَهُ حَيْثُ دَارَ</span>).</p>
<p>I am the city of knowledge and Ali is its gate; so whoever desires knowledge, let him enter the gate.</p>
<p>O Ali, you are a leader (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدٌ</span>) in this world and the Hereafter. Your beloved is my beloved, and my beloved is the beloved of God; your enemy is my enemy, and my enemy is the enemy of God. Woe to those who hate you after me [after I have passed away].</p>
<p>Whoever desires to live my life and to die my death and to take his rest in the eternal Garden my Lord has promised me, let him orient himself toward Ali ibn Abi Talib, for truly he will never lead you away from right guidance, nor lead you into error.</p>
<p>The Prophet said that Ali was as my own soul (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عليٌّ مِنِّي بِمَنْزِلَةِ نَفْسِي</span>).</p>
<p>He said to Ali, You are from me and I am from you, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أنْتَ مِنِّی وَ أِنَا مِنْکَ</span>.</p>
<p>... whoever obeys Ali obeys me, and whoever disobeys him disobeys me.</p>
<p>You will clarify for my community that over which they will differ after me (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْتَ تُبَيِّنُ لِأُمَّتِي مَا اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ مِنْ بَعْدِي</span>).</p>
<p>There is one among you who will fight for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span> [spiritual interpretation] of the Quran as I have fought for its tanzil [literal revelation], <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِیٌّ یُقَاتِلُ عَلیٰ تَاوِیلِہِ کَما قَاتَلْتُ عَلیٰ تَنزِیلِہِ</span>. Abu Bakr asked, Is it I? The Prophet said, No. Umar asked, Is it I? The Prophet said, No, it is the one who is mending the sandal. The Prophet had given Ali his sandal to mend.</p>
<p>O Ali, there is in you something akin to Jesus, on whom be peace and blessings. The Jews hated him so intensely that they slandered his mother; and the Christians loved him so intensely that they ascribed to him a rank he did not possess. O Ali, whoever separates himself from me separates himself from God, and whoever separates himself from you, O Ali, separates himself from me.</p>
<p>Whoever curses Ali curses me, and whoever curses me curses God.</p>
<p>The Prophet said to his wife Aisha, Call unto me the leader (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيِّدٌ</span>) of the Arabs. She asked, O Prophet of God, are you not the leader of the Arabs? He said, I am the leader of the children of Adam, and Ali is the leader of the Arabs.</p>
<p>When Fatima was asked by some women why the Prophet had given her in marriage to a man as poor as Ali, she related this to the Prophet. He said: Are you not pleased that God, exalted and glorified be He, surveyed the people of the world and chose two men from among them: one is your father, and the other your husband?</p>
<p>The first of you to reach the [paradisal] pool (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَوْضَ</span>) is the first of you who entered Islam, Ali ibn Abi Talib.</p>
<p>Ali is from me and I am from him (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيٌّ مِنِّي وَأَنَا مِنْهُ</span>), and nobody can fulfill my duty but myself and Ali.</p>
<p>Ali himself relates that the Prophet told him that none but a believer will love him, and none but a hypocrite will hate him.</p>
<p>When the Prophet was about to depart on an expedition to Tabuk, he left Ali as his deputy in Medina. Ali was saddened not to be going with him. The Prophet said, Are you not content to stand in relation to me as Aaron stood in relation to Moses, except that there is no prophet after me?</p>
<p>The Prophet prayed to God to bring the most beloved of Thy creatures'' to share with him a meal of fowl. Only when Ali arrived did the Prophet invite him to join him.</p>
<p>Among the several Quranic verses on which the Prophet commented with reference to Ali is 13:7: Verily thou art a warner, and for every people there is a guide. The Prophet said, I am the warner... you are the guide, O Ali. After me, the rightly-guided shall be guided by you.</p>
<p>Verse 55 of Sura 5 (al-Maida (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْمَائِدَةُ</span>)) reads, Verily your wali is only God and His Messenger and those who believe, establish the prayer and give alms while bowing in prayer. The commentators tie its revelation to the occasion when Ali, while bowing in prayer, extended his ring to a beggar who had asked for alms. On being told of the incident, the Prophet recited this verse, and then added what are perhaps the most celebrated words ever spoken about Ali: For whomever I am the mawla [guardian, master, close friend], Ali is his mawla (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ كُنْتُ مَوْلَاهُ فَعَلِيٌّ مَوْلَاهُ</span>).</p>
<p>This last saying was expressed on a number of occasions, the most famous of which came after the Prophet''s final pilgrimage to Mecca, in the year 10/632, at a pool midway between Mecca and Medina known as Ghadir Khumm. The Prophet assembled all the pilgrims, had a pulpit erected, and delivered an address to the thousands gathered there. The address culminated in the declaration: For whomever I am the mawla, Ali is his mawla. For Shiis, this is an unambiguous designation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصّ</span>) of Ali as the Prophet''s successor; for Sunnis it marks the special closeness of Ali to the Prophet, but not his nomination as successor in the political domain. That the reference to Ali as mawla — in some versions as wali — carries the highest spiritual significance is, however, not seriously disputed. Umar is reported to have approached Ali and said, Bakh bakh, an expression of congratulation, you have become my mawla and the mawla of every Muslim. The heated debate that has occupied countless minds throughout history, and continues to this day, turns on the political implications of Ali s spiritual authority, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَايَة</span>.</p>
<p>Although the place of Ali within Shii Islam is not the subject here, his charisma was such that, even during the Prophet''s own lifetime, a group came to be known as the shia, or partisans, of Ali. Al-Tabari, author of the first great commentary on the Quran, reports that the Prophet interpreted the verse:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Bayyinah: 7</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّلِحَتِ أُو۟لَٓئِكَ هُمْ خَيْرُ ٱلْبَرِيَّةِ</span></p><p class="tr">Those who have faith and do righteous deeds, they are the best of created beings'' (Al-Bayyinah: 7)</p></blockquote>
<p>He interpreted it as referring to Ali and his shia.</p>
<p>Four individuals in particular were renowned for their devotion to Ali during the Prophet''s lifetime and are regarded as the prototypes of early Shiism: Salman al-Farisi, Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, Ammar b. Yasir, and Miqdad b. Amr. This early love for Ali should not be equated with the theological doctrines later elaborated in the Shii branch of Islam in the generations that followed. It is nonetheless worth emphasizing that, at the core of Shiism in all its different expressions, lies the notion of fidelity to Ali. This fidelity, loyalty, or love is one aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَايَة</span>; the other, complementary aspect is the spiritual authority or sanctity of Ali. It is equally important to stress that neither devotion to Ali nor the spiritual orientation toward the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَايَة</span> he embodies belongs to Shiism alone. Rather, it extends across the whole of the Islamic faith, for devotion to Ali as head of the Prophet''s Ahl al-Bayt is found throughout the Muslim world and is especially deep wherever Sufism flourishes. Ali is regarded as the first spiritual pole (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُطْبٌ</span>) of Sufism after the Prophet himself, and he stands at the summit of all the spiritual chains (salasil, sing. silsila) through which the Sufi orders trace their lineage back to the Prophet. To speak of Ali is to speak not of exclusivist sectarianism but of universal spirituality.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'the people of the house and the prophet''s witness';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The spiritual ethos of Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) has an intellectual heart. The intellect (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَقْل</span>) plays a central part in how his spiritual teachings are worked out and taken in. Sketching the key features of that ethos, with special attention to its intellectual side, gives firmer ground to the explorations that follow. One aim is to bring out something of the spirit of the intellect, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرُّوحُ الْعَقْلُ</span> — the phrase coined for the bond between the intellect and the spirit as it appears in the Imam''s worldview. This spirit surpasses the workings of the rational mind even as it contains them, and it reaches into areas not usually tied to the intellect today, such as moral conduct and a sense of beauty. This spiritual understanding of the intellect is directly connected to the Quranic idea of the spirit (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرُّوح</span>), and much of what follows examines what comes of engaging deeply with this unifying spirit that belongs to the intellect.</p>
<p>It helps at the outset to explain why the English word intellect is preferred over reason to render aql. The word is meant to call up the original sense of intellectus in Latin Christendom, a sense almost identical to that of nous in the Greek of the Church Fathers. Intellectus means understanding, and it holds within it the idea of standing under a thing: to understand something is to stand beneath a perception of reality and let that perception overshadow and govern one''s actions, for a person always acts in the shadow of how things are perceived. Intellectus, or nous, is therefore the faculty able to see transcendent realities directly, in contemplation — the very realities of which the Quran speaks but which are hidden from the senses. Reason, which renders the Latin ratio and the Greek dianoia, is by contrast indirect and step by step; it works through logic and reaches only mental concepts of those realities. The root of ratio means a relation or comparison between two numbers, and this catches how reason proceeds: by comparison, by a binary logic of affirming and denying, weighing one thing against another until it has defined what it examines. Reason so understood is the instrument the intellect uses, but it can carry a person only as far as the outward form of a thing, only as far as the mental concept or image of a reality. With the intellect one can contemplate, or see, the Absolute; with reason one can only think about it. To know about a reality is one thing; to contemplate and experience it directly is quite another, and it is the intellect, not reason, that is capable of that experience — even though it is through reason that the intellect is brought to it.</p>
<p>These opening reflections on the spirit of the intellect in the Imam''s ethos turn chiefly on a short discourse of the greatest importance in the Nahj al-balagha: the one the Imam gave to Kumayl, among his closest disciples. The method here is that of an interpretive commentary on the Imam''s words, drawing not only on his other sayings on the same theme but also on Quranic verses that agree with them and, at times, on traditions of the Prophet that either help clarify the Imam''s saying at hand or are themselves clarified by it. Three factors belonging to the spirit of this intellectual outlook must be stressed first: the centrality of revelation; the harmony between revelation and the intellect; and the distinction between the intellect, understood as the principle by which consciousness is articulated, and reason, understood as one expression of that principle.</p>
<p>To begin with the first: everything in the Imam''s life and teachings is centered on the Quranic revelation and on its embodiment in the Prophet''s conduct. The Quran is the center around which the intellect must turn, for the intellect needs a center, principles, and definitions to hold fast to if it is to be rightly guided. Both the thought and the life of the Imam can be read as a kind of spiritual commentary, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span>, on the sacred text. On the doctrinal plane, it can be said with confidence that Ali had an inborn gift for the work of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span>. On the plane of his own life as well, his path — from his birth in the most holy shrine of the Kaba to his martyrdom in the mosque of Kufa — reads as a dramatic unfolding of the inner meaning of the revealed text. Ali s refusal to stop at the surface of things, whether religious, ethical, or political, showed an unflinching fidelity to the sacred substance of the religion, a fidelity that called for inward intellectual penetration and outward armed resistance alike. The saying of the Prophet cited earlier bears closely on this: Ali would fight for the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span> of the Quran just as he, the Prophet, had fought for its tanzil, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِیُّ یُقَاتِلُ عَلیٰ تَاوِیلِہ کَما قَتَلُ عَلیَ تَنزِیلِہِ</span>. Ali s short caliphate was dominated by bloody civil wars, and a strong case can be made that all three — the wars of the Camel (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْجَمَلُ</span>), of Nahrawan, and of Siffin — were fought for the sake of interpreting and applying the Quran rightly, for the clarification of its central message of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, and for the realization of that oneness on every level, spiritual and social alike. These were not political battles or mere struggles for power; they were waged to clarify and uphold the oneness of God according to the principles of the Quran — in thought, in speech, and in action. The Imam''s response to a bedouin brings this vividly home. Just as the battle of the Camel (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْجَمَلُ</span>) was about to begin, the man approached him and said, O Commander of the Faithful (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">یَا أَمِیرَ الْمُوْمِنِینَ</span>)! Do you say that God is one? Those present rebuked the bedouin, telling him this was neither the time nor the place for such questions. The Imam, however, intervened: Leave him, for surely what the bedouin wishes is what we wish for the people.</p>
<p>This response bears directly on the principle that right intention comes before outward action, whatever that action may be. Had the Imam been guided by reason alone, he would have sided with those who rebuked the bedouin and told him to find a fitter time and place; but he saw with the intellect that stands above reason. For the Imam, the deepest intention behind every action, whether religious in the ordinary sense or military as here, is knowledge of the true nature of ultimate reality. What the Imam wishes for the people, then, is precisely this knowledge, and it must lie at the very heart of all concerns, motives, and actions, whether on the battlefield or the prayer-rug. There is therefore no time or place where the question What do you mean by saying God is one? is beside the point. It was for the sake of true knowledge of the divine, and of all that flows from it in society, that the Imam was fighting, in fulfillment of the prophetic saying just mentioned. On the very field of battle, then, the Imam gives a discourse on the meaning of divine oneness. The first part of his reply holds a key to his whole spiritual ethos. He rejects any notion that God''s oneness is numerical, a matter of counting. He tells the bedouin that one may not say one while holding any numerical idea in mind, for that which has no second does not enter into the category of numbers''. The moment a thing can be counted as one, it at once admits the possibility of two, three, four; a oneness understood in this numerical sense is thus undone by the very number that would affirm it.</p>
<p>That which has no second is not merely a divinity to which no partner can be ascribed, as a theologian might put it, but that reality which has no otherness'' — the Real, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>, at once transcendent and immanent, beyond all things'' and yet made manifest by all things'', at once out of reach and inseparable from all that is. To say that God has no otherness is not to say that things exist which might have been His partners but which believers simply choose not to associate with Him; it is to affirm that nothing in all of existence bears His likeness, nothing that could be set beside Him. In these few words the Imam works, for those who have ears to hear, a shift of awareness: from the doctrinal notion of one God against many gods to the mystical grasp of the one and only Reality, before which everything else is mere appearance. All things come to be seen as outward appearances waiting to be read, as signs (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آیات</span>) pointing beyond themselves to That of which they are the manifestations, for an aya is precisely a meaningful token that points beyond itself to another. Tracing these appearances back to their source and origin is the literal meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span>: a return to the awwal, the First (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَوْدَۃٌ اِلَی الْاَوَّلِ</span>). On the one hand, the Real is described as the beginning and end of all things, the apparent and the hidden; there is nothing in being but this one Reality: He is the First and the Last, the Outward and the Inward (Al-Hadid: 3).</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Hadid: 3</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُوَ ٱلْأَوَّلُ وَٱلْآخِرُ وَٱلظَّهِرُ وَٱلْبَاطِنُ ۖ وَهُوَ بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عَلِيمٌ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>On the other hand, apparent multiplicity is to be brought back to real oneness: We shall show them Our signs (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا</span>) on the horizons and in their own souls, so that it be clear to them that He is the Real (Fussilat: 53).</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fussilat: 53</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ يَتَبَيَّنَ لَهُمْ أَنَّهُ ٱلْحَقُّ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>In one saying attributed to Ali, the Prophet is described as the first whom God taught the science of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span>, and who in turn taught it to Ali. According to the Prophet, Truly, Ali is with the Quran, and the Quran is with Ali, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَّ عَلِیٌّ مَعَ الْقُرْآنِ، وَ قُرْآنٌ مَعَ الْعَلِیِّ</span>. In another famous saying, often quoted by Sufi commentators, Ali claimed that he could load seventy camels with the pages of commentary he could give on the Fatiha alone, the opening chapter of the Quran. Ali s cousin Ibn Abbas, often called the first commentator, likewise claimed to have learned the science of interpretation from Ali.</p>
<p>Closely related is a saying of the Imam of the first importance, which helps explain why he is held in the highest esteem within the discipline of Quranic exegesis, tafsir: The Quran consists of a book inscribed, between two covers; it speaks not with a tongue, it cannot do without an interpreter (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا بُدَّ لَهُ مِنْ تُرْجُمَانٍ</span>). The mutual dependence of revelation and the intellect is underlined again in the saying, The prophet of a man is the interpreter of his intellect (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ الرَّجُلِ تَرْجُمَانُ عَقْلِهِ</span>). Elsewhere the intellect is set in plain correspondence with the inner messenger: the intellect is the messenger of the Real.</p>
<p>The Imam''s teachings, which give voice to his spiritual ethos, should therefore be read above all as creative interpretations of the Quranic revelation. They set in motion a movement from the letter of the revelation to its spirit, and from the spirit of the revelation to the spirit of the Revealer — a work of retracing, of returning to the source, the origin, the beginning, al-awwal, achieved to the degree that the spirit of the intellect is brought to life within the interpreter. Yet the Revealer stays of necessity hidden even as He is revealed through the revelation, hidden because the essence of what is revealed, the divine Reality in itself, cannot be uttered or put into words; only the forms of its manifestations can be revealed. This play between form and the formless, the evident and the hidden, the revealed and the concealed — such pairs of opposites lie at the root of the paradoxical manner of expression for which the Imam is renowned, and which recurs throughout this book. The relationship that especially concerns the present discussion is the second of the three factors named above: that between revelation and intellection, by which is meant the creative use of the intellect. This relationship matters all the more in a setting so largely defined by fidelity to revelation. Where strict rationalists hold intellect and revelation to be mutually exclusive, here they are seen as fully harmonious, standing in creative reciprocity, each needing the other for its completeness, its wholeness, for the making one that is the very essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>.</p>
<p>Indeed, one of the most striking features of the Imam''s outlook is the way it lays bare the emptiness of many false or overdrawn oppositions that later came to mark, or were forced upon, Islamic thought. This owes much to the deeply synthetic quality of the Imam''s discourses, seen as pre-philosophical by some scholars and as supra-philosophical — inspired and evocative — by those within the Islamic spiritual tradition. The seemingly unbridgeable split between intellect and revelation shows up in several opposed forms: reason versus faith, convention (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span>) versus nature (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَبِيعَة</span>), imitation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْلِيد</span>) versus verification (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحْقِيق</span>), piety versus inquiry, and so on. In Ali s ethos, however, intellect and revelation are not mutually exclusive but complementary, and at the highest level inseparable, since truth is one. The human intellect plainly cannot be equated with divine revelation, yet the spirit that animates the intellect is not, in the end, bound by human limits. It is this spirit that perceives and grasps the divine character of revelation.</p>
<p>The divine spirit that shapes the form of revelation and fills its content must be fathomable, to some degree, by human awareness; without this, revelation would amount to no more than a set of rules and regulations for the will, rather than a source of creative inspiration for the intelligence. It cannot be stressed too strongly that divine revelation, far from curbing the workings of reason and imposing some unintelligible command, on the contrary asks for a creative engagement with all the resources of the intelligence — above all those acts of reflection and meditation for which the Imam himself is renowned, and which the Quran calls for again and again. It is the very opposite of a decree imposed from outside: the Quran constantly demands fikr, deep and sustained reflection, and such reflection does not shut the meaning of the text within its literal surface but opens it out, unfolding the inner horizons of meaning folded within the letter.</p>
<p>For the revealed text is itself silent; it speaks only through its interpreter, the intellect. This understanding of the bond between intellect and revelation shuts out all literalism and shallowness, every attempt to pen meaning within a flat, one-sided reading. How deeply the text''s meaning is sounded depends on the depth and penetration of the interpreter''s own intellect, for the text carries many levels of meaning at once. At its most profound, this prophetic intellect rejoins the very source of revelation itself; for it is this inner prophet, the innermost degree of consciousness, that is the ultimate ground on which a person can confirm the verses of the outer revelation as true — to validate them through lived experience, and not merely to affirm them as dogma. It is in this sense that the Imam says, in the first sermon of the Nahj, that one of the purposes for which God sent forth prophets to mankind was to unearth for them the buried treasures of the intellects'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَفَائِنُ الْعُقُولِ</span>).</p>
<p>This saying is one of the keys to the spiritual ethos under consideration, and it returns again and again throughout these essays. The intellect is not stifled by revelation; it is brought to life by it. Revelation is a clarion call to the spiritual depths of the intellect, cultivating the seeds of consciousness — the seeds of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">توحید</span> — that God has planted there and that lie buried within us, and bringing them to fruition. For the spirit of one''s intellect already carries the initial imprint of its Creator: in the world of the spirit it witnessed its Lord and testified to His lordship, and revelation, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذکر</span>, does no more than remind it of that primordial testimony. A reminder, after all, is never a new piece of information; it is knowledge one already possesses but has forgotten, for one can only be reminded of what one already knows. We were created in this world in a kind of amnesia. Just as a physician tells the family of a man who has lost his memory to surround him with familiar things, with signs (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آیات</span>) out of his past, to jog his memory, so God has set His reminders in the horizons and the souls (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آفَاق وَ أَنْفُس</span>). The spirit of one''s intellect, then, cannot be alien to what descends as revelation from on high; on the contrary, it must be fundamentally akin to it, and it is precisely for this reason that one can be absolutely certain that the revelation truly comes from the Absolute. The point is not to set up a dichotomy between intellect and revelation, but to see the intellect as revelation, or at least as one mode of revelation: the God-given means by which the individual comes to discern, affirm, and plumb the divine revelations'' outside himself, both those of scripture and those constituted by the signs'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آیات</span>) of the natural world. If the human intellect can perceive and recognize revelation, it is because both are rooted in one and the same ultimate source; we came from a spiritual realm, and so did revelation, sent to remind us of what we once witnessed and have since forgotten. Prophetic revelation awakens and quickens the depths of the human intellect; it does not replace or displace it. The messengers do for these buried seeds what the farmer does when he waters what lies hidden beneath the soil, what the miner does when he digs into the mountain for its gold and silver, and what the diver does when he descends to the sea-bed to bring up its buried treasures. From this perspective the revealed text is not some extraneous source set against the intelligence, but the objective, outward expression of the very principles that the intellect itself yields up to the inner prophet — the intellect serving as the inner messenger whom the outer messenger awakens and nourishes. By intellect is meant not simply the faculty of reasoning but the source of consciousness that articulates the human spirit.</p>
<p>The intellect holds unsuspected depths, buried treasures, and is far from being exhausted by its surface operations — the cognitions and reasonings proper to its purely mental dimension. A third matter now follows: the relationship between intellect and reason. Just as there can be no contradiction whatsoever between revelation and intellection, so too a false dichotomy between reason and intellect must be avoided. Reason should rather be seen as one of the modes of the intellect; reason no more contradicts intellect than intellect contradicts revelation. Reason is a mode of the intellect, but it is not to be equated with it wholesale, just as one cannot reduce the universal objectivity of revelation to the subjective specificities of any one individual''s intellection, to his or her own private revelation. To equate reason with intellect would amount to denying the very existence of the buried treasures'' of the intellect — those supra-rational dimensions that are, at first, buried beneath, and thus hidden from, the rational surface of the intellect. The revelation constituted by the intellect is embedded within the creation of the human being, the intellect being that which most critically defines the human being as such. Far from pitting the intellect against revelation, each is to be seen in the light of the other: the substance of revelation cannot be engaged except through the intellect, on the one hand, and the buried treasures'' of the intellect cannot be disclosed except through revelation, on the other — a genuine co-dependency of the two.</p>
<p>This understanding of the intellect stands close to what the Imam refers to as the heart (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْب</span>), that innermost mode of perception capable of seeing God. It is the heart that can see God — not with eyesight, not with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بصارۃ</span>, but with insight, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بصیرۃ</span>, that spiritual insight generated by the verities of faith (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقَائِقُ الْإِيمَان</span> / <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقَائِقُ الْاِیمَانِ</span>). As the Imam puts it, in response to the questioner asking whether he had seen his Lord: Eyes see Him not through sight''s observation, but hearts see Him through the verities of faith.</p>
<p>Between the spiritual depths of the intellect and its rational surface there is both continuity and discontinuity. The continuity shows in an evident fact: all thinking begins with concepts that convey intelligible meaning. Thought sets out from ideas that are rationally discernible, and these must bear some relation to the realities they express, or they collapse into ideas that lead nowhere. But there is discontinuity too between the spiritual and rational aspects of the intellect, in that reason stops short at the ideas it has de-fined. The Arabic word for definition, hadd (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدّ</span>), expresses — like its English equivalent — both senses at once: that of a conceptual definition and that of a limitation, a boundary, the very boundary that reason draws around an idea by weighing its pros and cons until the idea is fixed and defined. To go beyond the limits imposed by this rational act of definition, something more than reason is needed; and it is this something more that makes the ethos under consideration a spiritual one, and not a rationalistic one. It is certainly rational, in the sense that the form of the ethos is indeed open to rational scrutiny; but it is not rationalistic, inasmuch as its essence is not predicated on reason alone.</p>
<p>Now Ali frequently hints, in highly allusive terms, at the mysteries and marvels — the unearthed treasures of the intellect — that he has directly witnessed by the eye of his purified heart. He also alludes to this supra-rational mode of awareness indirectly, through a bewildering use of apophatic discourse and paradoxical utterance, of which the corpus attributed to him is full. In this ethos it seems that the higher the truth, the deeper the paradox: the sheer lack of any common measure between reality as it is in itself and the human conceptions formed of it is effectively evoked by this creative use of paradox. In modern parlance, each human conception of reality needs to be deconstructed. But then, unlike a modern deconstructionism so radically destructive that it has nothing left to reconstruct, the Imam''s apophasis feeds directly into modes of spiritual intuition, contemplation, and realization of that which reason, on its own, cannot attain. It is this intuition and contemplation which re-members'' — or reconstructs'', if one likes — the spiritual realities that transcend the categories of reason. The value of the paradoxes, the antinomies, the apophasis, and the negation is entirely predicated on the eminently positive realities which the intellect can plumb, thereby becoming aware of its own hidden depths. The resulting consciousness does not so much negate reason as situate it, revealing the limits of reason''s proper sphere of operation: it reveals that reason alone cannot comprehend the higher realities, nor express them fully, however indispensable reason may be in initiating the cognitive movement toward those realities.</p>
<p>Thus, when the Imam negates, as he does so often, the possibility of human conceptions encompassing the divine Reality, what is being negated is the limitation that is the inescapable consequence of all such conceptions — not the positive content, the mana or spiritual meaning, of the conception itself. For this conception is a necessary starting point for a process of assimilation that engages a deeper level of awareness than that de-fined by the mind, and which therefore extends far beyond the domain of speculative thought. In this light, the negation of the limitations of human conception should be seen as the prelude to an absolute affirmation: the affirmation of the Absolute, which has no limitation. The spiritual negation of conceptual limitation is of an upward, constructive order, not a nihilistic, deconstructive one. Nowhere is this style of discourse more apparent than in the famous opening lines of the first sermon of the Nahj al-balagha:</p>
<p>Praise be to God, whose laudation those who speak cannot deliver, whose graces those who count cannot number, whose rightful due those who strive cannot render; He who cannot be grasped by far-reaching aspirations, nor fathomed by profound intuitions; He whose attribute has no binding limitation, no existing description, no time appointed, no term extended.</p>
<p>The divine attribute (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّفَة</span>) has no limit in terms of which it can be defined — laysa li-sifatih hadd mahdud — and yet it is affirmed as real. It has no existing description, and yet in some sense it is described (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْصُوف</span>), by the very words of the Quranic revelation; for in the Quran God is described as having such attributes as mercy, compassion, and power. He is the Merciful, the Compassionate, the Almighty, and yet the Imam affirms that these very attributes cannot be defined, bounded, or described. The paradox sharpens still further when the Imam speaks of the perfection of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span> — the rendering sincere, the purification of one''s devotion to God and of one''s conception of Him: this perfection, he says, is to divest (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفی</span>) God of all attributes. On the one hand the attributes are affirmed; on the other they are to be negated. The rational mind can only think of God in terms of the attributes given verbal expression in the Quran; and yet here the Imam appears to negate these same attributes, thereby depriving the mind of any convenient conceptual handle by which to grasp the nature of the divine Reality. The simultaneous affirmation and negation of the attributes raises an apparently irresolvable contradiction, and in doing so enhances receptivity to a deeper mode of knowing. This style of discourse appears designed to awaken in the listener some latent awareness inscribed in the heart or the depths of the intellect, and to invite the seeker to dive into those depths, into the knowledge buried in the intellect. The very shock of the apparent contradiction in this apophasis seems intended to precipitate a leap of consciousness, from the formal activity of reason to the supra-rational contemplation of the spirit.</p>
<p>The words spoken by the Imam immediately after affirming that his heart had indeed witnessed God — not by means of outward vision but through the verities of faith — run:</p>
<p>My Lord is not described in terms of dimension (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُعْدٌ</span>), nor motion (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَكَة</span>), nor rest (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُكُونٌ</span>) ... nor through coming or going: He is subtle in subtlety but not described by subtlety, magnificent in magnificence but not described by magnificence, great in greatness but not described by greatness ...</p>
<p>The attributes are again affirmed and denied. God possesses these attributes but is not reducible to them. The mind can begin to think of God in terms of these categories, but He is not reducible to such conceptions. The attributes afford the mind something by which the divine nature can be understood, but this understanding is fragmentary, never exhaustive, and it needs to be negated as the prelude to a fuller apprehension. The initial conceptual orientation to the divine Reality needs to be both negated and sublimated: negated as a mental conception, while the positive content of the conception needs to be sublimated through spiritual intuition. Only then can the two-dimensional portrait defined by reason be given a third dimension — that of depth. But this third dimension emerges only after the insufficiency of the rational depiction is understood; whence the need for that cognitive shock which paradox produces, a shock intended to reveal the pretension of reason in its effort to disclose the nature of the Absolute, the transcendent source of reason''s own activities.</p>
<p>The Imam''s discourses show a repeated combination of affirmation and denial, which mirrors the negation and affirmation found in the first testimony of Islam: la ilaha, no god, being the negation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفی</span>); then illaLlah, except God, coming as the affirmation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِثْبَات</span>). This helps to show that the negation of the attributes which the Imam names as the perfection of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span> is in fact an affirmation of the infinitude of the divine Reality. Each of the attributes is to be negated, but only insofar as it is conceived by the mind. Such negation is necessary because, at the mental level, each attribute excludes other attributes, and also excludes the object of attribution itself — that is, the divine Essence — such exclusion following inescapably from the very possibility of conceptually distinguishing between the attribute and the object of attribution, between the divine quality and the divine Essence. For any conception to take place in the mind, defined ideas are necessary; and where there are definitions, there are limits and thus exclusions. The reality of the attribute, however, is unlimited, by virtue of its absolute identity with the Essence. The attribute that has been negated is paradoxically affirmed — not in itself, but inasmuch as it is nothing but God: illaLlah. Thus one can place each of the negated attributes in the position of the nafy of the first testimony and arrive at such formulations as la rahmana illaLlah, There is no All-Compassionate except God; la rahima illaLlah, No All-Merciful except God, and so on. The apparent negation of the attributes becomes, in fact, an affirmation of their true identity, shorn of any restrictive association with the poverty of mental conceptions. The attributes are not superadded to the Essence but are absolutely one with it, differentiable from it only conceptually, not ontologically. The Essence does indeed comprise all the attributes mentioned — greatness, subtlety, and so on — but it is also infinitely more than these attributes; and, for their part, the attributes are infinite only within and as the Essence. The Imam''s apophasis here can be seen as a commentary on the Quranic refrain, Glorified be God above what they describe (Al-Muminun: 91). The infinitude of God strictly requires, on the human plane, the negation of all that the mind can conceive through reason — but not of all that the heart can see through spiritual vision. This very negation on the plane of reason calls out to something deeper, and this in turn can only be understood in the light of spiritual praxis, which is always assumed as the reflective, devotional, or contemplative context for the hearers of the Imam''s sermons and discourses. The heart, symbolic seat of the contemplative intelligence, calls out to be polished, and this is precisely what the remembrance of God does — this dhikr (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِکْر</span>) Allah being, according to the Imam, the most excellent form of worship. Even at this level of praxis there is a further paradox: the reality of the remembrance is not attained until you forget yourself in your remembrance.</p>
<p>It is not just the rational mind and the determinate conceptions proportioned to it that are to be negated or deconstructed, but the individual soul itself, as the locus of all cognition, rational or supra-rational. This final denouement, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>, is the ultimate form of apophasis.</p>
<p>These considerations show the extent to which the spirit of the intellect, in Ali s perspective, transcends the rational faculty. The intellect must be seen, first and foremost, as a spiritual faculty; and the spirit, in turn, must be appreciated as the source of the intellect, as well as the source of life. Without the intellect the spirit is reduced to unconscious existence; conversely, without the spirit, the intellect tends toward lifeless thought. These aspects of the spirit are clearly expressed in the first sermon of the Nahj, which can be read as a commentary upon the Quranic verses describing the creation of man in terms of God''s breathing of His spirit into man: He began the creation of man with clay... He fashioned him and breathed into him of His spirit (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثُمَّ سَوَّىٰهُ وَنَفَخَ فِيهِ مِن رُّوحِهِ</span>).</p>
<p>The following lines, from the first sermon, form one of the many passages in which this infusion of the divine spirit into man is commented upon by Ali:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ثُمَّ جَمَعَ سُبْحَانَهُ مِنْ حَزْنِ الاْرْضِ وَسَهْلِهَا، وَعَذْبِهَا وَسَبَخِهَا۔ تُرْبَةً سَنَّهَا بالمَاءِ حَتَّى خَلَصَتْ۔ وَلاَطَهَا بِالبَلَّةِ حَتَّى لَزَبَتْ۔ فَجَبَلَ مِنْها صُورَةً ذَاتَ أَحْنَاءَ وَوُصُولَ، وَأَعْضَاءَ وَفُصُول۔ أَجْمَدَهَا حَتَّى اسْتَمْسَكَتْ، وَأَصْلَدَهَا حَتَّى صَلْصَلَتْ۔ لِوَقْت مَعْدُود، وَأجَل مَعْلُوم۔ ثُمَّ نَفَخَ فِيها مِنْ رُوحِهِ فَمَثُلَتْ إِنْساناً ذَا أَذْهَانُ يُجيلُهَا، وَفِكَر يَتَصَرَّفُ بِهَا، وَجَوَارِحَ يَخْتَدِمُهَا، وَأَدَوَات يُقَلِّبُهَا، وَمَعْرِفَة يَفْرُقُ بِهَا بَيْنَ الحَقِّ وَالبَاطِلِ۔</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Then He, glorified be He, gathered up a quantity of dust, from some hard earth and some soft, some sweet earth and some salty, and poured water onto it until it was pure, then kneaded into it some moisture until it stuck together; then He fashioned from this a form with curves and joints, limbs and parts. He compacted it until it held tightly together, solidified it until it became as sounding clay, assigning to it a measured duration and a known consummation. Then He breathed into it of His spirit, and it stood up as a human being, endowed with intellectual powers with which to reflect, thoughts by which to conduct himself, limbs to put to service, instruments at his disposal, and knowledge with which to discern between the true and the false ...</p>
<p>The body had been fashioned entire — its eyes, its ears, the organ of thought all in place — and yet it could neither hear nor see nor reflect until God breathed His spirit into it; only then did it stand up as a human being. It is this in-spiration — literally, the in-breathing and the insertion of the spirit, of God''s breath — that produces consciousness and life. Death, by contrast, is the ex-piration, the departure of the spirit. Life in the absence of aspiration — the intention to be true to one''s spirit — is a kind of death. It is through the spirit that inanimate, unconscious matter is brought to life and to awareness of itself: it generates not only knowledge but self-knowledge as well. This intimate intertwining between the gift of life and the capacity to know — existence that is conscious, and knowledge that is existential — is one of the foundations of any truly spiritual perspective. The question is not one of setting the spirit against the body, but of recognizing that the infusion of the spirit into the body is precisely what brings the body to life; and it is for this reason that knowledge vivifies, in the very measure that it is spiritual: O ye who believe, respond to God and His Messenger when they call you to that which vivifies you (Al-Anfal: 24). According to the Imam, one of the most important aspects of this call from God to human beings is to remind them of the buried treasures of the intellect.</p>
<p>Existentiation is the bestowal of the spirit, which is consciousness. The aql, or intellect, is the principle of articulation for the spirit, and so it is not merely a mental faculty but is itself constitutive of our true life. The intellect is that of which the mental faculty is one formal expression, one mode of its operation — but by no means the only one. The aql is many-faceted, comprising aspects that in our times are rarely associated with intelligence. To be true to one''s intellect — to the treasures buried deep within it, and not merely to the rational functions operative on its surface — amounts to being spiritual.</p>
<p>For Ali, the true intellectual — al-aqil — is one who not only thinks correctly but also acts ethically, and, at the deepest level, one who seeks to realize the ultimate Reality. The intellectual is defined as one who puts all things in their proper place (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العاقل من وضع شئی حق محلہ</span>), one who aligns his acts with the intentions of his Creator. This same alignment is the very definition of justice (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span>); only the true intellectual can therefore be fully just (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَادِل</span>), for only one who sees things as they truly are is able to put them in their right place.</p>
<p>Thought, action, and realization are all the concern of the true intellectual, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَاقِل</span>. Correct thinking, impeccable virtue, and authentic being are inextricably bound up with the intellect in the spiritual ethos considered here. These aspects of the ethos come into sharper focus in the light of the Imam''s discourse to Kumayl.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'intellect, not reason';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The discourse the Imam delivered to Kumayl brings these aspects of the ethos into sharper focus. Its importance lies above all in how deeply esoteric it is. The Imam took his disciple Kumayl by the hand and led him out of Kufa to a graveyard; once the city lay behind them, he drew a deep breath and gave him the following private counsel.</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا كُمَيْل بْن زِيَادٍ، إِنَّ هذهِ الْقُلُوبَ أَوْعِيَةٌ فَخَيْرُهَا أَوْعَاهَا فَاحْفَظْ عَنِّي مَا أَقُولُ لَكَ۔ النَّاسُ ثَلاَثَةٌ</span>- <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَعَالِمٌ رَبَّانِيٌّ وَمُتَعَلِّمٌ عَلَى سَبِيلِ نَجَاةٍ، وَهَمَجٌ رَعَاعٌ أَتْبَاعُ كُلِّ نَاعِقٍ يَمِيلُونَ مَعَ كُلِّ رِيحٍ، لَمْ يَسْتَضِيئُوا بِنُورِ الْعِلْمِ، وَلَمْ يَلْجَؤُوا إِلَى رُكْنٍ وَثِيقٍ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>O Kumayl ibn Ziyad, truly these hearts are vessels, containers for knowledge and wisdom, and the best of them are those which hold the most. So retain from me that which I say to you. People are divided into three types: a lordly knower (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِمٌ رَبَّانِيٌّ</span>); one who seeks knowledge (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَعَلِّمٌ</span>) for the sake of deliverance; and the common folk (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَمَجٌ رَعَاعٌ</span>), the word naming the moth that hurls itself at any flame, following just anyone, swaying with every current, neither desiring to be illumined by the light of knowledge nor seeking refuge in any strong support.</p>
<p>O Kumayl, knowledge is better than wealth, for knowledge guards you, while you must guard your wealth; and wealth diminishes as it is spent, while knowledge only increases as it is disbursed, replenishing itself the more it is given away; the fruits of wealth vanish when the wealth itself is gone, the friends bought with money and the comforts it purchased all falling away with it, while no one can take a man''s knowledge from him. O Kumayl, the wise apprehension of knowledge (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْرِفَةُ الْعِلْمِ</span>) is a religion by which God is worshipped, for din, religion, is kin to dayn, a debt, and it is by acquiring knowledge that a man repays the debt his own intellect has incurred to its Maker. Through it man acquires obedience in his life and a good name after his death. And knowledge is a judge, while wealth is judged: knowledge is active and wealth passive, for the one who knows takes command of what he owns, his knowledge directing him how to handle it, whereas wealth is forever something ruled upon by its possessor.</p>
<p>O Kumayl, those who hoard wealth perish even while they live, but the knowers endure for as long as time subsists; their material forms are absent, but their images in the hearts of men are present. Ah, what abundant knowledge is here (and he pointed to his breast), would that I could find one to bear it. Yes, I found one who was quick to understand, but he could not be trusted with it, exploiting the tools of religion for the sake of worldly gain, empowering himself with the graces of God against His slaves and with His proofs against His friends; for when the vessel of the heart is corrupt, this knowledge, pure and precious as it is, becomes a weapon turned against the very friends of God. Then there was another, obedient to the bearers of truth but lacking heartfelt insight, in whom, at the first appearance of obscurity, doubt was kindled in the heart. So alas, neither this one nor that! Nor one who is greedy for pleasure and submissive to passion, nor one obsessed with acquisition and accumulation, neither of them a guardian of religion in any respect. They resemble nothing so much as grazing cattle. Thus does knowledge die with the death of its bearers, when its trust falls to the worthless and the self-serving.</p>
<p>But indeed, O my God, the earth will never be empty of one who establishes the proof of God, whether overtly and in the open or fearfully and in obscurity, lest God''s proofs and elucidations come to naught. Yet how many are such as these, and where are they? By God, they may be the smallest in number, but with God they are the greatest in rank, for here authority is not the possession of the many, and truth is not settled by the count of those who hold it; the guardians of the proof are few, and it is they who stand highest in the sight of God. Through them God preserves His proofs and elucidations, so that they may entrust them to their compeers and sow them in the hearts of those who resemble them. Through them, knowledge penetrates the reality of insight; they take hold of the very spirit of certainty; they make easy what the extravagant find harsh, and they befriend that by which the ignorant are estranged; with their bodies they keep company with the world, while their spirits are bound to the transcendent realm. They are the vicegerents of God upon His earth, the summoners to His religion. Ah, how I long to see them! Go now, Kumayl, as you will.</p>
<p>The four sentences of the penultimate paragraph describe those who bear the title of lordly knower (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِمٌ رَبَّانِيٌّ</span>): the fewest in number, yet the greatest in the sight of God, and the ones through whom His religion is sustained. By His religion is meant the divine reality of religion — the pure and unchanging essence of the faith, perfect and constant — as against religion in the ordinary sense, whose outward institutions are subject to the changes of human history. From Adam to the Seal of the prophets those outward institutions have shifted with the conditions of men, but the essence, the message, and the purpose have never altered: the salvation of the soul through willing submission to God, as the Quran declares: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ٱلدِّينَ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ ٱلْإِسْلَمُ</span>, &quot;Truly, religion in the sight of God is submission&quot; (Ali Imran: 19). It is through the knowledge of the true knowers that religion stays true to itself, and only through such sages that religion truly lives in the world.</p>
<p>&quot;Through them, knowledge penetrates the reality of insight.&quot; The Imam does not say that their knowledge penetrates it, but that through them knowledge penetrates the reality of insight. Knowledge can remain merely abstract, far removed from that reality. There is the outward, surface knowledge of a thing — its zahir — and the inner, hidden knowledge of the same thing — its batin. Yet knowledge holds a potential that calls out to be realized. It needs to be brought to life so that it may reach something deeper than the words and concepts that give shape to its own outward form, the meaning that lies ready on the surface. A man may have a surface knowledge of a verse of the Quran, of a saying, of why he prays and fasts and how many cycles each prayer requires; but when he presses past that surface and asks what the bowing and the prostration truly represent, a world of knowledge opens that is wholly unlike the bare information he began with. Degrees of knowledge are plainly implied here, and with them the rejection of all literalism and superficiality, of the easy mistake of taking factual information for real knowledge, let alone for wisdom. The shift of consciousness this penetration requires is shown vividly by the Imam''s gesture toward his breast: it is not the chest but the heart hidden behind it that holds knowledge. The chest is the outward form, the zahir that conceals the heart within its batin, and to reach the heart one must first penetrate the chest.</p>
<p>The word chosen to translate hajama is &quot;penetrate,&quot; but the primary sense of the verb is to assault, to take by storm, to charge. In the warfare of that age the battle lines were drawn as a right wing, a left wing, and a center — the heart of the army (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْبُ الْعَسْكَرِ</span>), where the king or commander stood. The aim of the assault was to break through the wings and reach that heart, for once the king was taken the battle was won, and the victor took command of everything the defeated had held: the kingdom, its treasury, its army, its people. The chessboard preserves the same rule. So the word helps to show that conceptual knowledge holds a latent force — one might almost call it an explosive power — deriving from the reality of knowledge, its haqiqa or spiritual essence. It is this that calls out to be made real through tahaqquq, realization in depth rather than mere verification on the surface; and the striving by which one storms the outer wall of words to reach the heart of their meaning is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحْقِيق</span>, investigation. This haqiqa can be pointed to, but never captured, by the words and concepts that make up its formal, communicable aspect — just as the chest can only be opened in order to reach the heart. By means of this inner force, which its outer conceptual form at once hides and evokes, knowledge can effect a shift of consciousness from one degree of knowledge to another: a man begins to see the very realities of this world differently from those who rest in the surface play of cause and effect, so that the same act which is a burden to one is a delight to another. An indirect, mental conception of reality thus prepares the way for a penetrating, existential perception of reality. This is the difference between mere sight and true insight: sight reaches only the outward, superficial reality, whereas the reality of insight opens the intelligence to reality as such — ordinary insight, like abstract knowledge, remaining only at the surface level of understanding. The same idea appears in a saying of Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) concerning the true understanding that comes to plumb, and so truly to know, the buried treasures of knowledge: &quot;He who understands knows the depth of knowledge (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ فَهِمَ عَلِمَ غَوْرَ اَلْعِلْمِ</span>).&quot; And the effort that true knowledge demands, as against the mere piling up of facts, is likewise indicated: &quot;Through learning and assimilating knowledge, until it becomes deeply rooted in one''s consciousness, is knowledge attained (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِالتَّعَلُّمِ يُنَالُ الْعِلْمُ</span>).&quot;</p>
<p>The urgency of realizing this immanent power within knowledge — of moving from the abstract to the concrete, the potential to the actual, the static to the dynamic, the formal to the essential — is powerfully evoked by hajama, a word bound up with battle. It warns against taking the first understanding of an idea for its full comprehension, and full intellectual comprehension for the idea''s living interiorization in the spirit. For when knowledge at last storms the surface of religion and takes the heart, a transformation begins from within: the heart, now illumined by the realities it has seized, takes command of the limbs and the actions, which are the soul''s own resources, exactly as the victor takes command of the kingdom he has won. This interiorization is a process of endless unfolding within, not the mere registering of some fact from without. It requires the effective integration of knowledge within being: knowledge from without must be transformed into being from within, woven into the deepest texture of one''s existence. Knowledge thus demands not merely the attention of the mind but the total orientation of the soul.</p>
<p>In this ethos, then, the effective integration (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>) of knowledge within being cannot be reached without the participation of the whole soul; and this in turn requires the elimination of all the vices that flow from self-centeredness. Thus the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَاقِل</span>, the true intellectual, is described by the Imam as one constantly engaged in struggle against his own soul — against the lower inclinations, the passionate whims, and the distracting caprices of the ego. These vices are not confined to the plane of morality; they encroach upon the domain of knowledge as well, and this becomes clear in the very measure that knowledge is grasped as a concrete vision of things rather than a merely notional or conceptual grasp of them. The vices are then seen as so many clouds obscuring the vision proper to the reality of insight. According to the Quran, evil actions become a rust that covers over the heart: &quot;Nay, their hearts have become rusted by that which they have done&quot; (Al-Mutaffifin: 14). The aqil, then, is engaged in a constant inner struggle against the tendencies that not only sully the soul but blind the heart. As a virtuous man he opposes vice on the moral plane, because it runs contrary to virtue; but vice is opposed for a further reason, one that lies on the higher plane of spiritual reality: it contradicts the nature of the Real, it obscures the reality of insight, and it blinds the eye of the heart which alone can see God.</p>
<p>This does not deny the validity of the moral perspective; the stress falls rather on the deeper motive that drives the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَاقِل</span> toward virtue, a motive that rises above the moral plane while including it. Morality appears here as the fruit or effect, and spirituality as the seed or cause. By drawing attention to the intellectual side of the aqil''s motive for shedding the vices, the Imam shows more clearly how immorality is rooted in ignorance; and to uproot that ignorance, for the true intellectual, is to dissolve the very substance of the vices. This uprooting demands great effort, a relentless battle within — indeed the most formidable of all battles, al-jihad al-akbar, the greatest struggle, as the Prophet defined it.</p>
<p>The following sayings of Ali bear on the importance of this inner struggle:</p>
<p>&quot;Struggling against the soul through knowledge, such is the mark of the intellect.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;The strongest of people are those who are strongest against their own souls.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;Truly, one who fights his own soul in obedience to God, and does not sin against Him, has the rank of the righteous martyr in the sight of God.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;The ultimate battle is that of a man against his own soul.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;He who knows his soul fights it.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;No jihad is more excellent than the jihad of the soul.&quot;</p>
<p>In another saying the Imam clarifies what the Prophet meant by naming the greatest jihad the jihad of the soul (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفْس</span>). It is not so much a matter of fighting against the soul as of the battle for the soul itself. This emerges from the metaphor by which the Imam defines the struggle: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَقْل</span>, the intellect, leads the forces of al-Rahman, the Compassionate; al-hawa — whim, caprice, desire — commands the forces of al-shaytan, the devil; and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفْس</span>, the soul, wavers between them, drawn by both (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَجَاذِبَةٌ بَيْنَهُمَا</span>), falling under the sway of whichever of the two prevails.</p>
<p>These sayings make clear that for the true intellectual the soul in its entirety must be &quot;conquered&quot; and enlisted on the side of the intellect. The aim is not to destroy the lower soul but to free it from the destructive influence of its caprice, its egoistic whims, its subjectivism, its individualism — all of which are gathered up in the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَى</span>. The Prophet, the Quran tells us, does not speak from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَى</span>; rather: &quot;It is naught but a revelation revealed&quot; (An-Najm: 3-4). The divine objectivity of revelation is here set against the human subjectivity of passionate whim. In the setting of the holy war within, the Imam''s sayings bring a parallel opposition into focus: the objectivity of the intellect must prevail over the subjectivity of individual caprice and egotistic desire. It is the intellect that represents — in the sense of &quot;makes present&quot; — the divine power of al-Rahman, and that combats the forces of al-shaytan as embodied in al-hawa.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَى</span> is used further in the Quran to denote desires that spring from the ego, by which the ego becomes a kind of false god: &quot;Hast thou seen him who maketh his desire his god?&quot; (25: 43; almost identical at 45: 23). This is the subtle or hidden idolatry (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشِّرْكُ الْخَفِيُّ</span>) that the Prophet described as more hidden than a black ant crawling on a dark stone in a moonless night. To root out this idolatry of the ego, a force higher than the ego itself must be brought to bear, since the ego can never be controlled by anything that belongs to it; and that higher force is the divine Reality, access to which is opened through the power of the intellect and the graces of faith. This complementarity between the effort to overcome the egoism of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَى</span> and the inflow of divine grace is well expressed in the following Quranic verse: &quot;As for he who fears the station of his Lord, and restraineth his soul from whim (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَى</span>), verily, Paradise will be his abode&quot; (An-Naziat: 40-41).</p>
<p>The soul''s fundamental energy is not to be destroyed but converted and redirected — away from the fleeting objects of individualistic desire and the impulses of al-shaytan, and toward the one true object named by al-Rahman. This &quot;conquest&quot; of the soul is far more than the arrival at some mental conception. It is not only the mind, the will, and the character that must be transformed; the soul''s very deepest disposition must be integrated into the truth perceived by the intellect, if a man is to perceive that truth in all its dimensions and ramifications across the different planes of being.</p>
<p>For the Imam the need to win the soul over in its totality follows naturally from his understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, the most fundamental principle of Islam. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> is a verbal noun, and to translate it as mere oneness or unity fails to capture its full force. Literally it means &quot;affirming one&quot; — the affirmation of and belief in God''s oneness, which is the outward, theological meaning — or &quot;realizing one,&quot; the realization that there is but one reality, which is the inner, spiritual meaning. In the Imam''s view, then, the absolutely Real is perceived not only as being one but also as making one, as integrating. The Real is not only unique; it is also unifying. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> thus comes to mean not merely affirming the oneness of God, but being one oneself so as to reflect the One perfectly: to know God is to be &quot;integrated.&quot; Here one may see a link with the etymology of aql, whose primary meaning is &quot;to bind.&quot; Yet whereas the later mystical tradition tends to stress the limiting aspect of this word — the intellect as that which defines and encloses meaning, over against the heart, which intuits and discloses spiritual truth — here the intellect is that which &quot;binds together&quot; the scattered elements of the soul into an organic unity. This principle is expressed in the first of the following sayings of the Imam, each of which merits deep reflection:</p>
<p>&quot;He who knows God integrates himself (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ عَرَفَ اللهَ تَوَحَّدَ</span>).&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;He who knows his soul disengages himself (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ عَرَفَ نَفْسَهُ تَجَرَّدَ</span>).&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;He who knows people isolates himself (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ عَرَفَ النَّاسَ تَفَرَّدَ</span>).&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;He who knows the world withholds himself (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ عَرَفَ الدُّنْيَا تَزَهَّدَ</span>).&quot;</p>
<p>Elsewhere this total adherence to the Real is described by the Imam as &quot;sincerity&quot; — sidq or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span> — terms that mean far more than simple truthfulness, as the root of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span> already shows. The verb khalusa means to be utterly pure, true to one''s essence, unmixed with any foreign element. This side of the meaning comes through clearly in the following saying of the Imam: &quot;Make purely for God (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلِصْ لِلَّهِ</span>) your action and your knowledge, your love and your hatred, your taking and your leaving, your speech and your silence.&quot;</p>
<p>To be totally sincere, then, means to make oneself &quot;purely&quot; for God — to carry no &quot;stain&quot; of impurity, understood here as whatever is other than God, in one''s devotion, action, intention, or even sentiment. One must love and hate only in relation to one''s bond with God. Of all the tendencies that work their way into one''s motives and intentions, the most destructive is egotism. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>, pure sincerity, is the active effort to be, think, and act purely for God — that is, with selflessness — which is why the Imam lays such stress on the holy war within the soul as the &quot;ultimate battle.&quot; One who wins this battle attains the rank of the &quot;righteous martyr,&quot; for the victor here is a martyr in the truest sense: he has sacrificed his lower life for the sake of God, with whom is the real life — the life witnessed by the intellect, the only element within human consciousness able to look upon the soul as &quot;the other&quot; with genuine objectivity.</p>
<p>As the saying holds, &quot;struggling against the soul through knowledge, such is the mark of the intellect.&quot; The intellectual engaged in this battle is also one who bears &quot;witness&quot; (al-shahid, meaning both martyr and witness, as in the original Greek sense of the word). He bears witness to God through the intellect''s sacrifice of whatever opposes the truth of God, and the chief opponent is his own egotism, his own &quot;false life.&quot; To possess perfect <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>, then, is to be rid of all hypocritical self-preoccupation and to put in its place a sincere theocentricity — a mode of being centered upon God rather than oneself. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>, therefore, means being for God, or belonging to God, in the sense pointed to by the Quranic verse: &quot;Say: Truly my worship and my sacrifice, and my living and my dying, are for God, Lord of the universes&quot; (Al-Anam: 162).</p>
<p>That worship is the first thing named in this verse is of particular significance for understanding Ali s spiritual ethos, and the indispensable role of worship and devotion must be emphasized in setting that ethos out. The second chapter takes up this role in the context of the quest for the fundamental virtue of justice. The ultimate fruits of worship and devotion — of which the quintessence is the remembrance of God — are the subject of the third chapter. One of the defining features of the Imam''s spiritual ethos is indeed the emphasis he places on prayer, taking that term in its widest sense to include all the means — from formal worship and supplication to meditation and reflection, remembrance and contemplation — by which the individual is oriented and attuned to the Real, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>.</p>
<p>Worship is therefore an integral aspect of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>, precisely because it answers to and sustains a fundamental dimension of the human soul. That within the soul which yearns to worship, and is nourished by worship alone, must be given its due. In a spiritual context, if worship is pushed to the margins, one''s sincerity is compromised: one is no longer being sincere with God, no longer being &quot;just&quot; to God, no longer rendering God His due. And the spiritual logic of the Imam holds that if one is not just with God, one cannot be just to His creatures. &quot;Whoso establishes well-being between himself and God,&quot; he says, &quot;God establishes well-being between him and mankind.&quot;</p>
<p>One might also cite here the Quranic verse: &quot;Worship thy Lord until certainty cometh to thee&quot; (Al-Hijr: 99). The quest for knowledge demands total dedication if it is to yield its inner riches, and that dedication includes the near-alchemical transformation of consciousness worked by total concentration on the divine Reality — prayer in one of its deepest modes. In this form of prayer, the intensity of dedication is such that nothing else can occupy one''s consciousness alongside its object: the &quot;remembrance&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِکْر</span>) of God strictly implies the forgetting of everything else, and above all of oneself. The spiritual reality of the dhikr can only be reached when &quot;you forget your soul in your remembrance.&quot; Concentration upon the divine Reality as the supreme object of knowledge, made present through the Name or Names of God, is thus a mode of prayer — one which, to be effective, requires both presence of mind and a genuine engagement of the will. To pray absent-mindedly or mechanically is far from enough. On this the Imam says, &quot;He who prays without exertion is like one who shoots [arrows] without a bow.&quot; In the same way, there is nothing passive or easy about acquiring knowledge: &quot;Knowledge calls out for action; if it is answered [it is of avail], otherwise it departs.&quot;</p>
<p>The same idea is expressed more forcefully in this saying of the Imam: &quot;When you hear knowledge [being imparted] cleave to it, do not mix it up with jest, lest hearts spew it forth.&quot; Knowledge that is not taken seriously will be of no avail; it remains &quot;undigested&quot; and cannot be assimilated by the &quot;heart&quot; — that innermost faculty which has access to the &quot;reality of insight&quot; and which can thus &quot;see&quot; God. For it is the heart that sees Allah, not by eyesight, not by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بصارۃ</span>, but through the spiritual insight, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بصیرۃ</span>, generated by the true principles of faith, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقَائِقُ الْاِیمَانِ</span>. Indeed, it is not the individual who &quot;assimilates&quot; knowledge to himself; it is knowledge — or rather the reality of being expressed through true knowledge — that assimilates the individual to itself: &quot;Acquire knowledge, and [true] life will acquire you.&quot; The relationship between life and consciousness, so central to the intrinsic function of the spirit, is again at the heart of this maxim. At first the individual seeks knowledge and cultivates consciousness; then life &quot;seeks&quot; the individual, as being makes itself more fully real within him. Put differently, the individual first makes an active effort to integrate knowledge within his being; but at a certain point this activity turns into passive receptivity: being — &quot;true life&quot; — integrates the individual within itself. To return to the Imam''s guidance to Kumayl: &quot;Through them knowledge penetrates the reality of insight.&quot; It is not the knowers who penetrate; it is knowledge that penetrates to the reality of insight, doing so through them, and this in the very measure of their effacement within knowledge — an effacement that is the shadow cast by their effective integration within the life that &quot;acquires&quot; them.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'the discourse to kumayl';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The discourse to Kumayl continues: &quot;They rejoice in their intimacy with the spirit of certainty.&quot; This sentence speaks directly to the sheer happiness that flows from the life of the spirit. In the Arabic phrase <em>wa basharu ruh al-yaqin</em>, the word <em>bashara</em> means first of all to have intimate experience of something, to come into direct physical contact with it. In this form the verb also carries a sense of joy, so that &quot;to rejoice in intimate contact&quot; conveys better what the single word holds. To touch the spirit of certainty is not to reach some cold logical fact at the end of a purely mental process. It means entering deeply into the very object of one''s certainty — and that object, in the end, is nothing less than the divine itself. This reality is infinitely more blessed than any bliss of Paradise one could imagine, for the Quran affirms that the divine <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضْوَان</span>, its beatitude and contentment, is &quot;greater&quot; than Paradise. It cannot, then, but bestow joy upon the soul that truly opens itself to it:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">At-Tawbah: 72</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَعَدَ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَٱلْمُؤْمِنَتِ جَنَّتٍۢ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَرُ خَلِدِينَ فِيهَا وَمَسَكِنَ طَيِّبَةًۭ فِى جَنَّتِ عَدْنٍۢ ۚ وَرِضْوَنٌۭ مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ أَكْبَرُ ۚ ذَلِكَ هُوَ ٱلْفَوْزُ ٱلْعَظِيمُ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;God has promised the believers, men and women, gardens beneath which rivers flow, wherein they abide, and goodly dwellings in gardens of Eden. And beatitude from God is greater. That is the supreme triumph&quot; (At-Tawbah: 72).</p></blockquote>
<p>In the words of Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>), &quot;Learning and reflecting upon knowledge is the delight of the knowers.&quot;</p>
<p>This might be called a way of knowing steeped in joy, for &quot;the most joyous of mankind is the intellectual (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَاقِل</span>).&quot; Such a view stands in sharp contrast to the modern conception, modeled on science, that treats knowledge as dry, cold, and abstract. It deserves careful thought, for it opens up a crucial dimension of the spiritual knowledge proper to the Imam''s perspective, and indeed to every spiritual tradition worthy of the name. To engage with truths ought to bring happiness, and it does so to the degree that those truths are fundamental to one''s own being, or to Being as such. In the end these two are one and the same, given the uniqueness — the &quot;one-and-onliness&quot; — of Being. What has no second, no other, cannot be counted; Being is indivisible. So the being of the individual cannot finally differ from Being as such, however vast the difference in existence between the individual and God — a difference, indeed, past all common measure. True knowledge is knowledge of Being, and so it cannot help but be a form of self-discovery, even though knowledge of the Absolute is impossible without absolute self-abandonment. The highest discovery calls for the deepest self-effacement.</p>
<p>In the early stages, though, the true knowledge the intellectual gains is a self-discovery that brings the happiness of a homecoming — a return from the exile of ignorance. This is so because &quot;the most intellectual of people&quot; is &quot;the one who is closest to God&quot; and &quot;the one who is most alive.&quot; To be close to God is to be fully alive, and this true life gives a deep joy, the <em>joie de vivre</em>, the sheer joy of living. It is a joy that belongs to existence itself, independent of any particular experience, pleasant or not.</p>
<p>Here the crucial bond between life and consciousness comes into view — between the fruits of the in-spiration of God''s spirit on one side, and this life of spiritual awareness and joy on the other. Those who are true to the spirit of God within them truly possess joy. It is likewise from their intimate and unshakeable certainty of God''s goodness and compassion that the Imam defines the true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَقِيهٌ</span> — not so much &quot;the jurist&quot; as &quot;one who truly understands&quot; — as &quot;he who does not make people despair of the mercy of God, and does not make them lose hope in the gracious spirit of God (<em>rawh Allah</em>).&quot; So deeply is the sage immersed in the joyous certainty of God''s mercy that no room is left for despair. To despair of this mercy is itself a mark of unbelief:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Hijr: 56</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَالَ وَمَن يَقْنَطُ مِن رَّحْمَةِ رَبِّهِۦٓ إِلَّا ٱلضَّآلُّونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;And who despaireth of the mercy of his Lord except those who stray in error?&quot; (Al-Hijr: 56).</p></blockquote>
<p>The Quran''s assurances of mercy come fully alive in souls that possess a deep knowledge of the blessed nature of the divine Reality — a knowledge lived, not merely thought:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-A''raf: 156</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱكْتُبْ لَنَا فِى هَذِهِ ٱلدُّنْيَا حَسَنَةًۭ وَفِى ٱلْآخِرَةِ إِنَّا هُدْنَآ إِلَيْكَ ۚ قَالَ عَذَابِىٓ أُصِيبُ بِهِۦ مَنْ أَشَآءُ ۖ وَرَحْمَتِى وَسِعَتْ كُلَّ شَىْءٍۢ ۚ فَسَأَكْتُبُهَا لِلَّذِينَ يَتَّقُونَ وَيُؤْتُونَ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ وَٱلَّذِينَ هُم بِـَٔايَتِنَا يُؤْمِنُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;My mercy encompasseth all things&quot; (Al-Araf: 156)</p></blockquote>
<p>and</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-An''am: 54</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا جَآءَكَ ٱلَّذِينَ يُؤْمِنُونَ بِـَٔايَتِنَا فَقُلْ سَلَمٌ عَلَيْكُمْ ۖ كَتَبَ رَبُّكُمْ عَلَىٰ نَفْسِهِ ٱلرَّحْمَةَ ۖ أَنَّهُۥ مَنْ عَمِلَ مِنكُمْ سُوٓءًۢا بِجَهَلَةٍۢ ثُمَّ تَابَ مِنۢ بَعْدِهِۦ وَأَصْلَحَ فَأَنَّهُۥ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;thy Lord has prescribed for Himself mercy&quot; (Al-Anam: 54).</p></blockquote>
<p>These are the ones who truly understand, the true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُقَهَاءُ</span>.</p>
<p>This opening to joy through intimate knowledge of God''s mercy cannot be separated from beauty, and in the Imam''s view the perception of beauty is another unexpected companion of the intellect. The love of beauty is not merely a matter of private taste; it belongs objectively to the intellect''s own working. Beauty also leads straight to the heart of morality. The Arabic <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> is often translated simply as &quot;virtue&quot; or &quot;excellence,&quot; yet one of its plain, literal meanings is &quot;making beautiful,&quot; since <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَ</span> means &quot;beauty.&quot; Virtue, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>, can thus be understood as what makes the soul beautiful. Seen this way, the bond among intellect, morality, and beauty grows far clearer. &quot;The excellence of the intellect,&quot; says Ali, &quot;is in the beauty of things outward and inward (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمَالُ الظَّوَاهِرِ وَالْبَوَاطِنِ</span>).&quot; The outward beauty of forms and actions must be matched by inward beauty — beauty of soul, or virtue in its fullest and deepest sense. All of this follows naturally from two sayings attributed to the Prophet: &quot;Truly God is beautiful and He loves beauty,&quot; and &quot;Assume the character traits of God.&quot;</p>
<p>To cultivate beautiful traits within oneself makes one lovable to God, since He loves beauty. And making oneself lovable to God cannot be separated from imitating the virtues brought to perfection in the soul of the Prophet:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ali ''Imran: 31</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ إِن كُنتُمْ تُحِبُّونَ ٱللَّهَ فَٱتَّبِعُونِى يُحْبِبْكُمُ ٱللَّهُ وَيَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ ذُنُوبَكُمْ ۗ وَٱللَّهُ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Say: O ye who believe, if ye love God, follow me, the Prophet; God will love you&quot; (Ali Imran: 31).</p></blockquote>
<p>Another saying of the Imam points to this drawing-down of God''s love through making oneself beautiful in the fullest sense: &quot;The foundation of the intellect is the assimilation of love (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَّلُ الْعَقْلِ التَّوَدُّدُ</span>).&quot; Instead of speaking of love as such (<em>wudd/mawadda</em>), the Imam chooses the form <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَدُّد</span> to draw attention to the need to make oneself lovable to God. The word points to a twofold work: taking the principle of love into oneself and making it real there, and at the same time letting the love felt within shine outward — for love is at once an outward radiance and an inward attraction.</p>
<p>To follow the Prophet, and so make oneself open to the inflowing of divine love, means among other things cultivating the prophetic virtues. These virtues are called prophetic not because they belong to the Prophet alone, but because he is held to have realized virtue in all its fullness and is therefore the exemplar, par excellence, of virtue itself. Beauty and love are not the only qualities that give shape to the intellect''s openness to the divine Reality. Kindness and contentment, courtesy, generosity and modesty, and wise forbearance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلْم</span>) matter just as much. Not one of these moral qualities is missing from the soul of the true intellectual. There can be no wisdom or intellect in the fullest sense in a soul that lacks them. It is exactly the presence of these virtues that lets the intellect grow in depth and in breadth. Without them it stays trapped on the flat, horizontal plane of its own operations, held to the work of reason alone. Such reason, until faith touches it and grace deepens it, does not know what lies beneath the surface of its own field. It stays blind to the hidden treasures of the intellect, and blind to the need to gather the whole soul within the mold of that integral, or unifying, virtue which gives intelligence its integrity. This making-one, this integration, lies at the very root of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, now understood as a living process of spiritual unification demanded by the very idea of God''s oneness. Truth must plainly inspire the soul; but just as necessary, in the other direction, is that virtue should aerate the intelligence — the leaven that lets it rise to its full dimensions.</p>
<p>In this perspective, sinful and immoral qualities are seen not only as vices but as so many blind spots, so many failures of the intellect. Vice sickens the heart; it blinds the heart''s eye and so leaves it unable to see the Real. As the Quran says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 10</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِى قُلُوبِهِم مَّرَضٌۭ فَزَادَهُمُ ٱللَّهُ مَرَضًۭا ۖ وَلَهُمْ عَذَابٌ أَلِيمٌۢ بِمَا كَانُوا۟ يَكْذِبُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;In their hearts is a disease and God increased them in disease&quot; (Al-Baqarah: 10)</p></blockquote>
<p>and</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Hajj: 46</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَلَمْ يَسِيرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَتَكُونَ لَهُمْ قُلُوبٌۭ يَعْقِلُونَ بِهَآ أَوْ آذَانٌۭ يَسْمَعُونَ بِهَا ۖ فَإِنَّهَا لَا تَعْمَى ٱلْأَبْصَرُ وَلَكِن تَعْمَى ٱلْقُلُوبُ ٱلَّتِى فِى ٱلصُّدُورِ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;It is not the sights that are blind, but blind are the hearts&quot; (Al-Hajj: 46).</p></blockquote>
<p>All the vices are contained within egotism, which in turn is often called simply caprice (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَوَى</span>). In one saying the Imam treats the conquest of caprice as the same as salvation: &quot;He attains deliverance whose intellect dominates his caprice.&quot; Deliverance, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَاحٌ</span>, which carries the sense both of salvation and of blessedness, is thus within reach to some degree here and now; it is not a grace granted only after death. Such deliverance means tasting the joy that flows from certainty, from intimate contact with the spirit of that certainty. As for how to cultivate an openness to this spirit of certainty: beyond obedience to the rules and regulations of the revealed Law, what is required is that union of pure intention and sincere devotion that the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span> expresses. Without this sincerity, this purity, obedience to formal rules is nothing but hypocrisy (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِفَاقٌ</span>) and pretension (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>) — which the Imam in turn calls the gravest of all sins, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, the setting-up of partners to God: &quot;Know that the slightest pretension (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>) is polytheism.&quot; This saying can be read as a commentary on one of the Quran''s weightiest condemnations of hypocrisy. The verses speak of those who deny the essence of faith while outwardly keeping to it:</p>
<p>&quot;Hast thou seen the one who belieth religion? He is the one who repelleth the orphan and urgeth not the feeding of the poor. So woe be to those who pray, those who are heedless of their prayer, who want only to be seen, and withhold small kindnesses&quot; (Al-Maun: 1-7).</p>
<p>This short sura makes plain that formal acts of devotion have no value at all unless they are woven into a life of virtue and performed with the right intention — that is, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>. It also helps explain why the Imam''s ethos is called here not merely religious but spiritual: it carries the ideals of religion to their deepest application. To be spiritual in this sense is precisely to be true to one''s intellect, true to the treasures buried deep within it, and not merely to the reasoning that works on its surface. Without this depth, religion is empty. &quot;There is no religion for one who has no intellect,&quot; says the Imam. And likewise: &quot;Action without knowledge is error.&quot; What counts is the intention behind the action, for &quot;action, all action, is dust, except what is purified within it.&quot; This turning away from the action in itself, toward an inner state of being from which good actions must flow, clearly reflects the Quranic teaching:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Hajj: 37</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَن يَنَالَ ٱللَّهَ لُحُومُهَا وَلَا دِمَآؤُهَا وَلَكِن يَنَالُهُ ٱلتَّقْوَىٰ مِنكُمْ ۚ كَذَلِكَ سَخَّرَهَا لَكُمْ لِتُكَبِّرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ مَا هَدَىٰكُمْ ۗ وَبَشِّرِ ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;It is not their sacrificed flesh nor their blood that reacheth God, but it is piety from you that reacheth Him&quot; (Al-Hajj: 37).</p></blockquote>
<p>&quot;There is no religion for one who has no intellect.&quot; Perhaps no group shows the truth of this saying better than the Kharijites. Among those who rebelled against the Imam were many readers (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرَّاء</span>, sing. <em>qari</em>) of the Quran — men who enjoyed considerable religious prestige for their Quranic knowledge and their intense worship. Their piety, though, was more apparent than real; it was in fact a zeal born of bitterness, a caricature of true piety. When his supporters spoke of the long night vigils kept by members of this group, the Imam answered sharply: &quot;Sleeping with certainty is better than praying with doubt.&quot; Certainty brings peace and joy. So for one immersed in what the Imam calls the spirit of certainty, sleep is a rest of the body that only deepens the happiness flowing from a good conscience and from spiritual certitude — a certitude that is itself a kind of unbroken wakefulness, an awakeness to higher realities. Sleep of the body, then, does not contradict wakefulness of the spirit. As the hadith of the Prophet has it: &quot;My eyes sleep, but my heart sleeps not.&quot;</p>
<p>When it comes to formal worship, the true intellectual, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَاقِل</span> — whom the Imam defines as the one who puts each thing in its proper place (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العاقل من وضع شئی حق محلہ</span>) — is one who obeys the divine command to pray. Yet he does not pray by rote, supposing that the act alone is enough. He knows that such obedience is necessary but not sufficient for opening himself to grace. There must be awareness of what one is doing and why; and it is this awareness, together with virtue, inward beauty, that draws divine acceptance. &quot;The most worthy knowledge for you,&quot; says the Imam, &quot;is that by virtue of whose presence action is accepted by God.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;They make easy what the extravagant find harsh; they befriend that by which the ignorant are estranged.&quot; This sentence bears, among other things, on how the remembrance of death — its certainty, and the renunciation of this world that certainty demands — works upon the souls of the true sages. To begin with, what the extravagant, the greedy, worldly sort find harsh is outward hardship, difficulty, and loss. The Quran views all such circumstances as the trials that must accompany life in this world:</p>
<p>&quot;And We shall try you with something of fear and hunger and loss of property and life and fruits; but give glad tidings to the patient, those who, when struck by affliction say: Verily, we belong to God and verily unto Him we are ever-returning. These are the ones upon whom there are blessings from their Lord, and mercy; and these are the ones who are rightly guided&quot; (Al-Baqarah: 155-157).</p>
<p>The passage continues cleanly. Here is the articulated chapter prose.</p>
<p>The rightly-guided perceive the blessings poured out upon those who stay patient under affliction—a patience that grows from their knowledge of the truth: &quot;By the Age, truly man is in a state of loss, except those who believe and do virtuous deeds and exhort one another to truth and exhort one another to patience&quot; (Al-Asr: 1-3). These believers stand firm through trials, rooted in their awareness of where they came from and where they return, and in the knowledge that unto Him we are ever-returning. So they can make the trials and hardships of life easy to bear, while the extravagant meet those same trials only as harshness and bitterness. The ease folded into every difficulty stays hidden from the ignorant but is plain to the wise: &quot;Verily, with hardship cometh ease; verily with hardship cometh ease&quot; (Ash-Sharh: 5-6). The wise do more than await ease as the certain fruit of hardship; they taste that fruit already as the seed hidden inside hardship itself, for they befriend the very thing that the ignorant flee.</p>
<p>One layer of this meaning is lit by a saying of Ali: &quot;Death is more intimate to me than the breast of the mother is to the suckling babe.&quot; In the verse cited above, the mention of trials and hardships follows a reminder that those slain in the path of God are not truly dead, &quot;nay, they are alive but ye perceive not.&quot; This affirms the life hereafter, but it also points to the inner meaning of being slain for God: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْفَنَاءُ فِي اللَّهِ</span>, extinction in God. The Imam''s intimacy with death can be understood in relation to this death while still alive, which he touches on in another saying: &quot;I am the death of the dead.&quot; Because life in the world is itself a kind of death—a limitation, and so a lack—set against the boundless fullness of the divine realms, dying to this death amounts to true life:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-''Ankabut: 64</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا هَذِهِ ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ ٱلدُّنْيَآ إِلَّا لَهْوٌۭ وَلَعِبٌۭ ۚ وَإِنَّ ٱلدَّارَ ٱلْآخِرَةَ لَهِىَ ٱلْحَيَوَانُ ۚ لَوْ كَانُوا۟ يَعْلَمُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;And the life of this world is nothing but sport and play,&quot; says the Quran, &quot;and verily, the abode of the Hereafter, that is true life, did they but know&quot; (Al-Ankabut: 64).</p></blockquote>
<p>What must be negated is not the physical world as such, but worldliness—the self-centered attachment to the world. One key principle of the Imam''s outlook, stressed again and again throughout the <em>Nahj al-balagha</em>, bears directly here: the principle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْدٌ</span>, which no single English word can render adequately. It gathers several meanings: asceticism, austerity, abstention, detachment, doing without, and making little of. The following passage from the <em>Nahj</em> conveys well the attitude of the <em>zahid</em> toward this world:</p>
<p>The world is an abode for which annihilation is ordained, and for its people departure from it is decreed. It is sweet and green, and it hastens to one who seeks it, and confounds the heart that gazes upon it. So set out from it with the best of the provision available to you, and ask not from it above what suffices and seek not from it more than what can be attained.</p>
<p>What is this best of provision, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَادٌ</span>? In many places the <em>Nahj</em> describes it as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, another word that resists simple translation, its meaning spanning piety, fear of God, awe, and God-consciousness. &quot;I exhort you, O slaves of God, to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>, which is the provision,&quot; says the Imam. This provision for the next world is won only in the measure of one''s detachment from the perishable side of the life of this world, for &quot;whoso desireth that which hasteneth away, in that We hasten for him what We will&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Isra: 18</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَّن كَانَ يُرِيدُ ٱلْعَاجِلَةَ عَجَّلْنَا لَهُۥ فِيهَا مَا نَشَآءُ لِمَن نُّرِيدُ ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَا لَهُۥ جَهَنَّمَ يَصْلَىٰهَا مَذْمُومًۭا مَّدْحُورًۭا</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Whoever desires the fleeting life, We hasten for him therein what We will, for whom We intend; then We appoint for him Hell, wherein he burns, disgraced and rejected.&quot; (Al-Isra: 18)</p></blockquote>
<p>The Imam describes the contemptible side of the world in vivid terms. &quot;By God, this world of yours is more contemptible in my eyes than a chewed-up bone of a pig in the hand of a leper.&quot; &quot;An image of the world is the snake, soft to touch, but virulent poison within.&quot; The world is &quot;as worthless to me as that which drips from the nose of a goat.&quot; It is the grandeur and beauty of God that renders the world small and distasteful; only in the light of the Absolute do the world''s pleasures and attractions show themselves as fleeting and illusory: &quot;The greatness of the Creator for you belittles creatures in your view.&quot; In the same spirit, the Imam speaks of a brother in God: &quot;What made him great in my eyes was the smallness of the world in his eyes (<em><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَكَانَ يُعَظِّمُهُ فِي عَيْنِي صِغَرُ الدُّنْيَا فِي عَيْنِهِ</span></em>).&quot;</p>
<p>This, in the Quranic usage of <em>zahid</em>, is the real meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زُهْدٌ</span>. In the sura named after Joseph (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُوسُفُ</span>), the brothers of Yusuf held him of little value in selling him for a small price, and in relation to him they are described as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَاهِدِينَ</span>:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Yusuf: 20</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَشَرَوْهُ بِثَمَنٍۭ بَخْسٍۢ دَرَهِمَ مَعْدُودَةٍۢ وَكَانُوا۟ فِيهِ مِنَ ٱلزَّهِدِينَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;And they sold him for a paltry price, a few dirhams counted out, and they were of those who deemed him of little worth.&quot; (Yusuf: 20)</p></blockquote>
<p>In the Imam''s outlook the world is likewise held of little value—not valueless outright, but rendered all but insignificant beside the Hereafter, beside eternity, beside ultimate reality. All created things shrink to tiny, fleeting shadows bound for annihilation: &quot;Everything perishes except His Face&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Qasas: 88</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تَدْعُ مَعَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَهًا آخَرَ ۘ لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا هُوَ ۚ كُلُّ شَىْءٍ هَالِكٌ إِلَّا وَجْهَهُۥ ۚ لَهُ ٱلْحُكْمُ وَإِلَيْهِ تُرْجَعُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;And do not invoke with God another deity. There is no deity except Him. Everything will perish except His Face. His is the judgment, and to Him you will be returned.&quot; (Al-Qasas: 88)</p></blockquote>
<p>Yet the world is not stripped of all value; indeed, its significance lies precisely in what it cannot help but signify: &quot;And there is nothing that does not hymn His glory with praise&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Isra: 44</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تُسَبِّحُ لَهُ ٱلسَّمَوَتُ ٱلسَّبْعُ وَٱلْأَرْضُ وَمَن فِيهِنَّ ۚ وَإِن مِّن شَىْءٍ إِلَّا يُسَبِّحُ بِحَمْدِهِۦ وَلَكِن لَّا تَفْقَهُونَ تَسْبِيحَهُمْ ۗ إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ حَلِيمًا غَفُورًۭا</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;The seven heavens and the earth and whatever is in them exalt Him. And there is not a thing except that it exalts Him by His praise, but you do not understand their way of exalting. Indeed, He is ever Forbearing and Forgiving.&quot; (Al-Isra: 44)</p></blockquote>
<p>Everything in the world, every creature in existence, proclaims the boundless creativity of the Creator. So that the imagination might grasp the mystery of this universal praise, the Quran says: &quot;And the birds in flight, each one knoweth its prayer and glorification&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Nur: 41</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُسَبِّحُ لَهُۥ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱلطَّيْرُ صَٓفَّتٍۢ ۖ كُلٌّۭ قَدْ عَلِمَ صَلَاتَهُۥ وَتَسْبِيحَهُۥ ۗ وَٱللَّهُ عَلِيمٌۢ بِمَا يَفْعَلُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Do you not see that God is exalted by whoever is within the heavens and the earth and by the birds with wings spread in flight? Each one has known its prayer and its exalting, and God is Knowing of what they do.&quot; (An-Nur: 41)</p></blockquote>
<p>There is thus no contradiction in the Imam''s despising the world and yet growing lyrical over the beauties of untouched nature, including that unsurpassable marvel, the peacock. This comes in a sermon that also describes birds in general, along with ants, serpents, and elephants. The sermon ends with a stirring description of Paradise, setting the marvels of this world beside the delights of the next; in the light of the latter, all earthly things fade into insignificance. For once the eye of the heart turns toward Paradise, &quot;your soul would become averse to the marvels of this world, its passions, its pleasures and its embellished scenery.&quot;</p>
<p>It might seem contradictory to hold together such apparently opposed qualities as joy in certainty, discussed above, and the befriending of hardship. Yet the latter is in fact the fruit of the former. Every difficulty without exception is borne through the spiritual resources that faith makes available when it is joined to intellectual assimilation—or rather, in the measure that faith is intellectually assimilated and so becomes <em>marifa</em> (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معرفۃ</span>) or gnosis, holding a certainty so deep and so complete that &quot;even if the veil be lifted&quot; it cannot increase, according to the famous saying noted earlier. It is possible to believe, at one level of the mind, that there is no god but God. The Imam, however, draws attention to what this apparently simple belief should imply when it is sincere or pure: &quot;I bear witness that there is no god but God alone Who has no partner, a testimony whose sincerity has been tested and whose essence has been assimilated with conviction (<em>mutaqadan musaffaha</em>).&quot;</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُصَفَّى</span> names the choicest and purest part of any substance, its very essence. The root <em>massa</em> means &quot;to suck,&quot; the idea being that the liquid or juice of a substance or fruit holds its essence, the part drawn out from it. Here the Imam is stressing the spiritual substance of the testimony, as distinct from mere spoken affirmation or mental grasp of the words. He affirms a heartfelt bond with the reality to which the testimony bears witness; the words that define it are an echo of the reality they carry, and are not to be confused with that reality itself. He continues: &quot;We cling to it always, for as long as we endure, storing it up as protection against the terrors that will befall us. For truly, it renders faith resolute, opens up spiritual virtue, pleases <em>al-Rahman</em> (the All-Compassionate), repels <em>al-shaytan</em> (the devil).&quot;</p>
<p>Returning to &quot;befriending that by which the ignorant are estranged,&quot; this points not only to the trials of life—a source of strength for the wise and of repulsion for the ignorant—but also to the inescapability of death. For the wise, death means release, the meeting with God, blissful eternity—in short, a mode of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, whereby the soul enjoys ever deeper degrees of integration within higher principles. For the ignorant, by contrast, death appears as a terrifying disintegration, the end of their world rather than the beginning of endless life. There is also the prefiguration of death in life already noted above: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>, &quot;extinction,&quot; &quot;effacement,&quot; the &quot;perishing&quot; of egoistic consciousness before the Absolute. The ignorant recoil in horror at the prospect of losing what they falsely take to be their life, not seeing that this sacrifice of egoistic and limited self-consciousness is the very ground on which infinite consciousness is glimpsed or tasted. The ocean of the Real can be tasted only by being drowned in it.</p>
<p>The means to this ultimate attainment is likewise embraced by the wise and feared by the ignorant: the act of pure prayer, total concentration on the Real. Such concentration shuts out every worldly or egotistic preoccupation; the sheer intensity of focus upon the Absolute burns away thoughts of the relative, and for those who ignorantly identify with relativities, inward and outward alike, this amounts to a kind of death. For the wise, by contrast, this concentration on the Absolute, the unsullied remembrance of God, is a joyous contemplation of reality before which the ego is displaced and all illusions dissolve. This leads straight to the next sentence of the passage: &quot;With their bodies, they keep company with the world, while their spirits are tied to the transcendent realm.&quot;</p>
<p>This sentence grows more concrete when read alongside another well-known expression of the Imam. He describes himself as belonging to a group whose spiritual degree is such that &quot;their hearts are in the Gardens of Paradise while their bodies are at work.&quot; To be tied or attached to that realm—which takes in the paradisal abodes and reaches up to the divine Presence itself—means that they already dwell within that transcendent realm more fully than within this earthly one. This helps explain the saying quoted earlier: God keeps man perpetually in that which his soul desires. Those whose hearts or spirits are in the transcendent domain are those whose consciousness is shaped, substantially and not merely conceptually, by transcendent realities. Those whose consciousness, by contrast, is shaped by worldly concerns are in this world in the sense of being enclosed by its formal limits; their orientation and imagination are trapped within a worldly frame. Their actions bear the mark of this worldliness, whereas the actions of the wise are the fruits of their immersion in a higher reality. Their outward actions in the world belie their inward transcendence of all action; they act, yet are not bound by the fruits of their action. As noted earlier, they know that action, all action, is dust, except what is purified, done sincerely, within it.</p>
<p>At this point the notion of the Hereafter deserves consideration within the Imam''s ethical framework. The Hereafter is invoked again and again in the Imam''s discourses. Is this simply a matter of holding out a heavenly reward and threatening a hellish punishment to enjoin the good and forbid the evil? Even if the answer is partly yes, there is a deeper dimension to this apparently straightforward use of divine warnings and promises as levers for moral action.</p>
<p>The ethical orientation here is defined not merely by belief in God but by the spiritual reality of the Hereafter as well, both objectively, in itself, and subjectively, insofar as the individual assimilates it. It thereby becomes a living dimension, a process, an orientation, a reflux, within the believer''s own soul, rather than a bare belief or hope. Anticipation of the Hereafter marks every believer in principle; its realization marks the soul of the <em>wali Allah</em>, the friend of God, the saint and the sage. As noted earlier, the Imam speaks in a famous sentence that bears witness to his total immersion in the reality of which he is certain: &quot;Were the veil to be removed, I would not increase in certitude (<em>law kushifal-ghita, ma azdadtu yaqinan</em>).&quot; The anticipation—or better, the foretaste—of the Hereafter deepens rather than fades in proportion to one''s virtue, piety, and sanctity; it does not dwindle into a mere heuristic device whose value is spent once its moral impact is achieved. The following moving passage from the <em>Nahj</em> deserves reflection in this light. What appears here is the description of a blissful vision of the reality of Paradise, not merely a rhetorical flourish in the service of moral exhortation:</p>
<p>If you were to cast the eye of your heart toward what is described for you of the Garden, your soul would become averse to the marvels of this world, its passions, its pleasures and its embellished scenery. And your soul would be rapt in contemplation of the swaying trees whose roots lie hidden in dunes of musk on the banks of the rivers of the Garden, and the clusters of dazzling pearls hanging down from the branches of its trees …</p>
<p>The highest degree of spirituality does, of course, transcend the desire for Paradise and the fear of Hell, along with the moral conduct proportioned to them. But this means that when such higher degrees of realization are attained, the soul''s state can properly be called paradisal—that is, already so utterly content in the beatific presence of God that it desires nothing more. This state of soul is called in the Quran <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلنَّفْسُ الْمُطْمَئِنَّةُ</span>, &quot;the soul at peace in absolute certainty&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Fajr: 27</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَٓأَيَّتُهَا ٱلنَّفْسُ ٱلْمُطْمَئِنَّةُ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;O reassured soul.&quot; (Al-Fajr: 27)</p></blockquote>
<p>The Imam frequently echoes, implicitly if not explicitly, the Quranic principle that &quot;the Hereafter is better and more lasting&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-A''la: 17</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلْآخِرَةُ خَيْرٌۭ وَأَبْقَىٰٓ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;But the Hereafter is better and more enduring.&quot; (Al-Ala: 17)</p></blockquote>
<p>The Hereafter is not only the time at which divine warnings and promises reach their fulfillment; it is also the space in which all positive qualities—human, angelic, divine—are manifested in their fullness, those qualities that appear on earth as reflections of higher realities and that encompass and subtly penetrate the whole sphere of earthly existence. The Quran, perhaps more than any other scripture, furnishes the imagination with numerous and complex descriptions of paradisal bliss. One of the key functions of these descriptions is alluded to in the Quran itself: &quot;Assuredly, We purify them with a pure quality, remembrance of the Abode of the Hereafter&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Sad: 46</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّآ أَخْلَصْنَهُم بِخَالِصَةٍۢ ذِكْرَى ٱلدَّارِ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Indeed, We chose them for an exclusive quality: remembrance of the Home of the Hereafter.&quot; (Sad: 46)</p></blockquote>
<p>The mere thought of Paradise is itself a purification of mind and heart, a way of guarding the soul against the ever-present temptation to seek its ultimate happiness and well-being in this world alone. Conversely, one''s well-being here below, where action is concerned, lies in the doing of good deeds, and such well-being is proportioned to the depth of one''s belief that &quot;Whatever good ye send before for your souls, ye will find it with God, better and more tremendous as reward&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Muzzammil: 20</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ رَبَّكَ يَعْلَمُ أَنَّكَ تَقُومُ أَدْنَىٰ مِن ثُلُثَىِ ٱلَّيْلِ وَنِصْفَهُۥ وَثُلُثَهُۥ وَطَآئِفَةٌۭ مِّنَ ٱلَّذِينَ مَعَكَ ۚ وَٱللَّهُ يُقَدِّرُ ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ ۚ عَلِمَ أَن لَّن تُحْصُوهُ فَتَابَ عَلَيْكُمْ ۖ فَٱقْرَءُوا۟ مَا تَيَسَّرَ مِنَ ٱلْقُرْآنِ ۚ عَلِمَ أَن سَيَكُونُ مِنكُم مَّرْضَىٰ ۙ وَآخَرُونَ يَضْرِبُونَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ يَبْتَغُونَ مِن فَضْلِ ٱللَّهِ ۙ وَآخَرُونَ يُقَتِلُونَ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ ۖ فَٱقْرَءُوا۟ مَا تَيَسَّرَ مِنْهُ ۚ وَأَقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَآتُوا۟ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ وَأَقْرِضُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ قَرْضًا حَسَنًۭا ۚ وَمَا تُقَدِّمُوا۟ لِأَنفُسِكُم مِّنْ خَيْرٍۢ تَجِدُوهُ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ هُوَ خَيْرًۭا وَأَعْظَمَ أَجْرًۭا ۚ وَٱسْتَغْفِرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ ۖ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۢ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;And whatever good you put forward for yourselves, you will find it with God. It is better and greater in reward.&quot; (Al-Muzzammil: 20)</p></blockquote>
<p>In other words, a sincere orientation toward the Hereafter is transformed into a perception of heavenly beatitude already here below, in the form of those very acts and attitudes which, being good, share the same nature as the felicity to which they lead. In this light, morally good actions and noble intentions are grasped by one who sees beyond the veil of this world as vivid prefigurations of heavenly realities, not merely as ethical prerequisites for salvation. Goodness, in other words, does not simply lead to a heavenly reward; it already participates in that reward: &quot;Is the reward of goodness (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>) aught but goodness?&quot;:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ar-Rahman: 60</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ جَزَآءُ ٱلْإِحْسَنِ إِلَّا ٱلْإِحْسَنُ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Is the reward for good anything but good?&quot; (Ar-Rahman: 60)</p></blockquote>
<p>The Imam''s saying, &quot;The fruit of good action is like its root,&quot; can be read as an echo of the idea that the end is already present in the means that lead to it. Fitting to close is the following verse of the Quran, which also serves as an introduction to what follows:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 25</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَبَشِّرِ ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّلِحَتِ أَنَّ لَهُمْ جَنَّتٍۢ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَرُ ۖ كُلَّمَا رُزِقُوا۟ مِنْهَا مِن ثَمَرَةٍۢ رِّزْقًۭا ۙ قَالُوا۟ هَذَا ٱلَّذِى رُزِقْنَا مِن قَبْلُ ۖ وَأُتُوا۟ بِهِۦ مُتَشَبِهًۭا ۖ وَلَهُمْ فِيهَآ أَزْوَجٌۭ مُّطَهَّرَةٌۭ ۖ وَهُمْ فِيهَا خَلِدُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;And give good tidings to those who believe and perform virtuous deeds, that for them are Gardens underneath which rivers flow. Every time they are given to eat from the fruits thereof, they say: This is what we were given to eat before. And they were given the like thereof.&quot; (Al-Baqarah: 25)</p></blockquote>
<p>The fruits of the paradisal gardens are thus tasted already here below, in the form of all goodness, beauty, and truth, and the diverse modes of happiness that flow from them. All such experiences are so many foretastes of the ultimate beatitude in Paradise. On the ethical plane, the doing of good deeds, and, even more directly, the realization of intrinsic virtue, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>, is therefore not simply a prerequisite for posthumous salvation, but is already a kind of deliverance, here and now: a deliverance from the imprisonment of sin on the outward plane, and from the bondage of egocentricity on the inward plane. This theme of salvation here and now, grounded in unshakeable certitude, is fundamental to the spirit of the Imam''s teachings and will be explored further throughout the rest of this book. In the following chapter, the cardinal virtue of justice will be considered within Ali s perspective, rooted as it is in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>, a virtue defined by the Prophet as worshipping God as if one saw Him, and concretely affirmed by the Imam as real and not merely hypothetical, for God is indeed seen by the pure of heart through the verities of faith.</p>
<blockquote class="quran aside editorial"><p class="tr"><strong>Editorial note (tradition-grounded).</strong> A related teaching preserved in this tradition names <em>ihsan</em> as the highest form of a single root, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ح</span>-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">س</span>-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ن</span>, in which goodness and beauty are one word — so that to do well is to make something beautiful. What that tradition adds is a definition this chapter''s own argument reaches toward: <em>ihsan</em> is to worship and act as though seeing God, giving every deed its full inward beauty. This joins the two threads the chapter holds side by side — the intimate certainty that touches its divine object, and the virtue that &quot;makes the soul beautiful.&quot; The one who acts as though seeing God is at once the person of certainty and the person of <em>ihsan</em>; the beauty of outward and inward that Ali praises is simply what such worship looks like from without and within.</p></blockquote>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'the fruits of certainty';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>A Sacred Conception of Justice</p>
<p>The reflections on justice that follow are not an exercise in theoretical speculation about what justice means in Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>)s letter to Malik al-Ashtar, written fourteen centuries ago. Nor are they the kind of definition found in a dictionary, where justice amounts to little more than fairness, equality, and fair play. The aim is, first of all, to bring the discussion of justice into alignment with the concept and the reality of the sacred — the reality of the sacred One, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القدس</span>. On one side, the principle of justice carries a sacred and spiritual dimension that contemporary discussion so often overlooks. On the other, a careful appreciation of what the sacred means opens a deeper understanding of the roots of justice within the divine nature itself, and a fuller awareness of why justice is necessary, both in the world and in the soul.</p>
<p>The central argument is this. In Ali s worldview, the ethical orientation toward justice is immeasurably deepened once it is consciously tied to the spiritual precepts of the Islamic faith. The spirit that underlies moral rules and ethical values is strengthened by a commitment to transcendent principles — even though, or precisely because, those principles rise above the realm of action in which the rules themselves operate. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span> may be pictured as having a body and a spirit, much as a person does. Its body is made up of actions: prayer, fasting, the paying of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زکوۃ</span>. But the spirit that animates those actions rests on the nature of the divine, which is justice. To adhere to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span> is therefore to train in becoming like the divine through the principles of justice, and so action must be joined by contemplation of the divine. Here contemplation and action complete each other rather than conflict, for their union lies at the very heart of the Islamic message of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">توحید</span> — an integrating oneness that makes theory and practice one, so that a person becomes a true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">موحد</span>. This unity shines with dazzling clarity in the life of the Imam, as it does in the life of the Prophet. The harmony between the two principles — contemplation and action, being and doing, theory and practice, ideal and reality — runs throughout the remarkable document that is the subject of these reflections.</p>
<p>These reflections stand against the background of contemporary ethical discourse and may be seen as a modest contribution, from an Islamic perspective, to the scholarly debates on virtue and ethics. In particular, they should be understood in light of one of the most serious and far-reaching critiques of modern and postmodern conceptions of virtue in general, and of justice in particular — that of Alasdair MacIntyre. Put simply, his critique holds that for several centuries ethical discourse in Western society has drifted far from its moorings in absolute values. He argues, compellingly, that debates about morality and ethics, together with their philosophical foundations, are no longer grounded in any conception of the good life or of the Good as such. As a result, MacIntyre holds, various shades of relativism, nihilism, utilitarianism, and opportunism have emerged in practice as the shifting bases on which ethical values are formed, if they are formed at all. Relativism holds that knowledge, truth, and morality are not absolute but relative, existing only in relation to a culture, a society, or a historical moment. Nihilism rejects all religious and moral principles and takes life to be essentially meaningless. Utilitarianism counts an action right only if it is useful or benefits a majority, so that good and bad rest on nothing more than a calculation of greater benefit or loss. And opportunism seizes whatever advantage circumstances offer, heedless of any plan or principle — the way of the manipulator, who flatters, performs, or deceives to get what he wants.</p>
<p>Whatever value these human-made theories occasionally yield, none of it is rooted in the divine; and so, even where they happen to mirror the values of faith, they remain baseless, without spirit — dead corpses. Virtue practiced on an ad hoc basis is not virtue properly speaking. It is a series of pragmatic adjustments to changing circumstances, made in the absence of any genuine principle of virtue. Truthfulness illustrates this. The underlying reason for always speaking the truth is twofold: that truth is the very nature of our Creator — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحق</span>, the absolute truth — and that our purpose in this world is to take on the virtues of our Creator. If one resolves to speak the truth without grasping that reason, truthfulness will in the end become situational and self-serving, something to be compromised whenever circumstances seem to warrant it, spoken when it profits and abandoned when it costs. But once truth is seen as the nature of the divine, it becomes everlasting and eternal, indifferent to consequence, because one speaks it in emulation of the immortal nature of God. The virtue itself becomes immortal, and the need to practice it on an ad hoc basis falls away. We are looking, writes MacIntyre, for a conception of the good which will enable us to order other goods, for a conception of the good which will enable us to extend our understanding of the purpose and content of the virtues, for a conception of the good which will enable us to understand the place of integrity and consistency in life ...</p>
<p>It is at this level, in particular, that the insights of Ali and other sages of the Islamic tradition are especially relevant. MacIntyre''s proposed solution to the crisis — a reformulation of an Aristotelian conception of ethics grounded in a Thomistic worldview — shares a great deal with the Islamic tradition of ethical discourse (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>). But one can argue further: a careful engagement with the Imam''s legacy yields far more than a merely theoretical approach to the question of justice, and this in two respects. First, the Imam was himself a paragon of justice, and thus a living embodiment of this and other fundamental virtues. In his life one sees a perfect, indeed unflinching, adherence to this cardinal virtue — a life governed not by his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> but entirely by the divine nature of God. The remarkable deeds recorded of him, at which one marvels, are the fruit of his irresistible attraction toward the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحق</span>, for his whole existence was oriented toward God. His attention rested never on the action itself but on the source of the principles that generated it; fixing upon the beauty of the divine nature and striving to take it on, he found good actions arising from him naturally and inevitably. Although this work does not enter into many details of his life and its lessons, certain incidents and events that help illustrate the point will be recalled. Secondly — and more important for the present purpose — the discourses, letters, and sayings attributed to Ali offer a rich source of reflection on the dynamic spirituality that lies at the root of a life of virtue. In them one is granted a glimpse of a way of life wholly governed by the sacred, one in which the desire for the good life emerges as the fruit of an irresistible attraction toward the Good as such, which is identical to the absolutely Real, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>.</p>
<p>In other words, one is ethically inspired by the Good in direct proportion to one''s existential orientation toward the Real. If moral rules rest on ethical perspectives, and those perspectives rest in turn on a conception of the good life, then that conception of the Good is itself drawn from the moral agent''s fundamental understanding of what life means. Amyn B. Sajoo, in a recent thoughtful work on Muslim ethics, draws a careful distinction between ethics and morals. Ethical discourse, he argues, is concerned more with conceptions of the good, while morals pertain more specifically to rules about right and wrong. Ethics deals with the soul and the spirit of the good, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>, and out of ethics morals are born. Certainly, he continues, the concepts overlap substantially in theory and practice; but it is well to bear in mind the more encompassing sense of reality that typifies ethics. To set ethics within the wider framework of spiritual virtue — in the sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> — widens the compass of both ethics and morality still further, lending each a dimension of existential depth and grounding both in a more organic and all-encompassing whole. For the spiritual life has three components: Islam, which supplies the outer aspects of morality; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ایمان</span>, which is the framework of ethics; and the union of the two, serving as their underlying principle, which issues in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> — the good life, the beautiful life. Abstract questions of right and wrong thus come to rest on the more definitive principle that emerges in answer to the question: what is reality?</p>
<p>The previous chapter''s discussion of the deeper meanings of divine unity bears on this. In the Imam''s spiritual vision of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, one perceives, according to one''s capacity, an ultimate reality that not only has no second but also has no moral opposite. People tend to picture the world as good against evil, God against <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شیطان</span>, forever thinking in opposites; yet the Imam teaches that God has no opposite at all. Satan does not stand against God, for nothing has the power to stand against God. He stands against God''s creation, and his whole effort is to drive people away from their inherent divine nature by making its opposite beautiful — beautifying arrogance where God''s nature is modest, lewdness where it is decent, boastfulness where it is humble. For the goodness proper to God is not the moral opposite of evil. It occupies an altogether different plane, that of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحق</span>, the absolutely Real, whose only opposite is unreality, or nothingness. Goodness is at one with the pure positivity of the Absolute, while evil arises solely from a negative capacity to negate the Real, the Good. The essence of all goodness and beauty, in other words, is identified with the Creator, whereas evil is a mode of the created — the corrupted state of a creation that has deviated from the divine nature. This is strongly implied in the Quranic verse of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَۃُ الْفَلَقِ</span>, which calls the believer to seek refuge in God from the evil of that which He created (Al-Falaq: 2):</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Falaq: 1-2</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ ۱ مِنْ شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ ۲</span></p><p class="tr">Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak, from the evil of what He has created.</p></blockquote>
<p>This verse is often mistranslated as from the evil which He created, which is wrong both theologically and lexically. The expression min sharri ma khalaqa, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ</span>, plainly refers to the evil that derives from creation, not to creation as such. In theological terms, God creates morally free agents, and it is they who are the authors of evil in creation; when creation, by its own free will, chooses to negate the divine nature of its Creator, evil is born. God may be said to create all actions only in the metaphysical sense that He is the source of all being, and so of all modes within being, of which action is one; He cannot be said to have created evil as such, but only the conditions within which evil arises. Evil is thus the negation of the Good, which is at one with the Real, and as such it has no ultimate ontological principle. It is only a temporary state of deviation from that pure and divine nature — an illusion witnessed by the eye of the heart.</p>
<p>The verse, then, may be understood as a call to seek refuge in the Real from the unreal, since between goodness and evil there is no common measure in being. Goodness can be opposed only by the appearance of evil — an appearance the eye of the heart grasps as illusion, that eye which truly sees'' God through the verities of faith. This vision of the merely relative reality of evil, far from breeding indifference toward it, greatly strengthens the spiritual resolve to overcome it, for the moral effort to be good intensifies in proportion to one''s conviction of accountability to that goodness. That conviction is in turn carried inward, from the mind to the heart, by the certainty that goodness is more real than evil — a certainty itself nourished by the spiritual vision of the good as the Real. To perceive the radical incommensurability between good and evil, therefore, leads not to indifference in the face of evil but to a more existentially compelling orientation toward the good. In the Imam''s perspective, the notions of right and rightful due gathered within the meaning of haqq, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حق</span>, are charged with an aura of absoluteness. To understand what is right leads to the commitment to fulfill one''s obligations; what is rightfully due is the restoration of reality through the removal of illusory evil; and both are equally infused with reality, which is justice. The moral notions of rectitude and propriety are expressions of a principle that transcends morality, a principle belonging to ultimate reality. One''s commitment to fulfilling one''s obligations — to giving every being its rightful due — becomes charged, likewise, with a powerful sense of reality, and the resolve to be just is made more absolute by the very absoluteness proper to the Real.</p>
<p>This duty to give each and every being its due connects to the most explicit definition of justice the Imam gives: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَضَعَ الشَّئُی حَقَّ مَحَلِّہِ</span>, Justice puts everything in its right place. One is just in so far as one gives everything its proper due, rendering the haqq owed to each person — indeed to each and every thing in existence; and the capacity to be just in this demanding, universal sense grows in proportion to one''s attunement to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>, the Real. As the previous chapter makes clear, this spiritual conception of virtue not only takes in one''s duty to all but engages all that one is. One must be good before one can do good, and the only way to be is to be at one with the Real — that which alone truly is''.</p>
<p>The Imam''s remark on justice calls to mind the more famous definition of Plato: ...we have laid down, as a universal principle, that everyone ought to perform the one function in the community for which his nature best suited him... that principle, or some form of it, is justice. There are indeed many remarkable parallels between Plato''s <em>Republic</em> and the Imam''s letter to Malik al-Ashtar. Some of the more obvious correspondences deserve attention, along with the way the Imam''s explicit spirituality illuminates what stays implicit in Plato''s philosophy: namely, how one may actually attain the vision of the Real, which Plato calls gazing upon the Good. It is this contemplation of the Good as such that serves as the source of one''s capacity to act ethically at every level, for it lets one take the Good as a pattern for the right ordering of the state and of the individual.</p>
<p>Platonic and Neoplatonic ideas of justice have left a well-documented mark on the way ethics came to be discussed in Islam. In that discussion, justice is not one virtue standing beside others; it is the perfection, the sum, of all the rest — above all of the three chief Platonic virtues: temperance, courage, and wisdom. These belong, in turn, to the appetitive, the spirited, and the rational parts of the soul. When every part of the soul is filled out by its proper virtue and stands in its right place, the soul may be called &quot;just.&quot;</p>
<p>What has not received enough attention is how closely Plato''s chief means of reaching the vision of the Good — anamnesis — corresponds, at the spiritual level, to the central place of dhikr in Islam. Both words can be rendered in English as &quot;remembrance.&quot; And how is this justice achieved? What releases its power is precisely this remembrance: the recollection of ultimate reality and of unchanging principles as they apply at their several levels. It is not a matter of learning something new, but of calling back to mind principles already present in the very substance of the intellect — principles by which all things can be understood, and which are rooted, finally, in the Sovereign Good itself. Such remembrance is the ground on which ethical thought rests, both as knowledge and as reasoning. That its importance has been largely overlooked is a symptom of the modern habit of cutting ethics off from spirituality, treating each as though it were sealed away from the other. Ethics is still taken seriously, as a branch of philosophy and a discipline in its own right; spirituality, by contrast, is too often waved aside as beside the point in serious scholarship and consigned to the realm of the imaginary, the vague, and the mythological. This pushing of spirituality to the margins of supposedly serious discourse is closely bound up with the shrunken view of knowledge that has increasingly marked Western thought since the Renaissance — a narrowing that goes hand in hand with the steady draining away of the sacred dimension of life.</p>
<p>In the Islamic tradition, ethics and spirituality are closely bound together. This shows nowhere more clearly than in the Sufi tradition, which is now often, and rightly, called the ihsani tradition. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> is taken here in the sense the Prophet gave it in a saying of central importance about the three foundations of the Islamic way. That saying is known as &quot;the hadith of Gabriel,&quot; because the man who put the questions — though the Companions present did not know it — was the angel Gabriel (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جبرئیل</span>) in human form. He came up to the Prophet as he sat among his Companions and said:</p>
<p>O Muhammad, tell me about submission (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>), <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا</span> Muhammad<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">، أَخْبِرْنِي عَنِ الْإِسْلَامِ</span>.</p>
<p>The Prophet replied: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلاَمُ أَنْ تَشْهَدَ أَنْ لاَ إِلهَ إِلاَ اللهُ وَأَنَّ</span> Muhammad<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اً رَسُولُ اللهِ، وَتُقِيمَ الصَّلاَةَ، وَتُؤْتِيَ</span> al-zakat<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">، وَتَصُومَ رَمَضَانَ، وَتَحُجَّ الْبَيْتَ إِنِ اسْتَطَعْتَ إِلَيْهِ سَبِيلاً</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is to testify that there is no god but God and Muhammad is the messenger of God, to perform the prayers, to pay the poor-due (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الزَّكَاةَ</span>), to fast in Ramadan, and to make the pilgrimage to the House if you are able to do so.</p>
<p>You have spoken truly (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقْتَ</span>), Gabriel said. The Companions, who saw only a man and did not know he was an angel, were astonished that he should question the Prophet and then declare that he had spoken truly.</p>
<p>Then tell me about faith (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِيْمَانِ</span>), he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَخْبِرْنِي عَنِ الإِيْمَانِ</span>.</p>
<p>The Prophet said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْ تُؤْمِنَ بِاللهِ، وَمَلاَئِكَتِهِ، وَكُتُبِهِ، وَرُسُلِهِ، وَالْيَوْمِ الآخِرِ، وَتُؤْمِنَ بِالْقَدَرِ خَيْرِهِ وَشَرِّهِ</span>, It is to believe in God, His angels, His books, His messengers, and the Last Day, and to believe in divine destiny, both the good and the evil thereof.</p>
<p>You have spoken truly (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدَقْتَ</span>), he said. Then tell me about virtue (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِحْسَانِ</span>), he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَخْبِرْنِي عَنِ الإِحْسَانِ</span>.</p>
<p>He said: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْ تَعْبُدَ اللهَ كَأَنَّكَ تَرَاهُ، فَإِنْ لَمْ تَكُنْ تَرَاهُ فَإِنَّهُ يَرَاكَ</span>, It is to worship God as though you see Him, and if you see Him not, yet truly He sees you.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> can also be translated literally as &quot;doing what is beautiful,&quot; since its root (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَ</span>) is bound up first of all with beauty, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَ</span>. In Islamic ethical discourse, virtue and vice are often called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَ</span> and qubh (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُبْح</span>) — beauty and ugliness. In the end it is the vision of divine beauty that awakens virtue, which is beauty of soul, just as turning away from the divine qualities is what leaves the soul ugly (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُبْح</span>). It is no surprise, then, that — broadly speaking — while the jurists, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُقَهَاءُ</span>, have been the specialists in outward action, and the theologians the specialists in formal belief, it fell to the mystics to be the guardians of spiritual virtue, of &quot;making beautiful.&quot; And it is no accident that the great majority of artists in the Islamic world — calligraphers, painters, poets, musicians, and the rest — have been practicing Sufis.</p>
<p>The Prophet''s definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> as &quot;worshipping God as if one could see Him&quot; makes plain the crucial bond between worship and virtue — between spirituality and ethics, between devotion to the Creator and goodness toward His creatures. One cannot be had without the other. This bond lies at the very heart of the spiritual tradition in Islam. And it is the denial and betrayal of that tradition by some modern Muslims that, more than any other single cause, lies behind the atrocities now being committed by Muslims in the name of Islam.</p>
<p>Both the theory and the practice of ethics have always been part of the very fabric of Sufism. Even some who wrote in more philosophical and less openly spiritual terms — al-Farabi among them — were known to be practicing Sufis as well. The figure in whom spirituality and ethics meet most powerfully is, of course, al-Ghazali, whose great Revival of the Sciences of Religion stands as a monument to what Islam''s deepest spirit does to the ethical temper of the believing soul. He has rightly been called the greatest thinker on ethics in Islam; and since his thought is shaped through and through by Sufism, this by itself testifies to how closely Sufism and ethics are joined. It would be no exaggeration to say that most treatises written by Sufis are in fact treatises on &quot;ethics&quot; — provided ethics is understood not only as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span> in the narrow sense, but also as the fruit of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>, of spiritual virtue. The various spiritual stations (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامَات</span>) the Sufis describe often bear the names of particular virtues: patience (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَبْرٌ</span>), gratitude (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُكْرٌ</span>), contentment (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِضَاءٌ</span>), generosity (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَم</span>), and so on.</p>
<p>The thread running through all these stations is a transforming awareness of God — the principle of being and acting &quot;as if you could see God,&quot; of carrying and composing oneself in the constant awareness of standing in the divine presence, for even if one cannot see Him, &quot;He sees you.&quot; The call of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> thus lies at the very root of a life of virtue in the spiritual world of Islam. In his sermons in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَھْجُ الْبَلَاغَۃِ</span>, Ali turns his hearers again and again to the &quot;vision&quot; of God, in whatever measure, as the true ground of a virtuous life, and warns again and again against its opposite — the all-absorbing vision of oneself — as the source of every vice. On the one hand the Imam says, &quot;Know that your every action is dependent upon your prayer,&quot; that is, upon one''s way of &quot;gazing upon the Good,&quot; to put it in Platonic terms. On the other, he warns against vanity, conceit, and the lavish praise of oneself by others, for these are Satan''s (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَیْطَان</span>) surest openings for effacing the virtue, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>, of the virtuous (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُحسنین</span>). Set against each other here are two visions. The true, objective vision of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>, the Real, is reached by effacing oneself in prayer, for in the prostration (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَۃ</span>) of prayer one effaces one''s own ego, one''s nafs (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>). Opposed to it stands the distorted, self-centered vision of the ego, swollen by pride — which is exactly what Iblis (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِبْلِیس</span>) desires. The first leads to justice, putting all things in their proper place, and, most fundamentally, the ego itself; the second leads to every injustice that flows from self-aggrandizement, fanaticism and bigotry among them. For Satan, the very embodiment of pride and disobedience, is described as &quot;the leader of the fanatics and the forerunner of the proud&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِمَامُ الْمُتَعَصِّبِینَ وَ سَلَفُ الْمُسْتَکْبِرِینَ</span>, imam al-mutaassibin wa salaf al-mustakbirin). Bigotry is intolerance toward those who hold different opinions; and fanatical bigotry is an especially fierce form of injustice, for it carries that poisonous prejudice which bends the objective demands of justice to the supposed privileges of one''s own self — whether that self is defined as the individual, the family, the clan, the tribe, the nation, or the religion.</p>
<p>The Imam''s letter appointing Malik al-Ashtar as his governor of Egypt appears as letter 52 in most editions of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَھْجُ الْبَلَاغَۃِ</span>. Across the centuries it has served as a source of inspiration, read as an ideal constitution for Islamic government. In its fairly detailed account of the duties and rights of the ruler, of the various officers of the state, and of the main classes of society, it fills out the more general framework of principles set down in the famous &quot;Constitution of Medina&quot; that the Prophet dictated. Yet the counsel in the letter goes far beyond the bounds usually set by political or legal texts on government. Though it is addressed in the first place to the ruler or governor, much of its counsel in fact concerns ethical principles that hold everywhere, and so it speaks as much to the governed as to those who govern. The letter is as much a statement of ethics as of politics — not a worked-out &quot;system&quot; or theory of ethics, to be sure, but a moving expression of the spiritual ethos the Imam embodied and radiated. That ethos flows straight from the sources of the Islamic revelation, and so it lays bare the roots of virtue and ethics within a world governed by the all-embracing principle of revelation. The document can therefore be read as a commentary on that revelation as well — one that, in turn, draws its readers to look more deeply into the meanings held within the revealed texts. Malik al-Ashtar was a long-standing and devoted follower of the Imam, spoken of as one of his &quot;right-hand&quot; men. Muawiya, the Imam''s chief opponent in the civil war that dominated his caliphate, was among those who used the phrase — notably on hearing that his plot to have Malik poisoned had succeeded before Malik could take up his post as governor of Egypt. Tabari reports Muawiya as saying: &quot;Ali ibn Abi Talib had two right hands. One of them was cut off at Siffin, and the other today.&quot; The Imam, for his part, met the news of Malik''s assassination with these words: &quot;Malik, and what was Malik! By God, were he a mountain, he would be the solitary one soaring above all others, and were he a rock, he would have been impenetrably solid. No horse could ascend such a mountain, no bird could fly up to it.&quot;</p>
<p>The Imam''s definition of justice stands at the center of what follows: &quot;Justice puts everything in its right place.&quot; In everyday speech, justice means little more than fairness, equality, and fair play — a workable account, but a merely formal one. The idea at work here is of an altogether different order. It joins justice to the sacred, to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القدس</span>, the All-Holy, and finds the very roots of justice within the divine nature itself. What ties this conception of justice to the sacred is the demand that one first put one''s relationship with God in its right place; everything else follows from that spiritual demand. The same order of priority is set out plainly in an important line of the Imam''s letter to Malik: &quot;Be just with God and be just with people [giving them what is their due] from yourself.&quot;</p>
<p>Being just with God means, among other things, conforming as far as one can to His own nature — which is not merely the source of justice but is justice. The weight falls on that word is. Justice is not one attribute among His many, ranged beside His mercy, His wrath, and His love; it is the very essence of His nature, and all His other qualities flow from it. His mercy is an expression of His justice, and so are His anger, His love, His creating, and His destroying. &quot;I bear witness,&quot; says the Imam in one of his sermons, &quot;that He is Justice (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَدْل</span>) and He acts justly.&quot;</p>
<p>It might be objected that there is a deep gulf between justice as a divine reality and justice as a human act. In God, justice means that everything is in its right place; in man, justice means the effort to put everything in its right place — an unchanging metaphysical quality on the divine side, a living act of will on the human side. To this the reply is that man''s moral effort to act justly, in a world where things are not in their right place, springs from an inborn spiritual leaning toward justice. Just action thus expresses the principle of justice, which, in man as in God, is that unchanging principle by which everything stands in its right place. As divine action in creation, judgment, and elsewhere expresses absolute justice, so in man just action expresses not only a moral, willed, and intellectual effort to bring justice about, but also a spiritual kinship with the ultimate nature of reality — with things as they truly are in God, and therefore as they ought to be here on earth.</p>
<p>Applied to state and society, one of the plainest consequences of this conception of justice is that rulers must be pious and upright. As the Imam says in another sermon: &quot;The most momentous of the reciprocal rights that God has made obligatory is the right of the ruler over the subjects, and the right of the subjects over the ruler, a reciprocal obligation which God has ordained for them.&quot; He then makes a point wholly indispensable to any account of good governance in Islam: &quot;The subjects will not be righteous except through the righteousness of the rulers, and the rulers will not be righteous except through the uprightness of the subjects. So if the subjects render to the ruler his rightful due and he renders them theirs, then will right be honored among them, the ways of religion will be upheld, and the banners of justice will be erected.&quot; This insistence on the ruler''s piety is, as one would expect, central to the Imam''s message to his governor; but it involves far more than a simple call to keep the formal rules of Islamic law.</p>
<p>The whole of the Imam''s life can be seen as a quest to conform as perfectly as he could to the divine nature, and to this quality of justice above all. The principle has far too many sides — social, economic, political, legal — as they appear in his life and teachings to be treated in a short space. One defining moment in his caliphate shows the rigor with which he held to justice whatever it cost. The revolt of Muawiya, and the civil war into which the young Muslim empire was thereby plunged, can be said to have begun not at Siffin but in Medina. When Ali took up the caliphate, his cousin Ibn al-Abbas advised him to confirm all of Uthman''s governors in their posts for the time being, and then, once the new order was secure, to remove them. The Imam''s reply to this proposal was reported thus: &quot;I do not doubt that this would be best for the sake of reconciliation in this world. But there is my obligation to the Truth (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَّذِي يَلْزَمُنِي مِنَ الْحَقِّ</span>) and my knowledge of Uthman''s governors, so, by God, I shall never appoint one of them.&quot;</p>
<p>And so the die was cast: the Imam dismissed Muawiya along with all of his predecessor''s governors. The Imam saw clearly that Ibn al-Abbas'' proposal had every chance of working; he knew that all Muawiya wanted was worldly power, and that Muawiya would have been content to govern Syria had the Imam allowed it. Commentators have taken this episode — which made the governors'' revolt all but inevitable — as evidence of Ali s want of political shrewdness and his stubbornness. It should rather be understood as an expression of his disdain for worldly ease when that ease can be bought only at the price of essential principles. As he says in one of his sayings: &quot;People renounce no part of their religion for the sake of rectifying their world without God opening them up to something worse than it&quot; — that is, worse than the very defect they were trying to set right.</p>
<p>Clearly, to keep in power governors whose corruption was beyond both doubt and remedy would have amounted, for the Imam, to &quot;renouncing&quot; a part of his religion — for religion without justice is not religion. To support unjust governors, even as a tactical move, was a compromise of religious principle simply unthinkable to him. This attitude ran through countless episodes of his life, all of which show that the principles set down in the letter to Malik al-Ashtar were far from merely theoretical.</p>
<p>What follows examines one particular dimension of justice — political on the surface, ethical in its outward expression, but spiritual in its depth: the justice of the political ruler, understood against its most clearly definable opposite, which is injustice, tyranny, oppression, and corruption. A deep taking-in of the Imam''s spirituality can lend a real, existential power to the moral effort to live justly — a power that is missing when justice is conceived only in individual or horizontal terms, that is, as a secular moral philosophy cut off from religious or spiritual principles. The crucial place of devotion and worship, both formal and beyond-formal, belongs to this same inquiry. Contemplation of the divine Reality is the wellspring from which whole and undivided virtue flows freely and without end — a point brought into sharper relief by comparison with Plato''s &quot;gazing&quot; upon the Sovereign Good as the source of all virtue.</p>
<p>The inquiry that follows takes its bearings from a single document above all others: the letter of Ali to Malik al-Ashtar. It is there that a sacred conception of justice is set out most fully, and it is from its key passages that the present analysis draws.</p>
<p>Around that letter stand the Imam''s other writings. His further letters, his sermons, and his sayings are gathered here as they bear on the same theme, extending and deepening what the letter to Malik sets down. Treated in this way, justice is not read out of one text in isolation but heard across the whole body of his counsel.</p>
<p>Beyond the words of Ali himself, the analysis turns to certain traditions of the Prophet and to verses of the Quran. These are brought forward not as ornament but as light, for they illuminate the very themes the letter raises and lay bare the ground on which its conception of justice rests.</p>
<blockquote class="aside editorial"><p><strong>Editorial note (tradition-grounded).</strong> The chapter turns on <em>tawhid</em> — the &quot;integrating oneness&quot; that binds contemplation to action and makes one a <em>muwahhid</em>. A related teaching preserved in this tradition clarifies the word itself: it is built from a root meaning &quot;to be one,&quot; and as a form-II noun it names not the mere counting of God as a single item but the deliberate <em>act</em> of declaring Him one — a oneness that admits no partner, division, or second. That grammatical shape is worth holding onto here, because it mirrors exactly what the chapter argues: unity is not something passively observed but something a person actively enacts, joining theory to practice until being and doing become one. Read this way, becoming a <em>muwahhid</em> is itself the work of justice the chapter describes — the willed alignment of the soul with the undivided nature of the divine.</p></blockquote>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'a sacred conception of justice';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The letter is addressed to one of the Imam''s closest and most trusted companions. For that reason, its warnings about injustice and oppression carry a very precise force, over and above the universal theme they express. They apply to everyone who lives under Islam and its laws, and who begins with every right intention, yet remains vulnerable to the temptations of political power. They reckon fully with an unfortunate but proven truth, caught in the maxim that power corrupts — or, in the Imam''s own words, he who rules, appropriates (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ مَلَكَ اسْتَأْثَرَ</span>).</p>
<p>The Imam''s understanding of justice plainly reaches beyond both its own historical moment and the formal boundaries of the Islamic faith. The letter can be read as speaking not only to those who wish to govern by just principles, but to all who, though not corrupt at the outset, are corruptible — a class that takes in the overwhelming majority of humanity.</p>
<p>A note of somber realism sounds from the very opening of the letter. Man''s potential for evil, once he is left to his own devices and cut off from divine grace, dominates the mood of the preamble. Hence its emphasis on abiding by God''s wajibat, the religious obligations binding on every Muslim. Malik is instructed to set obedience to God above all else, to abide by what He has commanded in His Book ... for no one prospers except through abiding by them, and no one is wretched except through repudiating and neglecting them. The preamble is introductory, but by no means perfunctory. Obedience to the divine commands is to be understood not merely as legally binding but as spiritually liberating. Such obedience plays a crucial part in freeing a person from the tyranny of the soul which incites to evil (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفْسُ الْأَمَّارَةُ بِالسُّوءِ</span>) — to which the Imam turns at once after calling Malik to obedience: For truly, the soul incites to evil, unless God has mercy. This sentence is almost identical to the words of the Prophet Joseph in the Quran (Yusuf: 53), and it matters greatly in setting the scene for the ethical and socio-political instructions that follow. The relationship that determines the spiritual substance of the soul — and all the more its moral conduct — is the relationship between the human soul and divine grace. Without grace the soul inclines toward evil; with God''s help it is freed from its base inclinations and guided back to its true, original nature, its fitra, to which the Quran refers in the verse that follows:</p>
<p>So set thy purpose for religion with unswerving devotion: the nature of God (firat Allah), according to which He hath created man. There is no altering God''s creation. That is the right religion (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلدِّينُ ٱلْقَيِّمُ</span>), but most men know not. (Ar-Rum: 30)</p>
<p>The present state of the fallen soul, then, is called to give way to the ideal state of original human nature. That nature is both the origin and the end of the human condition, and at once the true substance of every human soul — always accessible in principle, even if for most people it stays clouded by fallen nature in practice. The quest to revive this original nature turns on the complementary work of the human intellect and divine revelation. In the first sermon of the Nahj, the Imam states that God sent mankind His messengers, dispatching prophets in succession, in order to claim from them [His creatures] the fulfillment of the covenant of His creation; to remind them of His forgotten graces; to remonstrate with them through communication [of His revelation]; to unearth for them the buried treasures of the intellects (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَفْعُ الْعُقُولِ</span>).</p>
<p>These buried treasures'' can be identified with the original, God-given knowledge that belongs to the fitra. Out of this whole human nature, in complete harmony with the divine nature, all the essential virtues flow freely and unhindered. Let your most beloved treasure be the treasure of virtuous acts'', the Imam writes. Virtue will indeed be the most beloved treasure of one who reverses the natural inclinations of the self-centered soul and sets a supernatural orientation toward its own deepest nature — and by that very act, toward the divine Reality that furnishes the foundations in being of all authentic virtue. For <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>, the Real, is not only the deepest foundation of virtue; it is the true substance of every positive quality, and, in the end, of every single thing that exists. As one of the most celebrated supplications attributed to the Imam puts it:</p>
<p>By Thy tremendousness (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِعَظَمَتِكَ</span>), which has filled all things ... by Thy Names which have filled the foundations of all things, by Thy knowledge which encompasses all things, by the light of Thy face, through which all things are illumined ...</p>
<p>From this vantage point, every virtue can be seen as the reflection, on the human plane, of a divine attribute — what is called a divine Name. Hence a theme running through the whole ihsani tradition, the path of spiritual excellence (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>) that the Prophet defined as worshipping God as though one could see Him, knowing that even if one does not see Him, He sees him. That theme finds expression in the saying: Assume the character traits of God (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخَلَّقُوا بِأَخْلَاقِ اللهِ</span>).</p>
<p>The link between human virtue and divine reality is wholly indispensable in the Imam''s perspective. Subjectively, the practice of virtue draws down the matching divine quality; objectively, the divine quality is the source and — in accordance with true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> — the actual substance of all human virtue. Every positive quality, both in itself and in its manifestation, belongs to God, the One who has no partner theologically and has no second ontologically. This mystical grasp of the principle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> is strongly implied in the Quranic verse, And thou threw not when thou didst throw [a handful of dust], but God it was who threw (Al-Anfal: 17). These divine qualities are not to be seen as purely transcendent, as though they were wholly one with the divine essence and utterly beyond the soul''s reach. They are also to be grasped as exemplars, as patterns of perfection for the soul to imitate as fully as it can. The goal is to become as much like God as possible; yet this growth can unfold only within the bounds of one''s servitude to Him. A person is generous for the sake of God, because God is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الکریم</span>, the Generous; he is compassionate and merciful because He is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن الرحیم</span> — never out of any claim that I am generous'' or I am merciful, for the aim is to imitate God''s qualities, not to claim to embody Him in person. The individual must therefore be effaced before God in order to be truly virtuous, and must at the same time share in and embody the very qualities he wishes God to show him. He stays lowly, humble, and effaced throughout, certain that no measure of virtue or intelligence can ever lessen or match the qualities of God — there being no proportion between the Absolute and the relative, the Creator and the created. His effort to embody these qualities is strengthened to the degree that he apprehends the divine attributes existentially, through intuition, rather than merely through reason, in theory. It is like gazing into a mirror: what one sees is a reflection, but an inverted one. The aim is to catch, on the human plane, the inverted reflection of divine transcendence. For man is at once <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِیفَۃُ اللَّہِ</span>, God''s vicegerent upon the earth, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادُ اللّٰہِ</span>, His slave. The awareness of servitude keeps him, even while he represents God, from slipping into rivalry with Him in grandeur; the active commitment to representing God''s attributes guards him from the spiritual paralysis of neglecting his duties toward the world. Such is the meaning of the verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ أَنتُمُ ٱلْفُقَرَآءُ إِلَى ٱللَّهِۖ وَٱللَّهُ هُوَ ٱلْغَنِىُّ ٱلْحَمِيدُ</span>, O mankind, you are the destitute before God, and God is the Self-sufficient, the Praiseworthy (Fatir: 15). There must be a synthesis between the active human virtues and the spiritual awareness of one''s nothingness before the divine Reality. The bond between intelligence and humility deserves emphasis here: intellectual perception of the divine attributes produces humility, and the effacement of the soul in turn draws attention away from the ego and toward the transcendent reality as the source of all virtue and truth.</p>
<p>The notion of the soul which incites to evil, and the Quranic story of Joseph (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُوسُفُ</span>) in which it arises, deserves closer attention. The Imam could assume that his readers knew this context well, and he clearly meant it to stay before the mind of Malik, and of anyone else reading this letter on governance.</p>
<p>Joseph gives this description of man''s base inclinations after his innocence of every charge has been established before the Egyptian king. Even so, he says, I do not exonerate myself. Truly, the soul incites to evil, unless my Lord has mercy. On the two occasions when he faced the seductive temptations of Zulaykha, the Quran makes clear that he resisted not through his own moral resources but through the grace of God.</p>
<p>This theme of human helplessness before gratification that is alluring but immoral is strongly implied in the Imam''s repetition of the Quranic phrase, Truly the soul incites to evil, unless God has mercy. Here, then, are the two poles that draw the ordinary soul, the soul not yet at peace: its own base inclinations toward the negative possibilities of the fleeting world of appearances, and the higher pull toward the grace of God, which draws the soul back toward the pure positivity of ultimate reality. The sheer sharpness of the contrast within this powerful Quranic sentence quickens an awareness of the soul''s absolute and unconditional need of God in every circumstance. It is a most fitting way to open a letter of instruction to a man about to be given authority over a vast and wealthy country, where temptations lie in wait on every side. It stands as a solemn reminder of the soul''s openness to allurement on every plane, gross and subtle alike — an openness the soul cannot neutralize by itself, but only through the mercy and grace of God. And yet, to return to Joseph''s words, the vision of the evidence of God implies active discernment and, as its moral consequence, firm self-control. Human effort to avoid sin therefore does not contradict the need for grace in the attainment of virtue; rather, it expresses a grace already given, already present within the conscientious soul. The very capacity of the human will to strive for virtue is itself a grace already there, set within the spiritual constitution, or fitra — though a grace that must be made present, or actualized, through the sincere effort to draw upon this God-given power.</p>
<p>How this grace enters the soul as a shaping force of moral and spiritual life becomes clearer later in the Imam''s letter. For now, the negative side of the question calls for more elaboration, since the ruler''s ever-present temptation to abuse his power and commit injustice and tyranny deserves more than a passing mention. One might argue that how to refrain from injustice is a far harder and subtler question than its positive counterpart, how to act with justice. The Imam sets out the positive rules of political governance directly and plainly; what is far less plain is whether any holder of office can actually stay faithful to those rules when the temptations of power press upon him. Laws can be framed to punish corruption, but what no law can supply is the will, or the capacity, to stay incorruptible before the blandishments of political authority. This helps explain why the Imam''s exhortations take on so markedly negative a cast in the early part of the letter — the command to break the soul in the face of passionate desires'', to dominate your inclination, to withhold yourself from that which is not lawful for you. It also explains why the moral demand of justice must be set firmly within a lived spirituality. Without that grounding, commands to be fair, just, and honest lack the existential quality that turns them from abstract propositions into unquestionable realities, and that turns formal obedience to rules imposed from outside into a spiritual kinship with the source of those rules.</p>
<p>Living by just precepts then stops being a matter of compliance with something imposed from outside and becomes instead the moral companion of one''s inner being. In other words, a whole approach to justice leads to an identification with what is right, with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span> itself, rather than merely to the doing of what is right. There is a shift of consciousness from doing to being, from action to contemplation — yet without any weakening or undermining of action. On the contrary, action comes to reflect, on its own plane, the divine object of contemplation, and so becomes more effective, not less. An orientation toward the Real lends a deeper degree of reality both to what one does and to one''s own mode of being. One does what is right insofar as one is real; the Islamic spiritual tradition names this process of realization <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحْقِيق</span>, making real. One''s ethical conduct is made real through spirituality, and spirituality, in turn, is expressed by, and made more concrete through, the practice of virtue.</p>
<p>The Imam then issues a stern warning: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلاَ تَنْصِبَنَّ نَفْسَكَ لِحَرْبِ اللهِ</span>, Do not set your soul up for war with God. One may well ask what this means, and what kinds of acts or attitudes place the soul at war with God. The phrase seems to reach deeper than plain disobedience, deeper than the many breaches of religious obligation named at the letter''s outset. That is the most obvious sense of fighting against God, but it does not rule out subtler forms of opposition to divine reality — attitudes that may go along with outward acts of obedience yet contradict their very spirit. One example is the cardinal vice of pride (takabbur, or kibr), against which the Imam warns again and again: the inner state in which a person feels superior and treats others highhandedly, its signs so plain in his conduct that they cannot be hidden. A ruler''s pride is a theft of a divine attribute, a seizing of a greatness that belongs to God alone, who declares: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكِبْرِيَاءُ رِدَائِي، وَالْعَظَمَةُ إِزَارِي، فَمَنْ نَازَعَنِي وَاحِدًا مِنْهُمَا، قَذَفْتُهُ فِي النَّارِ</span>, Pride is My cloak and greatness is My robe; whoever contends with Me over either of them, I shall cast him into the Fire. God alone has the right to takabbur, and God alone may be called, without offense, al-Mutakabbir, the Proud. To vaunt oneself before others is, in effect, to defy God''s authority inwardly, and so to deny in an unspoken way His unique and incomparable majesty. This denial of something inseparable from divine sovereignty can itself be understood as a kind of war with God, for a subject who openly defies his king is, in effect, waging war against him. In short, enmity toward transcendent reality is the invisible substance that pervades all pride, and every act of arrogance or oppression a ruler commits against those beneath him.</p>
<p>The paradox here can be grasped only through a sense of spiritual values: an attitude of superiority toward those below is in fact an act of rebellion against that which is above. An injustice toward creatures does two things at once — it draws down upon its perpetrator the justice of the Creator, and it is, in itself, outright opposition to the Creator. What looks like a horizontal vice — horizontal in the sense that it belongs to this world — is in reality a spiritual disorder. The outward immorality of corruption is only the symptom of an inner spiritual malaise: an unspoken denial that the source of all power and authority is absolute, and hence unique, and a forgetting of the truth expressed in the Quranic verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لِّمَنِ ٱلْمُلْكُ ٱلْيَوْم لِلَّهِ ٱلْوَحِدِ ٱلْقَهَّارِ</span>, To whom belongeth the dominion this day? To God, the One, the All-Conquering (Ghafir: 16). This truth should sound constantly in the ears of every ruler, this day understood as every day, not only the Day of Judgment. In the Imam''s sermon known as al-Qasia (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القاسیۃ</span>), the Abasement, so named because it humbles the proud, Iblis (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِبْلِیس</span>) is described as the first of those who competed with God in grandeur, and thus as the very embodiment of pride and arrogance. When he was commanded to prostrate before Adam, Iblis refused, as the Quran recounts:</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذْ قَالَ رَبُّكَ لِلْمَلَٓئِكَةِ إِنِّى خَلِقٌۢ بَشَرًۭا مِّن طِينٍۢ ۝ فَإِذَا سَوَّيْتُهُۥ وَنَفَخْتُ فِيهِ مِن رُّوحِى فَقَعُوا۟ لَهُ سَجِدِينَ ۝ فَسَجَدَ ٱلْمَلَٓئِكَةُ كُلُّهُمْ أَجْمَعُونَ ۝ إِلَّآ إِبْلِيسَ ٱسْتَكْبَرَ وَكَانَ مِنَ ٱلْكَفِرِينَ</span>, When your Lord said to the angels: Truly I am going to create man from clay; and when I have fashioned him and breathed into him of My spirit, then fall down before him prostrate. So the angels prostrated themselves, all of them together; save Iblis: he was proud and was one of the disbelievers'' (Sad: 71-74).</p>
<p>He refused because vehement passion overcame him. He judged himself superior to Adam on the grounds that he was made of fire while Adam was made of clay, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَقْتَنِی مِنْ نَارٍ وَ خَلَقْتَہُ مِنْ طِینٍ</span>. In taking pride in this presumed superiority, and thereby rebelling against God''s command, Iblis becomes not only the first enemy of God but also the leader of the bigots and the forerunner of the proud (imam al-muta assibin wa salaf al-mustakbirin, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِمَامُ الْمُتَعَصِّبِینَ وَ سَلَفُ الْمُسْتَکْبِرِینَ</span>). The Imam warns his listeners: Beware lest he infect you with his disease through his insinuations, whisperings, and machinations. He urges them: Be resolute in placing self-abasement over your heads, casting self-glorification beneath your feet, and removing pride from your necks. And take up humility as the fortified watchtower between you and your enemy.</p>
<p>Humility is the chief weapon in the war against Iblis, whose own aim is the reverse: to instil pride. For pride is not only the worst of vices but the poison that infects and destroys every virtue. A virtue is turned into a vice the moment pride accompanies it. The sin that grieves you is better, in the sight of God, than the virtue that makes you proud. Bound up with this is the point made earlier about the dangers of excessive praise and the conceit it breeds, which are among Satan''s most reliable opportunities to efface the virtue of one who is virtuous''.</p>
<p>Taken together, these statements form a commentary on the Quranic account of Iblis'' disgrace. They grasp a simple act of disobedience at its spiritual root, and in doing so bring home the ever-present danger of falling prey to that hidden form of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> (polytheism) to which the Prophet refers in these words: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الشِّرْكُ فِي هَذِهِ الْأُمَّةِ</span> akhfa <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِنْ دَبِيبِ النَّمْلِ عَلَی حَجَرِ الْأَسْوَدِ فِی لِیلَۃِ الظُّلْمَا</span> — The creeping of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> in my community is more hidden (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْفَى</span>) than the creeping of a black ant over a hard rock on a dark night. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> is commonly taken to mean the worship of material idols, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْکُ الْجَلِیِّ</span>, its open and manifest form; but it must be understood more subtly as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْکُ الْخَفِیِّ</span>, the hidden form — false gods concealed within one''s intentions, attitudes, and orientations, all the elements of hidden pride, vanity, ostentation, and pretentiousness. As the Imam says, Know that the slightest pretension (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ریا</span>) is polytheism. To act for the sake of earning others'' praise is to act for others rather than for God, and so to attribute partners to God without saying so: Whoso acts for other than God, God entrusts him to those for whose sake he acted.</p>
<p>In this light the meaning of Iblis'' disobedience can be understood more deeply. It is the outward moral consequence of an inner spiritual malaise, rooted in that pride which is tantamount to hidden polytheism and which brings a disgraceful humiliation in its train. Iblis is disgraced because he disobeyed God, and he disobeyed God on account of pride. Pride is therefore, in and of itself, a disgrace — a state of dis-grace, a perversion of the human condition that carries within it the seed of its own inexorable inversion. Do you not see how God humiliated him through his pride? the Imam asks. To be proud is already, in reality, a form of humiliation, for to make oneself great is tantamount to self-destruction.</p>
<p>Returning to the pride of those in power, the Imam sets out concisely the train of thought that starts up in the mind of a ruler as he steps onto the slippery slope toward oppression: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلاَ تَقُولَنَّ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنِّي مُؤَمَّرٌ</span>! <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمُرُ</span>! <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأُطَاعُ</span>! <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ ذلِكَ إِدْغَالٌ فِي الْقَلْبِ، وَمَنْهَكَةٌ لِلدِّينِ</span> — Do not say, &quot;I have been given authority, I order and am obeyed&quot;, for this leads to corruption in the heart and the erosion of religion. This self-glorification is seen as producing first an inner, spiritual corruption, that of the heart, and only then the destruction of religion. The order of priority makes a point: the outward prescriptions of religion require the right inward attitude if they are to be applied with integrity and maintained with stability. The Imam''s thought might be restated thus: when power goes to the head, faith departs from the heart; and conversely, for faith to be properly manifested in the world, it must flow from a heart suffused with humility. This is another aspect of putting each thing in its proper place, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وضع کل شئی حق محلہ</span>.</p>
<p>The Imam continues: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذَا أَحْدَثَ لَكَ مَا أَنْتَ فِيهِ مِنْ سُلْطَانِكَ أُبَّهَةً أَوْ مَخِيلَةً، فَانْظُرْ إِلَى عِظَمِ مُلْكِ اللهِ فَوْقَكَ، فَإِنَّ ذلِكَ يُطَامِنُ إِلَيْكَ مِنْ طِمَاحِكَ، وَيَكُفُّ عَنْكَ مِنْ غَرْبِكَ،يَفِيءُ إِلَيْكَ بِمَا عَزَبَ عَنْكَ مِنْ عَقْلِكَ</span> — If the authority of your position engenders vanity and arrogance, then look at the grandeur of God''s dominion above you … this will calm your ambition, restrain you from your own vehemence, and restore to you what had strayed from your intellect. The bond between intellect and humility deserves close attention here. The part of the intellect that strays'' when vanity and arrogance take hold is precisely the part that is conscious of God''s absolute reality and of the relative, derivative, and ultimately illusory character of everything else. It is telling that the word for arrogance, makhila (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَخِيلَةً</span>), comes from the same root as the word for imagination, khayal (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خیال</span>), and is thus intimately connected with the realm of the imaginary, the fanciful, the illusory, and the unreal: when imagination fastens upon the unreal, it becomes infused with pride and turns into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مخیلۃ</span>, arrogance. Arrogant pride is therefore an intense form of self-delusion rooted in a defect of the intellect. It manifests as a vice, but its cause runs deeper than the moral level, for it involves not merely an overestimation of oneself but, more fundamentally, an underestimation of the Absolute. Seen this way, the overt egotism of the tyrant is an intensification of the congenital egocentricity that clings to anyone who has not realized the truth of God''s sole reality and the ultimately illusory nature of all else. The sage alone fully assimilates the truth expressed in such Quranic verses as: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلُّ شَىْءٍ هَالِكٌ إِلَّا وَجْهَهُ</span> — Everything is dying away except His countenance (Al-Qasas: 88); <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُلُّ مَنْ عَلَيْهَا فَانٍۢ ۝ وَيَبْقَىٰ وَجْهُ رَبِّكَ ذُو ٱلْجَلَلِ وَٱلْإِكْرَامِ</span> — Everything that is thereon is perishing, and there subsisteth but the Face of thy Lord, possessor of Glory and Bounty (Ar-Rahman: 26-27); and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقُلْ جَآءَ ٱلْحَقُّ وَزَهَقَ ٱلْبَطِلُ ۚ إِنَّ ٱلْبَطِلَ كَانَ زَهُوقًۭا</span> — The Truth hath come and falsehood hath passed away; indeed, falsehood is ever passing away (Al-Isra: 81). Only the sage draws from this truth the humility that dissolves all individualism and egocentricity — the roots of pride, vanity, and arrogance, which are in turn the lifeblood of tyranny and oppression.</p>
<p>The Imam''s warning can now be better appreciated: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيَّاكَ وَمُسَامَةَ اللهِ فِي عَظَمَتِهِ، وَالتَّشَبُّهَ بِهِ فِي جَبَرُوتِهِ، فَإِنَّ اللهَ يُذِلُّ كُلَّ جَبَّار، وَيُهِينُ كُلَّ مُخْتَال</span> — Beware of comparing [yourself] with God in greatness and likening [yourself] to Him in might, for God abases every tyrant and disgraces every braggart. A ruler who does not act with justice toward God and His creatures — for the Imam commands, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنْصِفِ اللهَ وَأَنْصِفِ النَّاسَ مِنْ نَفْسِكَ، وَمِنْ خَاصَّةِ أَهْلِكَ، وَمَنْ لَكَ فِيهِ هَوىً مِنْ رَعِيَّتِكَ، فَإِنَّكَ إِلاَّ تَفْعَلْ تَظْلِمْ</span> — will find that not only His creatures but God Himself becomes his adversary: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَنْ ظَلَمَ عِبَادَ اللهِ كَانَ اللهُ خَصْمَهُ دُونَ عِبَادِهِ، وَمَنْ خَاصَمَهُ اللهُ أَدْحَضَ حُجَّتَهُ، وَكَانَ للهِ حَرْباً حَتَّى يَنْزِعَ وَيَتُوبَ</span> — He remains at war with God until he desists and repents. The tyrant will in the end be compelled to repent, even if only at the moment of death and imminent judgment. And even if he seems to have escaped the consequences of his tyranny in this life, he cannot escape them in the Hereafter.</p>
<p>As for the nature of the tyrant''s war against God in this world — that is, before repentance or judgment overtakes him — it consists in his implicit opposition to ultimate reality, his violation of the deepest nature of things, of the true nature of his own being, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فطرۃ</span>. This war takes various forms.</p>
<p>One form is the very persistence of tyranny itself. The longer it continues, the more wretched the tyrant''s soul becomes, and the more firmly the seeds of misery are planted in his being. This is what is implied by the Quranic doctrine of istidraj (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِدْرَاج</span>), the drawing out of punishment by degrees. The word derives from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">درج</span>, meaning stages — as even in Urdu one says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">درجہ بدرجہ</span>, step by step. God uses it in Surat al-Araf: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمِمَّنْ خَلَقْنَآ أُمَّةٌۭ يَهْدُونَ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَبِهِ يَعْدِلُونَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۱۸۱</span>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلَّذِينَ كَذَّبُوا۟ بِـَٔايَتِنَا سَنَسْتَدْرِجُهُم مِّنْ حَيْثُ لَا يَعْلَمُونَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۱۸۲</span> — Of those We have created are people who guide with truth and dispense justice therewith. But those who reject Our signs, We will lead them on by degrees [to punishment] from whence they know not (Al-Araf: 181-2). This punishment meted out by degrees can take the form of apparent worldly success, as the verse makes clear: ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تُعْجِبْكَ أَمْوَلُهُمْ وَأَوْلَدُهُمْ ۚ إِنَّمَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ أَن يُعَذِّبَهُم بِهَا فِى ٱلدُّنْيَا وَتَزْهَقَ أَنفُسُهُمْ وَهُمْ كَفِرُونَ</span> ‎<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">۸۵</span> — Let not their wealth nor their children please thee. God desireth only to punish them thereby in the world, that their souls shall pass away while they are disbelievers'' (At-Tawbah: 85). A tyrant or oppressor can be a Muslim in the formal sense and yet at the same time be guilty of a mode of disbelief — taking the word kafir in its strictly etymological sense, as one who covers over the truth and its moral concomitants, both in his very being and in his actions. This connects directly with the contrast drawn in the verses just quoted (Al-Araf: 181-2): between those who dispense justice in accordance with the truth and those who reject Our signs'' — the oppressors and tyrants, precisely.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'justice and the tyrant''s pride';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>One form this enmity with God takes is the inner war against one''s own conscience — what Urdu calls the ⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضمیر</span>⟫. The voice of conscience is the whole substance of secular ethics, and it can be understood here within its fuller context, not merely as the overgrown foundation of individual morality that secular ethical thought has made of it. That fuller context is the source — beyond the individual, beyond the physical — from which individual conscience springs: the divine infusion of spiritual discernment into the soul, the divine inspiration that lies beneath every distinction between right and wrong. At the level of the morally responsible soul, then, the divine quality of justice becomes the voice of inner conscience:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ash-Shams: 7-8</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَفْسٍۢ وَمَا سَوَّىٰهَا ۷ فَأَلْهَمَهَا فُجُورَهَا وَتَقْوَىٰهَا ۸</span></p><p class="tr">And the soul and that which perfected it, inspiring it with [consciousness of] its wickedness and its righteousness'' (Ash-Shams: 7-8).</p></blockquote>
<p>The war against one''s own conscience, even when the oppressor scarcely acknowledges it, is in truth a war against God, and all the more so.</p>
<p>At a deeper level, then, this war against God is revealed as a war against the very reality that makes the soul a soul. The soul contains not only a moral conscience — a built-in, inseparable awareness of the difference between good and evil, placed in it by God — but also an inborn consciousness of God''s lordship and of its own answering servitude as a creature. Spiritual consciousness is therefore the source of moral conscience. The Quran describes how this spiritual consciousness dwells within each soul in terms that stress its nature before and beyond existence, calling up a moment at the very dawn of creation — a moment, or degree of being, that comes before the human condition itself yet enters into the very shaping of the human soul:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-A''raf: 172</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِذْ أَخَذَ رَبُّكَ مِنۢ بَنِىٓ آدَمَ مِن ظُهُورِهِمْ ذُرِّيَّتَهُمْ وَأَشْهَدَهُمْ عَلَىٰٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ أَلَسْتُ بِرَبِّكُمْ ۖ قَالُوا۟ بَلَىٰ ۛ شَهِدْنَآ ۛ أَن تَقُولُوا۟ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَمَةِ إِنَّا كُنَّا عَنْ هَذَا غَفِلِينَ ۱۷۲</span></p><p class="tr">And when thy Lord brought forth from the children of Adam, from their loins, their seed, and made them testify against their souls [saying], &quot;Am I not your Lord?&quot; They said, &quot;Yes, verily, we testify.&quot; [This was] lest ye say on the Day of Resurrection, &quot;Truly, of this we were unaware&quot; (Al-Araf: 172).</p></blockquote>
<p>The moment He created them He made them witness His glory and testify to it, so that they could plead no ignorance on the Day of Judgment.</p>
<p>What comes to light here is that the source of morality rises above the human level even while forming part of the very definition of what it is to be human. Seen this way, what makes the human soul fully human is precisely that element of transcendence which infinitely surpasses the soul and yet, mysteriously, supplies its own deepest being. In this light, tyranny and oppression take on a subtler meaning: these vices are drawn much closer to the lived reality of every soul, rather than confined to those who hold and abuse political power. The tyrant is, before anything else, one who violates his own soul, one who lets it be ruled by the soul which commands evil, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفْس</span> al-ammara (⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفْسُ الْاَمَّارَۃُ بِالسُّوءِ</span>⟫). And so the saying the Imam cites as summing up the attitude of one on the way to corrupting his own soul and becoming an oppressor — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلاَ تَقُولَنَّ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنِّي مُؤَمَّرٌ آمُرُ فَأُطَاعُ</span>, I order and am obeyed — is to be understood not only in social terms but also within the small world of the soul. It becomes the utterance of the evil element within the soul: the nafs al-ammara, which literally orders'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آمُرُ</span>) and is obeyed by every other faculty of the soul — intelligence, imagination, will, sentiment, sensibility, and the rest.</p>
<p>The struggle against one''s own soul (mujahadat <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفْس</span>, ⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاھَدَۃُ النَّفْسِ</span>⟫) is a theme the Imam returns to again and again. The need to wrestle with and master one''s own faults is an essential part of justice in the sense defined earlier: to put each thing in its proper place begins with setting oneself right before presuming to reform or govern others; it means restoring the divine nature within oneself. As the Imam says, If your aspiration is to reform the people, begin with yourself, for your pursuit of the reform of others while your own soul is corrupt is the greatest of faults. In his letter appointing Muhammad b. Abi Bakr (⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَمَّدُ بْنُ اَبِی بَکْرٍ</span>⟫), the governor of Egypt before Malik (⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَالِکُ اَشْتَرُ</span>⟫), the Imam draws a firm link between the granting of power and the duty to resist one''s own soul: And be aware, O Muhammad b. Abi Bakr, that I have appointed you as governor over the most immense of the forces at my disposal, the people of Egypt, so you are obliged to oppose your soul and protect your religion. The tie between the size of the power placed in one''s hands and the spiritual responsibility that comes with it is worth close attention. A ruler must be on guard, first against his own soul and its desires, and second in regard to the religion. The greater the power at hand, the greater the duty to control and restrain oneself; for if the soul gives way to its desires and satisfies them through that power, the outcome is the corruption not only of the ruler himself but of the very religion he is charged to uphold. Here again priorities are set by a principle that is essentially spiritual: the outward form and practice of religion depends for its integrity on the inward state of the Muslim''s soul. This is one more expression of the principle of putting each thing in its proper place. The commanding soul that claims for itself the right to independence and mastery is described with terse precision in the Quranic verse:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-''Alaq: 6-7</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَلَّآ إِنَّ ٱلْإِنْسَنَ لَيَطْغَىٰٓ ۶ أَنْ رَّآهُ ٱسْتَغْنَىٰٓ ۷</span></p><p class="tr">Truly man is rebellious, in that he deemeth himself independent (Al-Alaq: 6-7).</p></blockquote>
<p>Insofar as the soul pretends to be self-sufficient and tries to cut itself off from its total dependence on God, it sets itself up as a god in its own right — the person coming to believe that he himself is God. Herein lies the true portrait of the tyrant, the one who, as the Quran puts it, taketh his own desire as his god (Al-Jathiyah: 23): <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَرَءَيْتَ مَنِ ٱتَّخَذَ إِلَهَهُۥ هَوَىٰهُ</span>. To know one''s own nothingness before God, and therefore one''s absolute need of Him, is the chief means of loosening the grip of the soul that commands to evil. For only the soul that already commands itself to evil will command others to evil; and only the soul that is lowly before God will be humble, compassionate, and just in its dealings with people.</p>
<p>This principle emerges clearly from these words of the Imam: Let the most beloved of affairs to you be those most centered upon the right, the most comprehensive in justice, and the most inclusive of popular approval, for the disapproval of the common folk undermines the approval of the elite ... This stress on the importance of the common people is not only sound practical counsel for fair and effective government; it can also be read as an echo of the spiritual duty to recognize man''s real lowliness before God, and so as a way of pressing the ruler to identify with the poor rather than the rich, in keeping with the principle:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fatir: 15</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ أَنتُمُ ٱلْفُقَرَآءُ إِلَى ٱللَّهِۖ وَٱللَّهُ هُوَ ٱلْغَنِىُّ ٱلْحَمِيدُ</span></p><p class="tr">O mankind, ye are the poor before God, and God, He is the Rich, the absolutely Praiseworthy (Fatir: 15).</p></blockquote>
<p>The Imam''s own poverty and severe austerity were proverbial. Yet this was far from a simple turning away from the world for its own sake, as his exchange with an ascetic, Asim b. Ziyad, makes clear. Asim''s family had complained to the Imam that he denied himself too much, and the Imam told him to think of his family and not to cut himself off from the good things (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الطَّيِّبَاتُ</span>) that God has permitted. Asim retorted, O Commander of the Faithful, and yet here you are, in your rough clothes and your coarse food! The Imam replied, Woe to you, I am not like you. God, the Exalted, has made it incumbent on true leaders (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَئِمَّةُ الْحَقِّ</span>) that they proportion themselves to the weakest among the people (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يُقَدِّرُ أَنْفُسَهُمْ بِضَعَفَةِ النَّاسِ</span>), so that the poverty of the poor will not engender covetousness. Here is a rigorous statement of the duty of the most powerful to identify themselves, in material terms, with the most powerless. It is hard to imagine a more effective way of lessening worldliness and strengthening spirituality in a society than this deliberate self-lowering, in purely material terms, to the level of the weakest people one rules. He who could have the most chooses to have the least. The Imam implies that if a leader is to stand among the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَئِمَّةُ الْحَقِّ</span>, he must show his attachment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span> — the ultimate spiritual reality, in whose light material wealth counts for nothing. So even though the most powerful and the most powerless may both be poor in material terms, both alike have access to the treasures of the spiritual realm, in proportion to how far they grasp the truth expressed in the verse already cited: O mankind, ye are the poor before God, and God, He is the Rich, the absolutely Praiseworthy. On the level of social psychology, too, the Imam''s insistence that rulers live like the poor, borne out in his own life, is an effective way of ensuring that the unavoidable inequalities of a society will not breed envy among the poor.</p>
<p>The compassion of justice</p>
<p>Identification with the poor also deepens a ruler''s awareness of his own unceasing need of God, helping him to live more fully in reliance on divine mercy, in hope and trust; and the natural companion of such a life is the giving of mercy and compassion to one''s fellow human beings. The capacity to act with compassion in no way conflicts with the demands of justice; it is, rather, an intrinsic part of justice understood ontologically — that is, in accordance with the fundamental nature of reality. Acting with compassion agrees with justice not only in the sense of putting things in their right place, since compassion is to be extended wherever and whenever it is called for; it also agrees with justice by being at one with the true nature of things, with the intrinsic character of the Real. This is why, at the head of every chapter of the Quran but one, the name Allah is followed by the infinitely Compassionate, the ever-Merciful (al-Rahman, al-Rahim). To put it another way, the capacity for compassion springs not so much from personal feeling as from an inborn kinship with the very nature of divine reality — that higher reality in which, as the Quran tells us, My mercy encompasseth all things'' (Al-Araf: 156); a reality in which thy Lord has prescribed for Himself mercy (Al-Anam: 54); a reality whose essential character is fixed by the principle, My mercy takes precedence over My wrath. Among the many sayings of Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) on the need to show mercy are these: The dispensing of mercy brings down [divine] mercy. As you grant mercy, so will you be granted mercy. I am astounded by the person who hopes for mercy from one above him, while he is not merciful to those beneath him.</p>
<p>Neither the severe aspect of the nature of things nor the wrathful side of God is denied here, but both are clearly placed beneath the higher ontological principle of mercy. There is, in other words, no common measure between the merely relative things that are subject to wrath and punishment and the realities — blessed in their very nature — to which divine mercy and compassion open the way. A soul is therefore more real the more it reflects this predominance of mercy, both in its spiritual orientation and in its moral conduct. And within human society, it lies in the very nature of justice, understood in this sacred way, to lean toward compassion wherever possible, even though there must also be a place for the strict application of corrective penalty where that cannot be avoided. Indeed, in his letter the Imam goes on to instruct Malik to inflict corporal punishment on any executive officer found guilty of misappropriating public funds. The Imam''s counsels and letters are full of stern warnings of swift and severe retribution to his governors, the severity of these warnings matching his unflinching fidelity to the demands of fairness and honesty in handling public revenues. For example, he writes to Ziyad b. Abihi, deputy to the governor of Basra — Ali s cousin Abd Allah b. al-Abbas:</p>
<p>I swear by God an oath in all sincerity: if news reaches me that you have misappropriated the revenue of the Muslims, whether a small or large amount, I shall inflict a severe punishment upon you, one which will lighten your wealth, burden your back, and degrade your affair.</p>
<p>The Imam was just as severe toward his own relatives at the least hint of a request for favors that would require the abuse of public funds. In one sermon he recalls an incident in which his blind brother, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَاقِل</span> b. Abi Talib, asked for funds from the treasury beyond what was his due. He thought I would sell my religion for him, the Imam said. His response was to bring a red-hot piece of iron close to his brother''s body, telling him that by making such a request he was urging Ali himself to enter a far fiercer fire. In the same way, when told of the misappropriation of funds by his cousin and long-standing ally Abd Allah b. al-Abbas, the Imam answered with a thundering rebuke. He ordered his cousin: Fear God and return their property to these people. If you do not, and God gives me authority over you, I excuse myself before God in regard to you [and your wrongdoing], and I shall indeed strike you with my sword, with which whomever I have struck enters the fire [of Hell]. By God, even if Hasan and Husayn had done the like of what you have done, they would not have been granted any leniency by me.</p>
<p>The mention of the Prophet''s grandsons is telling. However close the tie to a wrongdoer, however exalted his standing, the principle of justice demands complete impartiality: there are to be no favors for one''s favorites. This stance is not only the exact opposite of the nepotism that had undermined the rule of the Imam''s predecessor; it is also precisely what the Quran commands: O ye who believe, be staunch in justice, witnesses of God, even though it be against yourselves, your parents or your near of kin ... (An-Nisa: 135).</p>
<p>On the theme of mercy, the passage that follows places the necessity of compassion in a universal context. It stands as one of the Imam''s most important statements on the unity of the human race and the equality of all human beings, and as a corrective to every form of prejudice, particularism, and sectarianism that would confine justice or compassion to members of one''s own group, however it is defined:</p>
<p>Infuse your heart with mercy for the subjects, love for them and kindness toward them. Be not like a ravenous beast of prey above them, seeking to devour them. For they are of two types: either your brother in religion or your like in creation. Mistakes slip from them, defects emerge from them, deliberately or accidentally. So bestow upon them your forgiveness and your pardon, just as you would have God bestow upon you His forgiveness and pardon; for you are above them, and the one who has authority over you is above you, and God is above him who appointed you ... and through them He tests you.</p>
<p>Here the universal reach of compassion is joined to a reminder of God''s absolute sovereignty. No man — neither a governor nor the ruler who appointed him — is anything more than a slave of God, wholly dependent upon His mercy. Anyone who stands in some position of superiority over others must therefore keep his own lowness before the Absolute always in mind. That awareness works in two directions at once: it moves him to compassion toward those below him, and it draws down upon him the compassion of God above him.</p>
<p>One incident shows well how the Imam put justice into practice with compassion, and gives vivid form to the principle already noted, that all people are your like in creation. The Imam came upon an old, blind beggar and asked about him. He was told that the man was a Christian. He said to those around him, You have employed him until he is old and infirm, and now you refuse to help him. Give him maintenance from the public funds (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْتُ الْمَالِ</span>). The words state in brief the Islamic principle of social welfare (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَصْلَحَة</span>), which rests on redistributive justice and on a strict refusal to discriminate between Muslims and others. Social justice and religious equality flow from a compassion understood not as mere sentiment but as an intrinsic dimension of the Real; and so they become a fundamental religious duty binding on every Muslim, ruler and citizen alike.</p>
<p>The last sentence of the passage quoted earlier — which warns the governor to regard his power over his subjects not as a privilege but as a trial from God — expresses the Imam''s own attitude to rulership. In the famous sermon al-Shiqshiqiyya he says that he accepted power only as an unavoidable duty:</p>
<p>Had God not taken from the learned [a promise] that they would not acquiesce in the rapacity of the tyrant nor in the hunger of the oppressed ... I would truly have flung its reins [that of the caliphate] back upon its withers ... and you would indeed have discovered that this world of yours is as insignificant to me as that which drips from the nose of a goat.</p>
<p>This is a striking embodiment of Plato''s ideal attitude toward power — the attitude of the true philosopher, who rules solely for the sake of the people, not regarding it as a distinction, but as an unavoidable task ... caring only for the right and the honors to be gained from that, and above all, for justice as the one indispensable thing in whose service and maintenance they will reorganize their own state.</p>
<p>The principle behind this approach to authority and governance is plainly that of noblesse oblige. To be granted the grace, the blessing, or the privilege of knowledge, together with a clear sense of justice, is to be placed under a correspondingly greater obligation: one''s duty to God grows in proportion to the graces bestowed. The Imam gives the principle memorable form in one of his sermons, spoken after one of his supporters had praised him: The blessings of God upon a person do not become immense without the right of God over him becoming immense.</p>
<p>A good opinion of God</p>
<p>A subtle point in the letter is easily overlooked, both because it is put so tersely and because its positive counterpart is never stated outright. It comes as the Imam instructs Malik about the kind of advisers he should keep near him, telling him to avoid misers and cowards:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلاَ تُدْخِلَنَّ فِي مَشُورَتِكَ بَخِيلاً يَعْدِلُ بِكَ عَنِ الْفَضْلِ، وَيَعِدُكَ الْفَقْرَ، وَلاَ جَبَاناً يُضعِّفُكَ عَنِ الاْمُورِ، وَلاَ حَرِيصاً يُزَيِّنُ لَكَ الشَّرَهَ بِالْجَوْرِ، فَإِنَّ الْبُخْلَ وَالْجُبْنَ وَالْحِرْصَ غَرَائِزُ شَتَّى يَجْمَعُهَا سُوءُ الظَّنِّ بِاللهِ</span>.</p></blockquote>
<p>Do not include among those you consult a miser who would hold you back from generosity and warn you of destitution; nor a coward who would make you feel too weak for your affairs; nor a greedy person who would make the amassing of wealth by evil means seem attractive to you. For miserliness, cowardice and avarice are so many diverse inclinations, all united by a bad opinion of God.</p>
<p>The phrase a bad opinion (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوءُ الظَّنِّ بِاللهِ</span>, su al-zann) points to a basic misunderstanding of the divine nature, and from that misunderstanding a whole series of false ideas about the qualities of God follows. Each vice grows from it. The miser holds the bad opinion that God may fail to provide in time to come; the coward, that God may fail to protect him; the greedy person, that God has given him less than he needs. Seen this way, every human vice traces back to a failure of the intellect, which is itself the fruit of weak faith, or even of outright atheism — for to disbelieve in God is to deny His reality, and so to hold the worst opinion of Him. The converse follows just as plainly. A strong and deep faith produces a good opinion (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُسْنُ الظَّنّ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَسَنَ</span> al-zann) of God: a true perception of the divine Reality, both intellectual and contemplative. And this good opinion in turn lets the soul be genuinely, and not merely superficially, marked by the divine qualities that Reality contains. Those qualities are to be understood as transcendent — absolutely one with the divine Essence, and utterly beyond the reach of the human soul — but also as models, exemplars, patterns, and perfections that the soul is to imitate so far as it can.</p>
<p>This is the very opposite of comparing oneself with God in greatness''. One must become as like God as possible, yet this growth unfolds entirely within the unchanging fact of one''s servitude to Him: it is the imitation of God''s qualities, never the personification of God. One must be generous because God is al-Karim (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْکَرِیم</span>), the Generous, and compassionate and merciful because He is al-Rahman (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرحمن الرحیم</span>), the Compassionate — not because one is generous or merciful in oneself. Yet one must be equally lowly, humble, and self-effacing, for no measure of virtue or intelligence can lessen the gulf between the Absolute and the relative, between the Creator and the created. Humility and self-effacement, and virtues like them, are so many ways of expressing the human being''s utter dependence upon God, or of manifesting the transcendence of the Absolute. Between the human and the divine, then, there is analogy of two kinds, positive and inverse. By the first, He who shows not mercy will not have mercy shown to him; by the second, as quoted above:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Fatir: 15</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ أَنتُمُ ٱلْفُقَرَآءُ إِلَى ٱللَّهِۖ وَٱللَّهُ هُوَ ٱلْغَنِىُّ ٱلْحَمِيدُ</span></p><p class="tr">O mankind, ye are the poor before God, and God, He is the Rich, the absolutely Praiseworthy (Fatir: 15).</p></blockquote>
<p>The two must therefore be brought together: the active human virtues, which are positive reflections of the divine qualities, and the spiritual awareness of one''s own nothingness before the divine Reality, which is, on the human plane, the inverted reflection of divine transcendence. The image is that of a mirror. What one sees in a mirror is oneself, but reversed; and to look into the divine mirror is to see, on the human plane, that same inverted reflection of God''s transcendence. A human being is at once the slave and the representative of God — abduLlah (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادُ اللّٰہِ</span>) and khalifatuLlah (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِیفَۃُ اللَّہِ</span>). Awareness of one''s servitude keeps one from sliding, even without knowing it, from representing God to rivalling Him in grandeur. A living commitment to representing God keeps that awareness of servitude from sinking into forgetfulness of the dignity and the responsibilities of the human state — a forgetfulness that ends in spiritual paralysis and the neglect of one''s duty toward the world. The balance between these two sides of human existence is itself a further aspect of sacred justice, of setting things in their right order, and it tunes the quality of moral action to the harmony of pure being.</p>
<p>State and society</p>
<p>The Imam then describes in the letter the different classes of people within state and society — the army, the scribes, the judiciary, executive officers, tax-collectors, peasants, merchants, artisans, and last of all the destitute — each with its own rights and duties. The instructions in this part of the letter may be regarded as justice at its sharpest edge in practice, taking full account of the demands of social and political life. The governor is to treat every class under his authority as holding both rights and obligations; yet the essential character of his relationship to society as a whole is once again fixed by his primary duty to God. For it is God who has assigned each class its share, and who has laid down, as a binding covenant from Him, the limits and duties of each, according to His Book or the Sunna of the Prophet:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَكُلٌّ قَدْ سَمَّى اللهُ سَهْمَهُ، وَوَضَعَ عَلَى حَدِّهِ وَفَرِيضَتِهِ فِي كِتَابِهِ أَوْ سُنَّةِ نَبِيِّهِ</span> (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَآلِهِ</span>) <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَهْداً مِنْهُ عِنْدَنَا مَحْفُوظاً</span></p></blockquote>
<p>God has ordained the share of every one of them and laid down His covenant about the limits of each in His Book and the Sunna of His Prophet by way of a settlement which is preserved with us.</p>
<p>The governor''s duty to society is therefore part of the universal covenant between man and God — the ahd (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عہد</span>) that recurs so often in the Quran. By calling on this Quranic term to reinforce the duty of acting justly toward every class of society, the Imam knew that his words would gain immeasurably in force from their resonance with verses such as these:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Ar-Ra''d: 19-20</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَمَن يَعْلَمُ أَنَّمَآ أُنزِلَ إِلَيْكَ مِن رَّبِّكَ ٱلْحَقُّ كَمَنْ هُوَ أَعْمَىٰٓ ۚ إِنَّمَا يَتَذَكَّرُ أُو۟لُوا۟ ٱلْأَلْبَبِ ۱۹ ٱلَّذِينَ يُوفُونَ بِعَهْدِ ٱللَّهِ وَلَا يَنقُضُونَ ٱلْمِيثَقَ ۲۰</span></p><p class="tr">Is one who knoweth that what is revealed to thee from thy Lord is the truth like one who is blind? But only men of substance take heed, those who abide by the covenant (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عہد</span>) of God, and break not the tryst (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمِيثَقَ</span>) (Ar-Ra''d: 19-20).</p></blockquote>
<p>and:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Isra: 34</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا تَقْرَبُوا۟ مَالَ ٱلْيَتِيمِ إِلَّا بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ حَتَّىٰ يَبْلُغَ أَشُدَّهُۥ ۚ وَأَوْفُوا۟ بِٱلْعَهْدِ ۖ إِنَّ ٱلْعَهْدَ كَانَ مَسْـُٔولًۭا ۳۴</span></p><p class="tr">Approach not the wealth of the orphan except with that which is more right, until he attains maturity; and abide by the covenant, truly, the covenant will be asked about (Al-Isra: 34).</p></blockquote>
<p>Significantly, just before the instructions quoted above, the Imam advises Malik to study long with the scholars'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَعُلَمَاءِ</span>) and to converse long with the sages'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحُكَمَاءِ</span>):</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quote"><span class="q-band q-band--quote"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Saying</span></span><p class="ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَكْثِرْ مُدَارَسَةَ الَعُلَمَاءِ، وَمُنَافَثَةَ الْحُكَمَاءِ، فِي تَثْبِيتِ مَا صَلَحَ عَلَيْهِ أَمْرُ بِلاَدِكَ، وَإِقَامَةِ مَا اسْتَقَامَ بِهِ النَّاسُ قَبْلَكَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>The purpose is to consolidate that which brings well-being to your lands, and to further entrench that which has already been established by your predecessors. The distinction between scholars and sages matters here. The scholars (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء</span>) know the formal rules as set down in the Quran and the Sunna — the letter of the law, the Sharia (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شریعۃ</span>). The sages (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حکما</span>) know how to apply that knowledge; theirs is the spirit of wisdom, for wisdom is knowledge put into practice. The distinction is between the letter of the law and the spirit behind it — the spirit without which the rules cannot be applied with care and sensitivity.</p>
<p>There follows a passage that describes, with admirable concision, how each class or segment of society depends upon the others. It begins with the army as the foundation of the state and ends with the governor himself as the center around which the different elements turn. The image is that of a wheel. The governor is the hub, working on the horizontal plane, among created things; the various classes — the army, the scribes, the executive officers, the dispensers of justice, the tax-collectors, the merchants, the artisans, and, lowest of all, the needy and destitute — are the spokes that radiate from him to the rim. Yet the hub is nothing without the axle that turns it. This horizontal center is nothing without the vertical axis that joins it, and through it the whole of society, to the Real. As a hub is useless without its link to the axle, so all the governor''s efforts are useless without his link to the Absolute, on which he must wholly depend if society is truly to flourish. The soldiers are called the fortresses'' of the subjects; and at the close of the passage the governor is told that he cannot accomplish what God requires of him without resolute determination and recourse to Him (al-ihtimam and al-istiana) — a point driven home later in the letter, where the needs of the poor are addressed. Of the instructions concerning the several classes of state and society, a few salient points are enough. For the commander of the troops, Malik is told to choose the man who is the most sincere in relation to God, the Prophet and your Imam, the purest of heart, the one most excellent in forbearance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلْم</span>), who is slow to anger, happy to pardon, kind to the weak, severe with the strong; one who is neither stirred by violence nor held back by weakness.</p>
<p>This portrait of the ideal commander reflects the Imam''s own character. What stands out is its emphasis on kindness, gentleness, and forbearance — qualities not usually associated with fighting men. Here is a spiritually refined approach to an activity that, left to itself, easily tramples the norms of decency and propriety. Once again the Imam''s description of the perfect warrior resembles Plato''s description of the guardian, the protector of the ideal state. Plato states the central difficulty in raising a force to protect and defend the city as this: how are men whose nature is spirited and courageous to be kept from behaving pugnaciously to one another and to the rest of their countrymen? For they must be gentle to their own people and dangerous only to their enemies. This echoes the Quranic description of those who are with the Prophet as severe against the disbelievers, compassionate among themselves'' (Al-Fath: 29) — hence the Imam''s insistence on the gentle qualities a warrior needs as the essential complement to the martial ones.</p>
<p>For the next class, the judges, the Imam names the virtues that must be present in those who dispense legal justice: they must not be confused by complexities or provoked by litigants; they must be unflinching before the truth, free from greed, unsatisfied by shallow solutions, rigorous in argument, steadfast in seeking out the truth of every matter, and proof against praise and temptation. Such people, the Imam adds, are indeed rare. He also recommends that judges be paid generously, so that any deficiency will be removed, thus diminishing the need for help from people — a condition for keeping any judiciary incorruptible.</p>
<p>For the administrators, Malik is told to test their honesty before employing them and to take scrupulous care that no partiality or favouritism sways his choices. He is to keep watching and investigating their conduct; and, as noted earlier, severe punishments are to be imposed wherever there is evidence of abuse of privilege or misuse of public funds.</p>
<p>For those who till the soil, from whom the land tax is collected, the Imam offers this wise maxim: Let your concern with the cultivation of the land outweigh your concern with the collection of the tax, for no tax will be collected if there be no cultivation. And whoever exacts the tax without cultivating the land ruins the land and destroys the people. His further urgings to attend to the needs of the tillers and to deal fairly with all who seek redress of wrongs close with this warning:</p>
<p>The devastation of the land only comes about through the destitution of its inhabitants; and the destitution of its inhabitants only comes about when the desire to amass wealth rules the souls of the governors, when they have doubts about what endures, and when they profit little from exemplary teachings.</p>
<p>As for the phrase having doubts about what endures'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُوءُ ظَنِّهِمْ بِالْبَقَاءِ</span>), the commentators suggest two meanings. It may be that the governors think they will last forever, forgetting that they will die and be judged in the Hereafter; or it may be that they doubt their position will last long, and so seize whatever opportunities they can while they can. The first reading seems the more likely, so closely does it echo the following Quranic verses — which may themselves be counted among the exemplary teachings'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبْرَة</span>) the Imam names at the very end of the sentence: Woe be to every slandering backbiter, who amasseth wealth and counteth it. He thinketh that his wealth will render him immortal. Nay, but he will be flung to the all-consuming Fire (Al-Humazah: 1-4).</p>
<p>The Imam uses for the amassing of wealth the same word, jamaa, that appears in this verse. The lesson is plain: greed is a symptom of spiritual short-sightedness. Forgetfulness of what truly endures breeds greed in one degree or another — greed being, here, the craving for a false material richness in place of a true spiritual plenitude — and greed in turn breeds exploitation and oppression. Once more the Imam''s vision shows that the vice of injustice does not merely produce corruption; it is itself the product of a chain of causes rooted in a fundamental inversion of spiritual values, a failure to see the true nature of things.</p>
<p>Conscience, Compassion, and the Ordering of Society</p>
<p>The same vision that traces injustice to a disorder within the soul governs the more practical counsel that follows, where the ordering of society is shown to rest upon the conscience of those who hold authority. Even the advice on appointing scribes is sealed with a spiritual message. Malik is told to resist the temptation to appoint only those who have shown him their best side. The governor is to examine each candidate''s past record along with the impression he has left upon ordinary people, and then to appoint only those well known for trustworthiness. To do all this is called proof of your sincerity toward God. The choice of officials is thus no mere matter of administration; it is a measure of the ruler''s own truthfulness before his Lord, and the care he takes in it answers to God, not to the men who court his favor.</p>
<p>Merchants and craftsmen the Imam then describes as those who bring many benefits to society and must therefore be treated well. On the whole they are a peaceable class, and should be left in peace to go about their work. Yet the vices they are prone to must be held in check: hoarding, miserliness, and cheating. Here again the outward regulation of trade rests upon an inward discernment — one that tells apart the honest labor that enriches a community from the greed that preys upon it.</p>
<blockquote class="aside editorial"><p><strong>Editorial note (tradition-grounded).</strong> A note on the two words this chapter turns on. In the wider tradition, <em>nafs</em> derives from a root meaning &quot;to breathe&quot; — the nafs is the breathing self, and in spiritual usage it names the lower self or ego whose cravings the seeker must master; this is exactly the &quot;commanding soul,&quot; al-nafs al-ammara, the chapter warns against. The covenant is worth pausing on too. The root of <em>ahd</em> is the language of solemn promise — to bind oneself by a pledge and be charged with keeping it. In this tradition it reaches in two directions at once: the primordial compact between God and humanity (&quot;Am I not your Lord?&quot;) and the charge of office a ruler receives — the very pledge Ali lays on his governor Malik al-Ashtar. The chapter draws on both, and the single word shows why they are one duty: the pledge made and the trust to be kept.</p></blockquote>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'conscience, compassion, and the ordering of society';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The Imam now turns Malik toward those who most need justice, opening the passage with the exclamation &quot;Allah, Allah&quot; to mark its urgency. The instruction concerns those with no resources whatsoever: the destitute, the crippled, orphans, the elderly, those most in need of justice from a governor, who must be treated in such a way that &quot;God may excuse you on the day you meet Him.&quot; The governor is to provide for all of these, and for others who are in need but too proud to beg. He ought to appoint an officer charged specifically with uncovering the needs of the most destitute and attending to them. The Imam then adds words that reach the heart of the matter: &quot;This is onerous for the governors, and [the fulfillment of] all rights is onerous, but God makes it light for those who aspire to the Hereafter, who restrain their souls in patience, and trust in the truth of that which is promised them by God.&quot;</p>
<p>It is the spiritual dimension that makes an otherwise crushing ideal workable. God''s help is assured to those whose attention is not confined to this world alone, but whose aspirations reach beyond it to the Hereafter. Only when this world is seen through to the next does a fully just attitude toward this world become possible. Those whose talab, their aspiration, does not reach beyond this world are far more likely to take the easy path and neglect the destitute; for in the purely horizontal reckoning of politics, the cost to a ruler of a policy of charity toward the poor may well outweigh any benefit he can see for himself. Here an ethics bound to a purely this-worldly assessment — to pragmatic politics or to personal interest — shows its inherent flaws. But when the sense of what is owed to the poor is charged with firm faith in God''s unfailing justice and boundless compassion, and filled with longing for the Hereafter, it generates an unflinching fidelity to the needs of the destitute and renews that fidelity again and again.</p>
<p>Such an attitude goes far beyond conventional notions of charity or generosity. A person who has realized it to perfection cannot be swayed by any contrary suggestion rooted in self-serving pragmatism or individualism, still less in indifference or wilfulness. With this attitude firmly in place, the next quality the Imam names — patience — follows naturally. Given a concrete rather than merely notional foretaste of the absolute values at stake, patience can be sustained without limit; it no longer rests on personal will alone, but is upheld by the empowering grace that flows from heartfelt faith. The Imam completes the thought by noting that those with the right aspirations come, in consequence, to trust in the truth of God''s promise. If that promise — of His reward, His unfailing justice, His boundless compassion — is true, then the motive behind the governor''s effort to show the poor mercy and justice is immeasurably deepened. The task the Imam calls onerous — the duty to help the weak and helpless, from whom no political benefit can be gained — is transformed into an inescapable duty that follows directly from spiritual conviction. That conviction rests on the certainty that such generosity and compassion are not merely good, right, and proper, but that these virtues accord with the true and inalienable substance of ultimate reality. The moral implications of spiritual reality, and equally the spiritual consequences of virtue, are hidden from worldly rulers — by their covert prejudices at best, and their overt vices at worst. Such rulers are blind, too, to the divine promise of ultimate felicity, and equally blind to the warnings of retribution for the oppressors, al-zalimun. The zalim may be understood as one who defies the divine commandments concerning justice and, more fundamentally, violates the compassion that lies at the very heart of the divine nature.</p>
<p>These considerations open onto the crucial question of intention. Even when the just person''s effort to live according to justice is thwarted by the uncertainties of worldly life, two things remain: the right intentions that move him, and his awareness of the immutable divine qualities that serve as the models and principles guiding his endeavor. Together these produce a real, inward &quot;taste&quot; of the grace that furnishes the spiritual foundation of his moral life. If God''s promise is true, and one is absolutely certain of that truth, then its effect on the soul of the sincere believer transforms a personal ethic into an unconditional imperative, and a desirable value into an undeniable reality. Morality is thus deepened by spirituality, and into it: a total way of being, one that embraces the whole of existence, within which the burden of individual duty is not only immeasurably lightened but becomes a companion to one''s taste of spiritual reality. One then acts justly out of gratitude for what one has already received — the joy of spiritual certainty — and not only out of hope for something not yet attained.</p>
<p>The Quran speaks of the reward of Paradise as something already granted to the true believer. For one whose certainty of Paradise is absolute, the future has already arrived: &quot;We have given thee [the paradisal fountain of] al-Kawthar; so pray to thy Lord and sacrifice ...&quot; (Al-Kawthar: 1-2). To worship God out of certainty of the blessedness to come — a blessedness that has, in one respect, already come — is well expressed in the following saying of the Imam:</p>
<p>Indeed there is a group who worship God out of desire [for something not yet attained]; and this is the worship of the merchants. And there is a group who worship God out of fear, and this is the worship of the slaves. And there is a group who worship God out of gratitude, and this is the worship of the free.</p>
<p>The just person is further freed from the material consequences of his actions, in proportion to the uprightness and sincerity of his intentions, since in the Islamic view actions are judged not by their consequences but by their intentions. The intention is to be just — not for the sake of any earthly reward or tangible worldly result, but purely for the sake of justice itself. And this essence or principle of justice is in turn inseparable from the divine nature. For justice is one with God not simply because whatever God commands is just; rather, God commands just acts precisely because they are just, and because justice is one with His own nature: &quot;I bear witness that He is Justice and He acts justly.&quot; Justice is not one attribute among others, something merely present in the divine nature; it is that nature. Were it only in His nature, it would stand alongside His other qualities; but because it is His very essence, all His other qualities essentially flow from it, so that His mercy no less than His wrath, His creation no less than His destruction, all proceed from His justice. If God acts justly by virtue of His very nature, which is pure justice, then the spiritual person will act justly out of a desire to conform to God''s nature, and not simply out of obedience to God''s commands, and never out of desire for any earthly reward.</p>
<p>This can be seen in any single virtue. If one speaks the truth without grasping the reason for it — that truth, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحق</span>, is the very nature of the Creator, and that the goal in this world is to take on the qualities of that Creator — then truthfulness soon becomes situational and self-serving, open to compromise whenever circumstances seem to warrant it: I speak the truth when it benefits me and lie when lying benefits me. But once truth is seen as the nature of the divine, it becomes everlasting and eternal, and the consequences of speaking it cease to matter; one speaks it simply in emulation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحق</span>, the absolute truth. Virtue practiced on an ad hoc basis is no virtue at all, merely a series of pragmatic adaptations to shifting circumstances. This is why the life of Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>), when studied, reflects an unflinching adherence to the divine virtues. His was a life governed not by his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> but entirely by the divine nature of Allah, and his every celebrated action was the fruit of an irresistible attraction toward the nature of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحق</span>. His gaze was never fixed on the action itself but on the source of the principles that generated it; by fixing his attention on the beauty of the divine nature and seeking to take it on, good actions emerged from him naturally and inevitably. As the Quran says:</p>
<p>The most pious will be far removed from it [the punishment of Hell], he who giveth of his wealth to purify himself. And nobody possesseth, in the sight of such a person, any favor that might be bestowed on him as reward [for his generosity]. [His action is performed] seeking only the Countenance of his transcendent Lord, and he will be content (Al-Layl: 17-21).</p>
<p>The same principle appears in the description of the righteous, the abrar. Commentators on the Quran agree that the occasion of revelation, sabab al-nuzul, for the following verses was a time when Ali and his family went three days running without food. At the end of each day, just as they were about to break their fast, someone in greater need came and asked for what they had. The Quran relates this as follows: &quot;They feed, out of love for God, the needy, the orphan and the prisoner, saying, We feed you only for the sake of God; we desire from you neither reward nor thanks''&quot; (Al-Insan: 8-9).</p>
<p>In the very measure that a good action is performed for the sake of that absolute goodness which God is, the one performing it rests wholly content, untouched by the blame or praise of others, neither deflated when outward adversity thwarts his good actions nor inflated when they succeed. This understanding of the relation between action and intention illuminates the meaning of the Imam''s saying that &quot;their hearts are in the Gardens [of Paradise] while their bodies are at work.&quot; Here again a difference emerges between a secular ethic of justice, one devoid of all spiritual or transcendental points of reference, and a spiritual conception of justice. A secular ethic stands or falls by how far it succeeds outwardly in realizing its ideal of justice; a spiritual conception already sees its ideal fulfilled in the sincere intention to be just. Even so, the fulfillment of a spiritual ideal of justice requires an unyielding struggle to achieve it, for without that struggle the intention proves to lack sincerity. Without matching action, aspirations and intentions remain shallow and purely theoretical. To hold an intention with total sincerity requires diligent, ceaseless striving toward its realization. Thus, however formidable the obstacles, however compelling the arguments of political pragmatism, however thoroughly his strenuous efforts are undone by the unavoidable pressures of outward life, the just person never despairs. He never abandons the effort to achieve justice, precisely because achieving justice in this world is not the whole of his aim. He knows that the intention is already, in an invisible but spiritually palpable way, its own fulfillment; and he knows, with sober realism, that the outward realization of any intention depends on circumstances beyond his control and, ultimately, on the grace of God.</p>
<p>On the surface this may seem to excuse the failure to achieve justice in society, and so to offer a subterfuge by which actual injustice is legitimated through an appeal to good but thwarted intentions. The very opposite is in fact the case. Far from betraying any lack of resolve in one moved by a sacred conception of justice, such a person''s efforts are, on the contrary, as ceaseless and unyielding as the eternal and unfailing reward in which he fervently believes. Those efforts are sustained, too, by an awareness of the subtle continuity between just intentions and the divine quality of justice, and through it the divine nature itself. An inalienable sense of the sacred sustains the just person in his pursuit of justice. The argument is not that all secular or atheistic conceptions of justice are without value; only that, set beside a sacred conception, they lack those immeasurable and inexhaustible spiritual resources that sincere faith nourishes. The theories that dominate the present age bear this out. Relativism holds that knowledge, truth, and morality are not absolute but exist only in relation to culture, society, or historical context. Nihilism rejects all religious and moral principle and holds that life is essentially meaningless. Utilitarianism counts an action right only insofar as it is useful or benefits the majority, reducing good and bad to a reckoning of gain and loss with no fundamental principle beneath them. And opportunism takes whatever advantage presents itself regardless of principle, in the manner of the manipulator who flatters, performs, and pretends, saying or doing whatever it takes to get what he wants. These are human constructions, and the world arrived at them by severing practice from principle. Whatever value may emerge from them is not rooted in the divine, so that even where they coincide with the values of faith they do so only by coincidence and remain, for all their machinery, without spirit — dead corpses. &quot;With men this is impossible, but with God all things are possible,&quot; as Jesus says in the Gospel. Applied to the present context, that principle might be stated thus: when justice is conceived in isolation from spiritual principles, personal idealism lies acutely exposed to outward contingencies and is all too likely to disintegrate under the pressure of difficulty and disappointment; in a sacred conception of justice, by contrast, divine grace holds the incorruptible ideal of justice high above the frailties and upheavals to which both the human soul and human society are subject. The more one''s ideals are rooted in spiritual knowledge, the less one is prone to disappointment and despair in the face of difficulty.</p>
<p>The importance of this ideal of justice is clearly visible in the Imam''s own experience as caliph. An unbroken succession of bloody conflicts against foes who professed Islam prevented him, outwardly, from enacting many of the principles and policies that flowed from his conception of truth and justice. Yet none of this produced dejection, despair, or cynicism, nor did it lessen in the slightest his efforts to achieve justice wherever and whenever possible. The quality of his own administration shows as much, as does the steady stream of letters and counsels to his officers and governors — the letter to Malik among them. Such despair is almost inconceivable for one whose inner life is shaped by the conviction that &quot;No act is negligible if it is accompanied by piety.&quot; For such a person, despair is excluded by piety in the deepest sense of the word — the permanent awareness of divine Reality; for this degree of piety is the makhraj, the &quot;way out,&quot; from the darkness of all earthly injustice. As the Imam says, &quot;He who has pious awe toward God, for him God establishes a way out of tribulation, and a light out of darkness (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اعْلَمُوا أَنَّهُ مَنْ يَتَّقِ اللَّهَ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ مَخْرَجًا مِنَ الْفِتَنِ، وَنُورًا مِنَ الظُّلَمِ</span>).&quot;</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'justice for the poor and the sincerity of intention';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The lynchpin of the entire epistle now comes into view: prayer, the most important means by which the ruler takes into himself the sacred substance of all the virtues. At the opening of the letter the Imam began with a counsel to observe the obligatory prayers. Here, having stressed the need to perform every administrative task at its proper time, he instructs Malik to set apart the most excellent portion of his available time for what lies between him and God — for acts of worship and devotion. Malik is urged to give special attention to the obligatory prayers, the means by which he purifies his religion for God and makes his devotion to Him sincere. This is not merely an exhortation to perform the formal prayers. It must be understood above all in the light of a deeper meaning: the purifying of religion for God is the purifying of the very substance of the relationship between the soul and God, and it is the depth of that relationship which determines the quality of all human virtue. As the Imam put it in a saying already noted, &quot;Know that your every act is dependent upon your prayer&quot; — that is, every action depends upon the way one gazes upon God, who is the source of all good. What matters is not only the formal, physical, and verbal performance of the prayer, but the inward quality that accompanies it and gives it life from within. Sincerity, intensity, and frequency are essential to its efficacy. The Imam continues, &quot;Give unto God of your vital energy in your nights and your days, and perform fully that by which you draw near to God, doing so perfectly, without becoming dull or deficient, taking your body to its limits (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَالِغًا مِنْ بَدَنِكَ مَا بَلَغَ</span>).&quot;</p>
<p>The whole emphasis here falls on the word taqarrub, drawing near. Divine nearness is attained most directly through deep devotion, offered up alone, in the dead of night, with all one''s heart. Once that nearness is realized, all one''s other actions in the world take on something of the spiritual quality gained through it. In this way all action can be drawn into devotion in the wider sense, all outward deeds coming to be governed by the sincere intention to give oneself to God. If the inner life is ruled by prayer, prayer comes to penetrate the outer life as well. And so the Imam, immediately after telling Malik to set apart the best of his time for God, adds, &quot;all times [and the actions performed therein] are for God if the intention underlying them is good and if your subjects derive security as a consequence.&quot; In other words, the actions of a ruler partake of the sacred to the degree that his devotion truly reaches into the source of the sacred. There is an overflow, as it were, from the fountain of obligatory and voluntary prayer into the outward domain of action. This subtle truth is expressed in the famous hadith qudsi of the Prophet, often called the hadith of taqarrub:</p>
<blockquote><p>My slave draws near to Me through nothing I love more than that which I have made obligatory for him. My slave never ceases to draw near to Me through supererogatory acts until I love him. And when I love him, I am his hearing by which he hears, his sight by which he sees, his hand by which he grasps, and his foot by which he walks.</p></blockquote>
<p>To see and to act through God can be understood, at one level, as seeing things as they really are and then acting upon that vision — which is to say, perfect justice, the placing of each thing in its proper order. This is why the Imam repeatedly draws the reader of his sermons back to the vision of God, setting that vision as the true foundation of a life of virtue. In prayer the ego is effaced through prostration, and this submission to God through self-effacement is itself what puts all things in their right place — including, and especially, the ego. The hadith qudsi marks a distinction between the obligatory and the supererogatory acts of devotion. Both are means of drawing near, but it is chiefly through the latter that the slave comes to perceive and act through the divine. The Imam''s phrase, taking your body to its limits, carries the same weight. Strenuous effort is called for; it is far from enough to perform the prayers and devotions in a mechanical or perfunctory way. &quot;He who prays without making an effort,&quot; says the Imam, &quot;is like one who shoots arrows without a bow.&quot; As noted earlier, the sincerity (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاص</span>) demanded by the Imam''s conception of the oneness of God calls for the integration of the soul, and it is in prayer that the deepest dimensions of the soul are expressed, cultivated, and integrated. The prayer must therefore be accomplished not merely with the tongue or the body, but with all that one is.</p>
<p>Elsewhere, Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) points to the key supererogatory practice by which the spiritual station of nearness is attained. He does so in a commentary on the words of a Quranic verse, &quot;… men whom neither commerce nor trade diverts from the remembrance of God&quot; (An-Nur: 36).</p>
<blockquote><p>Truly God has made the remembrance (al-dhikr) a polish for the hearts, by which they hear after suffering from deafness, and see after being blind … There have always been slaves of God … with whom He held intimate discourse through their thoughts and spoke with them through the essence of their intellects. They diffused illumination through the awakened light in their hearing and their seeing and their hearts, calling unto the remembrance of the days of God.</p></blockquote>
<p>The importance of the dhikr — both as a principle of awareness of ultimate reality and as a concrete means of attaining that awareness — is paramount in the Imam''s perspective. By the word dhikr the Imam means both a methodic practice of invoking a divine Name and a principle of permanent awareness of God that embraces all modes of worship, meditation, and reflection. It is to be understood not only as being aware of God or remembering God, but also as an intense mode of prolonged, prayerful meditation on ultimate reality, represented both symbolically and sacramentally by one or more of the divine Names. This remembrance is not the learning of new things from scratch. It is the recollection of ultimate reality — of the immutable, unchanging principles and divine attributes that are inherent in the very substance of our intellects, by which everything can be understood, and which are ultimately rooted in the divine nature of our Creator. This aspect of remembrance is treated more fully in the chapter that follows. The relationship between the remembrance of God and the cultivation of justice is made clear in this sermon, for the Imam describes those illuminated by the dhikr:</p>
<blockquote><p>Truly there are people who belong to the dhikr; they have adopted it in place of the world, such that &quot;neither commerce nor trade&quot; distracts them from it; they spend the days of their life in it … they instruct people about justice and themselves are steadfast therein.</p></blockquote>
<p>It is those souls who truly belong to the dhikr who are not only steadfast in justice but also able to instruct others in it. Putting first things first, they can show how to place all things in their proper order. Fervent, lived spirituality is therefore not opposed to dedicated activity in the world. It infuses that activity with an ethical depth springing from direct contact with the divine — a contact articulated by prayer in general and by the dhikr in particular. Just as the sincerity of a good intention is proven by the effort to realize it, so the sincerity of prayer is proven, and made efficacious, through the sustained effort to prolong and intensify it, taking your body to its limits. What this has to do with justice is plain. If justice is grasped as a quality rooted in the Real (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>), then nearness to the Real cannot but draw one nearer to justice and to all positive qualities — spiritual, intellectual, and moral. The relationship between worship and justice must therefore be firmly established if the conception of justice is to be qualified as sacred. Seen thus, justice is both cause and consequence of worship. On the one hand, justice requires that everything be given its due, and God must accordingly be given His due, which entails — sacramentally — worship and prayer. On the other hand, justice emerges ethically as a consequence of that worship, as do all the virtues, in proportion to the nearness God grants His worshipper and in accordance with the radiant repercussions of that nearness.</p>
<p>What the Imam offers, in his stress on prayer together with its essence, the remembrance of God, is a devotional principle that illuminates and, in a sense, sublimates Plato''s philosophical position. It may be seen as making explicit in spiritual terms what remains implicit in Plato''s pedagogy. After discussing the question of justice at great length, Socrates comes to the crux of the matter: the nature of the Sovereign Good, which is of paramount concern to the ideal ruler, the guardian, whose moral and intellectual formation lies at the heart of the dialogue''s practical dimension:</p>
<blockquote><p>… the highest object of knowledge is the essential nature of the Good, from which everything that is good and right derives its value for us … I need not tell you that, without that knowledge, to know everything else, however well, would be of no value to us …</p></blockquote>
<p>Socrates declines Glaucon''s entreaties to describe the nature of this Good, offering instead to speak of its offspring. This leads him to draw an analogy between the Good and the Sun: what the Sun is to visibility, the Good is to intelligibility and being. He proceeds with the famous allegory of the cave, and then describes the educational program that would turn his prospective rulers'' attention from the world of shadows to the world of real objects — a program comprising mathematics, arithmetic, geometry, solid geometry, astronomy, harmonics, and dialectic. Having passed through all the stages of this education, &quot;when they are fifty, those who have come safely through and proved the best at all points in action and in study must be brought at last to the goal. They must lift up the eye of the soul to gaze on that which sheds light on all things; and when they have seen the Good itself, take it as a pattern for the right ordering of the state and of the individual, themselves included.&quot; Yet Plato nowhere states what this gazing actually means, nor how it is to be achieved, nor by what means — intellective, contemplative, or methodic.</p>
<p>At an important point in the Republic, Glaucon presses Socrates for a definition of the Sovereign Good: &quot;But, Socrates, what is your account of the Good? Is it knowledge, or pleasure, or something else?&quot; Socrates refuses a direct answer, saying after some further discussion, &quot;I am afraid it is beyond my powers; with the best will in the world I should only disgrace myself and be laughed at.&quot; Very much in the manner of Glaucon, Dhilib al-Yamani provocatively asked Ali whether he had seen the object of his devotion. The Imam replied, &quot;I would not be worshipping a lord whom I have not seen.&quot; Dhilib then asked, &quot;O Commander of the Faithful! How did you see Him?&quot; The Imam replied with the sentence that serves as one of the leitmotifs of this book: &quot;O Dhilib! Eyes see Him not through sight''s observation, but hearts see Him through the verities of faith.&quot;</p>
<p>The polishing of the heart effected by remembrance, by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذکر</span>, comes to mind here, and this very word evokes the essence of Plato''s approach to knowledge as recollection, anamnesis. For remembrance in this sense is not the learning of something new from scratch, but the recollection of ultimate reality — of the immutable divine principles and attributes inherent in the very substance of our intellects, by which everything else can be understood. Yet in none of his dialogues does Plato make a direct reference to the devotional, methodic, or contemplative counterpart of his educational, ethical, and intellective endeavor. There is no explicitly postulated link between remembrance of the forms or ideas that engender true knowledge and the gazing upon the Good, the contemplation of that which sheds light on all things. It is not merely knowing and remembering the forms, but coming to know the Bestower of Forms — in Islam referred to by the divine Name al-Musawwir. Whether there was any spiritual method in Plato''s teaching, and if there was, why he did not divulge it, remains a matter of speculation. One answer may be gathered from a statement in Plato''s famous Seventh Letter, where he denies that any exposition can be given of the true reality of philosophy, and points instead to the disciplined way of life and rigorous daily routine that the subject demands if philosophy in the deepest sense is to be realized. &quot;No treatise by me concerning it exists or ever will exist. It is not something that can be put into words like other branches of learning; only after long partnership in a common life devoted to this very thing does truth flash upon the soul, like a flame kindled by a leaping spark, and once it is born there, it nourishes itself thereafter.&quot; This returns to the importance of active spirituality for the practice of authentic virtue. If the Real, indefinable in its essence, is the foundation of all good, the means of assimilating the Real existentially — and not merely comprehending it mentally — cannot be set aside. Plato''s philosophical approach to the Good can be augmented and deepened by an appreciation of Ali s means of drawing near to the Real, for in the Imam''s perspective intellectual comprehension and spiritual devotion are inseparable and complementary. Integral, deep-rooted justice emerges as one of the fruits of this drawing near — just as, for Plato, gazing on that which sheds light on all things, the Good itself, results in the capacity to take the Good as a pattern for the right ordering of the state and of the individual.</p>
<p>These pages have sketched the outlines of a spiritual approach to justice as it presents itself principally in the Imam''s letter to Malik al-Ashtar. The surface of this important subject has only been skimmed, yet the Imam''s illumination of the principle of justice — addressed in the first instance to a prospective governor of Egypt in the first century of the Islamic era — is by no means outmoded. Whether one operates in an avowedly Islamic or an overtly secular environment, whether the rule be autocratic or democratic, whether political institutions be strong or weak — wherever individuals are vested with power and authority, and by that very token exposed to the temptations that power offers, the approach to justice articulated in the Imam''s ethos will not be irrelevant. This ethos presupposes no particular form of government. It engages the universal theme of moral responsibility, but does so in terms of that which transcends the level of morality, offering insights drawn from a direct vision of ultimate spiritual realities. The principles contemplated through this vision surpass the domain of politics, and for that very reason can penetrate deeply and refashion, from above, the moral substance of political consciousness. Although the Imam''s perspective is particularly pertinent to the meeting of spirituality, morality, and governance within Islamic discourse and practice, its relevance extends to every context in which the sense of the sacred plays a part in the articulation of moral sensibility and the cultivation of political responsibility.</p>
<p>The Imam''s letters and discourses are a vast and largely untapped source of inspired ideas, capable of illuminating the role of spiritual principles in the formation of ethical values and the shaping of political practice. In particular, any integral Islamic approach to politics and society must take into account the requirements and fruits of worship, understood in its widest sense. Sincere worship transforms the notion of the Absolute into a concrete reality, giving the soul an existential impetus and a spiritual resource that buttresses, stabilises, and deepens one''s moral life. What is derived from consciousness of the divine Reality is not so much a philosophy of ethics as a spiritual morality — an existential orientation that is at once the consequence of a lived spirituality and the cause of its continuous, dynamic, and ever-deepening realization of spiritual truth, the Real as such, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>. This means that the haqq, the right due to each person or group, will be given in accordance with the principle of justice — that of putting each thing in its proper place, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَضَعَ الشَّئُی حَقَّ مَحَلِّہِ</span>. Virtue, from such a perspective, is a form of ethical realism, where realism denotes an assimilation of spiritual reality, and morally correct behavior translates that realism into action on every plane — individual, social, and political.</p>
<p>Should it be objected that this perspective is hopelessly utopian — the harsh exigencies of politics and the sublime ideals of spirituality being poles apart — the reply is this. If no effort is made to comprehend the deeper dimensions of Islamic piety and spirituality, then any ostensibly Islamic discourse, whether in politics, sociology, ethics, or any other field, is reduced to an outer shell, stripped of the inner life that gives it all its meaning and, more importantly, its transformative power. For Islamic concepts to be grasped aright, they must be assimilated not merely by the mind but by the heart; failing that, they remain mere abstractions. As the Imam insists, it is the heart — the inmost point of consciousness — that sees the true nature of things. This is why the vision of God as if one could see Him lays bare the crucial relationship between worship and virtue, between spirituality and ethics, and equally between devotion to the Creator and kindness toward His creation. One cannot have the one without the other, for to conduct oneself as though one could see God is to live in the consciousness of standing constantly in the divine presence, since even if we cannot see Him, He sees us. Without this vision there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> in the ethico-spiritual sense discussed earlier, and without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span> the very integrity of the Islamic way of life is fatally undermined. It is precisely the denial and subversion of this tradition that bears ultimate responsibility for the atrocities committed by a tiny minority of Muslims in our times — people who betray the highest ideals of the Islamic ethical tradition by resorting to unbridled violence in its name, as though one might harm creation in order to please the Creator. Without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>, religion is left acutely vulnerable, turned into an ideology, driven by individual passion and open to political manipulation. It is no surprise that those most prone to terrorism in the name of Islam have emerged from the currents most hostile to the ihsani, or mystical, tradition.</p>
<p>This returns to the devotional practices that are all too often regarded as the preserve of a minority — viewed as either mystical or misguided, depending on one''s standpoint — rather than understood as a God-given means of narrowing the gap between what one aspires to and what one is: of moving from the actual toward the ideal, of making concrete what would otherwise remain abstract, of living what is only thought about, and of rendering existentially galvanising what might otherwise be merely theoretically compelling. While the principles established here are universal in their import and applicability, it would be wrong to try to de-contextualise the Imam''s perspective by dismissing, as so much unnecessary superstructure, his repeated, insistent, and uncompromising emphasis on the imperative of prayer. The principle of justice requires that all the elements of one''s life — devotion, worship, ethics, action — be bound together in a harmonious, indissoluble, and interconnected whole, in accordance with the ideal of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> means precisely this integration, the making of many into one, so that when a person''s theory and practice have themselves become one, that person has become a true bearer of that oneness. There can be no question of detaching the domain of the virtues from that of spirituality, for in the Imam''s perspective the only true virtue is one that is rooted in, sustained by, and oriented toward the divine Reality. A virtue not so rooted — even one that happens by coincidence to mirror an Islamic value — is baseless and without spirit. Cut off from its source, it becomes situational and self-serving, open to compromise the moment circumstances warrant, and ceases to be a virtue at all, dwindling into a mere series of pragmatic adaptations to shifting conditions. Justice, together with all the other virtues, can be brought to fruition only in a soul, and in a society, permeated and penetrated by spirituality — the essence of which is the principle and practice of the remembrance of God. And this remembrance, this dhikr, is not the learning of something entirely new. It is a recollection of ultimate Reality, a recollection of the immutable principles and unchanging qualities inherent in the very substance of our intellects — principles by which everything else can be understood, and which are ultimately rooted in the divine nature of our Creator. Remembrance of this kind is nothing less than the cognitive and philosophical foundation of ethical thought in Islam. In the last analysis, it is the remembrance of God that determines the quality of human virtue. It is to the spiritual realization intended and cultivated by this remembrance that the following chapter is addressed.</p>
<blockquote class="aside editorial"><p><strong>Editorial note (tradition-grounded).</strong> A note on two terms this chapter leans on. &quot;Sincerity&quot; here renders <em>ikhlas</em>, from a root meaning to be pure and free of anything mixed in; a related teaching preserved in this tradition understands it as purifying one''s intention so that worship is for God alone, with no trace of showing off blended in — which is precisely what &quot;purifies his religion for God&quot; asks of Malik. The Imam''s phrase for strenuous devotion, <em>balighan min badanika ma balagha</em>, draws on the root b-l-gh, which is about arriving — reaching a destination or driving a message home to its hearer. Read this way, &quot;taking your body to its limits&quot; names not mere exertion but effort that reaches its aim exactly; the same root titles Ali''s collected words, the <em>Nahj al-Balagha</em>, &quot;the Way of Eloquence.&quot;</p></blockquote>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'prayer, remembrance, and gazing on the good';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The subject here is the meaning of dhikruLlah, the remembrance of God, one of the most fundamental principles in Islamic spirituality. Two sources open it up. The first is the sayings of the Imam, read essentially as commentaries on the Quranic idea of dhikruLlah. The second is the doctrinal formulations that arose within the mystical traditions of Islam. To speak of Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) is to speak of Islamic spirituality in its purest, most essential form. His influence is felt wherever spirituality has flourished in Islam — in the more clearly defined disciplines of Sufism and Shii Irfan, and equally in the realms of futuwwa (spiritual chivalry), the craft guilds, music, poetry and the arts. As the gate to the city of prophetic wisdom, he leaves his mark on all the noblest expressions of the spirit of Islam.</p>
<p>The Sufi tradition in particular has a uniquely organic connection with the Imam. Sufi orders differ in every outward way — Shii or Sunni, Arab or Persian, Turk or Malay, Mauritanian or Bosnian — yet they are united in the figure of Ali. He stands at the summit of all the chains of affiliation (salasila, sing. silsila) through which the masters trace their spiritual lineage back to the Prophet. This same unifying role appears in relation to the most significant internal division within Islam, that between Sunnism and Shiism. The two traditions already share a vast common ground in essential beliefs and practices. Beyond that, they are drawn together in a direct and living way by a shared orientation: both look toward sanctity (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَايَة</span>) and gnosis (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معرفۃ</span>) as the goal of the religious life. Seyyed Hossein Nasr writes: Islamic esotericism or gnosis crystallized into the form of Sufism in the Sunni world, while it poured into the whole structure of Shiism, especially during its early period. The esoteric core of Islam thus expresses itself in Sunni Sufism and in Shii Irfan with different accents. It takes on different forms and expressions, yet its attunement to the essence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> is never fundamentally altered. According to Sayyid Haydar Amuli — one of the great figures in whom Shiism and Sufism converge — the true Shii cannot help being a Sufi, if he plumbs the mystical depths of the Imams'' teachings. The reverse holds too: the Sufi is a Shii, once he recognizes that the most important roots of Sufism lie in the Imams of the Ahl al-Bayt. These Imams were the first poles (aqtab, sing. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُطْبٌ</span>) of the spiritual tradition, and through them the mystical blessings of the Prophet were transmitted within an initiatic setting. For Amuli, the differences of outward form between the two traditions dissolve in the fire of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَايَة</span>, sanctity — the true substance and goal of both alike. And for both, Ali is the wali Allah, the friend and saint of God, beyond all others.</p>
<p>The way Ali is regarded within the Sufi tradition is captured representatively by Ali Hujwiri, author of the first manual of Sufism written in Persian, the Kashf al-mahjub: Among them [the Sufis] is the son of Muhammad''s uncle, he who drowned in the ocean of trying affliction, and was consumed in the fire of loving passion (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَرِيقِ بَحْرِ بَلَاءٍ وَحَرِيقِ نَارِ وَلَاءٍ</span>), the leader of the saints and the pure ones (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُقْتَدَایِ أَوْلِيَاءِ وَالْأَصْفِيَاءِ</span>). In this Path he holds a place of tremendous honor and elevated degree. He had complete mastery over the precise expression of the principles of the supernal realities, such that Junayd said of him, &quot;Ali is our master as regards principles and affliction (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَيْخُنَا فِي الْأُصُولِ وَالْبَلَاءِ</span>).&quot;... That is, Ali is the Imam of this Path in respect of both theoretical knowledge and practical application (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَنْدَرْ عِلْمِ وَ مُعَامَلَاتِ اِمَامِ اِیْن طَرِیْقَتْ</span>), for the adepts of Sufism call the knowledge of this Path its &quot;principles&quot;, while its practice is summed up in the endurance of affliction.</p>
<p>The phrase endurance of affliction recalls the Imam''s description of the sages as those who befriend that by which the ignorant are estranged. One reading of that elliptical phrase is this: among the things the sages befriend and the ignorant flee is prayer — and more precisely, the act of pure concentration on the Absolute, which shuts out every other thought. Such concentration is a kind of death, for the ego and all its preoccupations are, as it were, sacrificed before the divine Reality on which the mind is fixed alone. The remembrance of God can be understood in two ways at once: as the fulfillment of that concentration, and as the path leading to the summit of spiritual realization — self-effacement before the sole reality of God.</p>
<p>The body of Ali s teachings sheds remarkable light on the meaning, the principle and the practice of dhikruLlah — a theme of central importance in both the Quran and the Sunna of the Prophet. The Imam''s sayings form a valuable commentary on those Quranic verses that speak of dhikr. And because the dhikr stands at the center of Sufi practice and doctrine, these sayings help explain why the Sufis regard the Imam as the first spiritual Pole (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُطْبٌ</span>) of Sufism after the Prophet himself. The close bond between remembrance and spiritual realization is illumined from two directions: by the Imam''s own sayings, and by the expositions of the Sufi tradition — above all those of the school of Ibn al-Arabi, known in the tradition as al-Shaykh al-akbar, the greatest Master, for his towering influence on later Sufi thought. These expositions can be read as doctrinal echoes of the Imam''s dense, often elliptical pronouncements. Taken together, the Imam''s sayings and the Sufis'' elaborations bring key aspects of Islamic spirituality into sharp focus.</p>
<p>The term dhikr deserves first consideration, along with the reason for its prominence in Islamic devotional practice. It is an extremely rich word, and no single English term can capture its full range. It carries the senses of remembrance, recollection, invocation, repetition, incantation and recitation; and also of memory, mention, telling, relating a story, praise, glorification, celebration, fame, eminence, honor, exhortation and warning; and it can denote the Quran itself, or any book of revelation or religion. The definition that comes closest to the spiritual principle the word expresses is the one given by al-Raghib al-Isfahani in his Quranic dictionary. He writes that dhikr is that by means of which man preserves that knowledge (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معرفۃ</span>) which he has attained, so it is akin to memory (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِفْظ</span>), except that memory is spoken of in respect of its function of acquisition (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْرَاز</span>), whereas dhikr is spoken of in respect of its function of rendering present (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْتِحْضَار</span>). Dhikr is also used to refer to the presence of a thing in one''s consciousness (lit. &quot;heart&quot;) or in one''s speech. Thus, it is said that there are two types of dhikr: dhikr of the heart and dhikr of the tongue, and each comprises two further kinds: a dhikr which follows forgetfulness, and a dhikr which does not follow forgetfulness but which, on the contrary, expresses a continuous remembering.</p>
<p>Much in this definition resonates with the Imam''s own perspective. The key point is this: when the thing remembered, or rendered present to consciousness, is God, the importance of dhikruLlah as a principle becomes self-evident. DhikruLlah is to be understood, first and foremost, as a principle of awareness — a deep taking-in of the divine presence. But it must also be understood as an action, a practice or set of practices, and so as a spiritual discipline. DhikruLlah can therefore be grasped both as the end and as a means to that end: both as spiritual awareness, and as the methodic practice that cultivates that awareness. It is the Sufi tradition that works out most fully this relationship between the practice of the dhikr and the state of consciousness it intends. There the dhikr is presented in two lights at once: as a contemplative discipline centered on the methodic invocation or repetition of a Name of God, and as the cognitive consequence and mystical fruit of that discipline.</p>
<p>The dhikr is thus the supreme spiritual method of the Sufis. It is a discipline that helps to realize within the heart what is already present to the mind, turning an abstract, notional grasp of certain ideas into a concrete perception of spiritual realities. Insofar as dhikruLlah means the permanent awareness of the divine Reality, it can be presented as the very essence of religion itself — that which once again binds'' (re-ligare) the human being to God, and so the reason for being of all religious rites and practices. If prayer is the core of religious practice, dhikruLlah is, as the Quran states simply, akbar — greater or greatest:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-''Ankabut: 45</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱتْلُ مَآ أُوحِىَ إِلَيْكَ مِنَ ٱلْكِتَبِ وَأَقِمِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ ۖ إِنَّ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ تَنْهَىٰ عَنِ ٱلْفَحْشَآءِ وَٱلْمُنكَرِ ۗ وَلَذِكْرُ ٱللَّهِ أَكْبَرُ ۗ وَٱللَّهُ يَعْلَمُ مَا تَصْنَعُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">Truly, prayer keepeth [one] away from lewdness and iniquity, and the remembrance of God is greater (Al-Ankabut: 45).</p></blockquote>
<p>Many sayings of the Prophet attest to the pre-eminence of the remembrance of God. It is related that the Prophet asked his companions: Shall I not tell you about the best and purest of your works for your Lord, and the most exalted of them in your ranks, and the work that is better for you than giving silver and gold, and better for you than encountering your enemy, with you striking their necks and them striking your necks? Those he addressed replied: What is that, O emissary of God? He said, The perpetual invocation of God, exalted and glorious (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرُ اللَّهِ عَزَّ وَجَلَّ دَائِمًا</span>).</p>
<p>Ali affirms the same: Perpetuate the dhikr, for truly it illumines the heart, and it is the most excellent form of worship (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هُوَ أَفْضَلُ الْعِبَادَةِ</span>). The Shii tradition places much emphasis on the practice of dhikr, and records many sayings of the early Imams on the virtues of invoking the Names of God and various sacred phrases. Imam Jafar al-Sadiq described his followers specifically in these terms: Our Shia are those who, when alone (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا خَلَوْا</span>), invoke and remember God much. The khalwa — the spiritual retreat in which one seeks to be alone with God — is described by Allama Tabatabai, one of the most important masters of Shii gnosis in the twentieth century: As to the particular spiritual retreat (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْوَتِ خَاصٍّ</span>) it is distancing oneself from people... it is especially required during certain types of verbal invocations (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَذْكَارٌ كَلَامِيَّةٌ</span>)... and is considered essential by the masters of the path.</p>
<p>Within the Sufi tradition, the following statement on the dhikr by Shaykh Ahmad al-Alawi may be taken as entirely representative:</p>
<p>Remembrance is the mightiest rule of the religion ... The law was not enjoined upon us, neither were the rites of worship ordained but for the sake of establishing the remembrance of God ... In a word, our performance of the rites of worship is considered strong or weak according to the degree of our remembrance while performing them.</p>
<p>Al-Ghazali raises the question of why the dhikr should be the most meritorious practice, when it is so simple to perform compared with all the other acts of worship, which demand so much effort. His answer is that this superiority can be fully understood only through the science of unveiling (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمُ الْمُكَاشَفَةِ</span>). What can be said from the standpoint of the practical religious sciences (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمُ الْمُعَامَلَةِ</span>) is that the most efficacious dhikr is the one performed continuously with the presence of the heart (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُضُورُ الْقَلْبِ</span>): This is the aim of the dhikr, and it is this which takes precedence over all acts of worship; nay, it is through this that the other acts of worship are dignified; this is the ultimate fruit of the practical acts of worship. The principle of dhikruLlah thus reaches to the highest levels of religious practice, contemplative discipline and spiritual realization, and so to the very heart of Islamic spirituality. For all their variety across time and place, and all their differing perspectives and emphases, the Sufis are unanimous that dhikruLlah is central to their way. And for all their differences of name, orientation, school of thought or mode of practice, the Sufi orders are united in the pivotal figure of Ali, the one who connects every later master of the spiritual path to the Prophet himself. This meeting of the Imam and the dhikruLlah is far from accidental. The Imam explained the meaning of the dhikr and himself initiated disciples into its methodic practice; both belong to his role as spiritual guide — the wali Allah, or the Pole (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُطْبٌ</span>) of his age — and so lie at the center of his esoteric function within the spiritual tradition of Islam, in both its Sunni and Shii forms.</p>
<p>As for this esoteric function, very little firm historical information survives about the Imam''s initiatic role — that is, his actual initiation of disciples into the practice of invocation, known in the tradition as talqin. Just as little survives about his own initiation into the esoteric practice by the Prophet. This is hardly surprising, since the practice is, precisely, esoteric: it is not imposed on all as an obligatory rite. Rather, within both Sufism and Shii Irfan, the invocation is bestowed on those with a particular vocation, those who have received a calling to practice something beyond the obligatory rites binding on everyone. Even so, some historical accounts offer prima facie evidence of the Imam''s role in transmitting the dhikr. The people described in the earliest sources as devoted to the dhikr as a particular practice were known as close associates, if not disciples, of the Imam. His role in this initiatic domain is stressed in the orally transmitted sources of the Sufi way, and set down in several written sources too. These record reports of the dhikr passing from the Prophet to the Imam, and from him to several other disciples, who went on to initiate others into what later came to be called the way of tasawwuf.</p>
<p>Even though little historically sound material survives on the earliest use and transmission of dhikruLlah, there remains an extremely important body of literary evidence: the sayings recorded in the Nahj al-balagha and the Ghurar al-hikam, together with the supplications attributed to the Imam. Taken together, these sayings form a coherent corpus, a distinct body of doctrine. It offers a rich source of philosophical reflection, spiritual meditation and metaphysical insight into the nature and meaning of this practice, which holds so central a place in the mystical tradition of Islam.</p>
<p>These sayings shed light not only on the meaning of the practice of dhikr, but also on the philosophy and the theory of knowledge that underlie it; and they give precious hints of the fruits of its practice, its fulfillment in spiritual realization. On one side, the Imam''s sayings are an important source of commentary on the Quranic verses that treat this theme. On the other, they clearly foreshadow the more elaborate doctrines of the dhikr found in the later Sufi and Irfani traditions. They thus serve as a bridge, joining what came before the Imam — prophetic revelation — to what came after him, the work of mystical elaboration. The texts and manuals of the mystical tradition seem to make explicit what was, more often than not, implicit in the Imam''s sayings. The mystics drew out esoteric truths that the Imam had conveyed in sayings that were concise, often paradoxical and elliptical, yet always intellectually challenging and spiritually fruitful. Reflection on these sayings — read in the light of the Quran and the Sunna they illuminate, and of the later Sufi doctrine they anticipate — can bring one closer to a proper understanding of the role of dhikruLlah in the practical, operative and visionary dimensions of Islamic spirituality.</p>
<p>Reflection on those sayings finds its most fitting starting point in the Imam''s comment on the dhikr in sermon 213 of the Nahj al-balagha. There the Imam first quotes words from verse 37 of Sura 24 of the Quran, entitled al-Nur (The Light), where the invocation of God''s Name is mentioned, and then comments on them. The words are these: men whom neither trade nor merchandise diverts from the remembrance of God. This fragment belongs to the preceding verse of light, the ayat al-nur so rich in mystical symbolism, and that bond gives the Imam''s comment a considerably deeper resonance. The Imam plainly knew that reciting the words from verse 37 would call the earlier verses to his listeners'' minds; by choosing not to quote the verse of light in full, he only sharpened the effect of the ellipsis — for such hints, implications, evocations and allusions are characteristic of Arabic balagha. His words are therefore best read alongside the verse of light in full:</p>
<p>God is the light of the heavens and the earth. A similitude of His light is a niche wherein is a lamp; the lamp is enclosed in a glass; the glass is as it were a shining star. [The lamp] is lit [by the oil of] a blessed olive tree, neither of the East nor of the West. The oil well-nigh shineth forth, though fire touch it not. Light upon light! God guideth to His light whom He will; and God striketh similitudes for mankind; and God knoweth all things (An-Nur: 35).</p>
<p>After this verse come the following two:</p>
<p>In houses which God has allowed to be elevated, and that His Name be remembered therein, He is glorified in the mornings and in the evenings by men whom neither trade nor merchandise diverteth from the remembrance of God, and from establishing the prayer, and from the bestowing of alms ... (An-Nur: 36-7).</p>
<p>After reciting the words men whom neither trade nor merchandise diverteth from the remembrance of God, the Imam begins his sermon:</p>
<p>Truly, God has made the remembrance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الذِّكْر</span>) a polish for the hearts, by which they hear after being deaf, and see after being blind and yield after being resistant ... There have always been slaves of God ... with whom He held intimate discourse through their thoughts and spoke with them through the essence of their intellects. They diffused illumination through the awakened light in their hearing and their seeing and their hearts, calling unto the remembrance of the days of God ... Indeed, there is a special group (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهْلٌ</span>) who belong to the dhikr; they have adopted it in place of the world, such that neither trade nor merchandise distracts them from it. They spend the days of their life in it ... It is as though they had left this world for the Hereafter, and they are there, witnessing what is beyond this world ...</p>
<p>The weight of this passage lies in the Imam''s description of the dhikr as a polish for hearts'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِلَاءً لِلْقُلُوبِ</span>), set beside his reference to the awakened light in the hearing, sight and hearts of those who have applied that polish. The link between the illumination of the heart and the verse of light is clear. The remembrance of God discloses the latent light of the heart, and the light within then shines together with the light from on high: light upon light. Once the heart is illumined, every other faculty shares in the luminosity the invocation generates, and so there is, again, further light upon light. The very word given for polish, jila, can itself be read as an implicit commentary on the light verse, for it derives from the verb jala, whose primary meaning is to become clear — exposed to view, disclosed, brought to light. The Quran speaks of the night when it enshroudeth in darkness, and the day when it shineth forth in brightness (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا تَجَلَّى</span>) (Al-Layl: 1-2), and likewise: By the day, as it bringeth to light [the sun] (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا جَلَّىٰهَا</span>) (Ash-Shams: 3). The consciousness that lies hidden within the heart is brought to light through the polishing worked by the remembrance of God.</p>
<p>The concern here is to clarify the organ of consciousness, the spiritual heart — both so that it may function as a mirror reflecting truths from above, and so that its own hidden depths are revealed, disclosing truths from within: those concealed contents of consciousness the Imam calls the buried treasures of the intellect (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَفَائِنُ الْعُقُولِ</span>). The dhikr is presented as a means of purifying this spiritual mode of perception, the heart, which cannot simply be identified with the rational faculty, still less with the organs of sensory perception, as discussed in Chapter 1.</p>
<p>The Quran points to the deeper significance of the heart and its distinctive mode of vision: Have they not traveled in the land, that they might have hearts with which to comprehend, and ears with which to hear? Indeed, it is not the eyes that are blind, but blind are the hearts that are in the breasts'' (Al-Hajj: 46). The outward sight of the eye reaches only the surface of things, whereas the heart''s proper vision is an insight that pierces beneath that surface to the reality concealed there. And again: They have hearts with which they understand not, eyes with which they see not, ears with which they hear not. They are like cattle, nay, even further astray. They are the heedless (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْغَافِلُونَ</span>) (Al-Araf: 179). That closing reference to the heedless'', the ghafilun, returns to the dhikr, for the ghafil is the very opposite of the dhakir, the rememberer; and the verse immediately following is one of the most important methodic foundations of the mystical practice of dhikr: Unto God belong the most beautiful Names, so call Him by them. And leave behind those who desecrate His Names; they will be requited for what they do (Al-Araf: 180). The bond between remembrance and the heart is affirmed plainly in these verses: ... Those who believe and whose hearts are at peace in the remembrance of God, is it not in the remembrance of God that hearts are at peace? (Ar-Ra''d: 28); Truly, therein is a reminder (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرَى</span>) for him who hath a heart (Qaf: 37); Those are true believers whose hearts quake with awe when God is invoked (Al-Anfal: 2); Is he whose breast God hath opened up to submission (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِسْلَام</span>), such that he followeth a light from his Lord [like he who remains closed to submission and in darkness]? So woe be to those whose hearts are hardened against the remembrance of God, they are in plain error (Az-Zumar: 22).</p>
<p>By contrast, the verse that follows speaks of hearts that soften to dhikruLlah: God hath revealed the most beautiful saying, a Book [containing] similitudes and paired [threats and promises] at which the skins of those who fear their Lord quiver; then their skins and their hearts soften to the remembrance of God (Az-Zumar: 23).</p>
<p>The Imam''s comment on verse 37 of the Light chapter should therefore be understood as shedding light on every Quranic verse that refers to the understanding, or spiritual vision, to which the heart has access in principle, and that names dhikruLlah as the means of turning this potentiality into actuality. The heart needs to be polished by the invocation of God. The Imam''s comment echoes and reinforces a saying of the Prophet often quoted in Sufi circles: For everything there is a polish (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صِقَالَةٌ</span>), and the polish of the hearts is the dhikruLlah.</p>
<p>What is being referred to here is an actual discipline — a practice, an action, or a regular series of actions — though this in no way excludes the meaning of dhikr as awareness, as a state of recollectedness in the divine presence, a disposition of soul shaped by the consciousness that if you cannot see Him, He sees you. The polishing in question can be understood in two ways at once: as the work of purifying thought through reflection and meditation, and as a regular methodic practice — whether that takes the form of glorification (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَسْبِيح</span>), magnification (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَمْجِيدٌ</span>), praise (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحْمِيدٌ</span>), sanctification (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْدِيس</span>), the declaration of divine unity (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهْلِيلٌ</span>), or the dhikr in the strict sense, the invocation of God''s Names, by which the rust of forgetfulness is removed. Ali also refers in his comment to a particular group who belong to the dhikr, calling them an ahl. The Prophet likewise spoke of the ahl <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الذِّكْر</span>: God has angels who rove the pathways, seeking out the ahl <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الذِّكْر</span>, and when they find a group of people (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَوْمٌ</span>) invoking God, they call out to one another, &quot;Come to that which you desire!&quot;</p>
<p>Later in time, one of the greatest members of the qawm — one of the names by which the Sufis call themselves — gave poetic expression to the image of the heart polished by the dhikr. It is the custom of kings, Rumi tells in the Mathnawi, to place the Sufis right before them: for they are a mirror for the soul, and better than a mirror — breasts polished by invocation and meditation, such that the mirror of the heart receives the pure, virginal image.</p>
<p>Another aspect of the heart''s polishing deserves reflection: the nature of the vision experienced by the heart that the dhikr purifies and faith vivifies. In the Imam''s perspective this vision is divinely inspired; it is nothing less than the spiritual vision of God already referred to: Eyes see Him not through sight''s observation, but hearts see Him through the verities of faith.</p>
<p>In another saying, the Imam calls pure hearts'' the places'' from which God can be seen: so whoever purifies his heart sees God. The connection between the purification of the heart and the remembrance of God is confirmed in this saying: Where are they whose actions are accomplished purely for God, and who purify their hearts [so that they become] places for the remembrance of God?</p>
<p>Taken together, these sayings of the Imam on the impact of the dhikr upon the heart can be read as a commentary on the important hadith qudsi cited in Chapter 2: My slave never ceases to draw near to Me through supererogatory acts (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَوَافِلُ</span>) until I love him. And when I love him, I am his hearing by which he hears, his sight by which he sees, his hand by which he grasps, and his foot by which he walks. The Sufis regard this divine utterance as defining a foundational principle of their metaphysical doctrine, while at the same time pointing to the goal of their mystical practice.</p>
<p>A mutual confirmation stands between the Imam''s saying and the hadith qudsi. Through the dhikr — the supererogatory act par excellence — the individual''s perceptions and actions are mysteriously assimilated to the divine Reality, and the heart thereby attains spiritual vision. But the heart, as the center of consciousness, can then no longer be regarded in its purely relative, human dimension. Its inmost essence, or secret (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِرٌّ</span>), belongs in truth to the divine, and this alone can see as God sees and, ultimately, see God Himself. As the Sufi master Abu Talib al-Makki wrote, None sees Him to whom nothing is similar but Him to whom nothing is similar. If God does indeed enter into the human acts of perception proper to the outward senses, then all the more must the supreme act of spiritual awareness, the vision of God, be in reality not a vision of God by man but a vision of God by God, effected through the individual''s heart rather than by it. The Imam says, Know God through God (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِعْرِفُوا اللهَ بِاللهِ</span>). In the final analysis, then, it is God who both sees'' and is seen. On a typical Sufi reading of the Quranic verse by a witnesser and that which is witnessed (Al-Buruj: 3), it is God Himself who is both the witnesser (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَاهِدٌ</span>) and the witnessed (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَشْهُود</span>). Al-Kashani, for example, writes in his esoteric exegesis — until quite recently wrongly attributed to Ibn al-Arabi — that the human witness is effaced in his witnessing, so that it is God who witnesses Himself through the individual whose separative consciousness is extinguished, such that He is the identity of the witness, there being no difference between the two except in terms of notional expression.</p>
<p>This anticipates the final section, where the ultimate fruits of invocation will be explored in terms of spiritual realization through self-effacement. For now the focus remains on a more relative aspect of the heart''s vision of God: that by which the reality of God is made clear and present to the heart through the verities of faith. The agent here is the inner dimension of human consciousness, and the object perceived is the divine Reality. In other words, profound faith reveals the objective presence of God — a presence that is everywhere and cannot be taken away, and that constitutes the ontological reality of all existing things. As the Imam''s famous Dua Kumayl puts it, noted earlier: By Thy tremendousness (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَظَمَتِكَ</span>) which has filled all things ... by Thy Names which have filled the foundations of all things, by Thy knowledge which encompasses all things, by the light of Thy face, through which all things are illumined ... (emphasis added).</p>
<p>Nothing can exist without the foundation — the ontological infrastructure, as it were — that the Name, or combination of Names, of God supplies for it. What constitutes the true being and the positive qualities of each manifested thing is therefore, in the end, a divine Name or combination of Names. To see things'' correctly means seeing the divine attributes manifested by and through them, and so perceiving them as existential reflections of principial realities — as so many tajalliyyat, or theophanies, self-disclosures of the divine Reality. The heart purified by invocation comes to see God everywhere.</p>
<p>This vision of the divine in all things rests on two principles the Quran affirms: the first concerns the divine object, that is, the objective, metaphysical nature of reality; the second concerns the human subject and the mystical assimilation of that reality. On the one hand, nothing that exists — not even the most outward aspects of material existence — can be excluded from the divine Reality, for He is the First and the Last, and the Outward and the Inward (Al-Hadid: 3), and Wherever ye turn there is the Face of God (Al-Baqarah: 115). On the other hand, We shall show them Our signs on the horizons and in their own souls, until it be clear to them that He is the Real (Fussilat: 53). Everything around us thus provides a sign pointing back to its Creator.</p>
<p>The heart polished and purified by the dhikr can decipher these signs and so see God everywhere and in everything; yet the possibility of such a vision in no way reduces the divine Reality to the level of sensible phenomena. As noted earlier, the first sermon of the Nahj al-balagha proclaims that God is with every thing, but not through association; and other than every thing, but not through separation. This withness'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعِيَّة</span>) of God implies no relativization of God, no association or conjoining (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُقَارَنَةٌ</span>) of the divine Reality with created things, and no diminution of divine transcendence. The interplay of ideas here can be read as a commentary both on the Quranic verses that proclaim divine immanence — the presence of God to and within all things — and on those that stress divine transcendence, surpassing and incomparable to all things. God may be absolutely close to us, yet we are infinitely far from Him. On the one hand, We are closer to man than the neck-artery (Qaf: 16) — He is as near to us as our own jugular vein — and on the other, ... there is none comparable unto Him (Al-Ikhlas: 4).</p>
<p>Nothing that exists can be wholly separate from the all-encompassing reality of God, and yet that reality has no common measure with anything that exists. His oneness both includes and excludes all things. To affirm God''s immanence within the world — His being with every thing — therefore takes nothing away from His transcendence; and to affirm His transcendence above the world — His being other than every thing — does not make Him absent from it. The oneness of God carries all of this, and at the same time it denies any merely numerical oneness, as the first chapter briefly discussed. In the Imam''s view, God is One not in the sense of a countable one, a single unit among other units, but in the sense that His oneness admits no other. In his own words, That which has no second does not enter into the category of numbers.</p>
<p>This can be read as an esoteric commentary on the Quranic verse, Say: He, God is One (Al-Ikhlas: 1). The opposite of this divine oneness — the multiplicity of gods implied in the error of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> — is refined by this conception and turned into a statement about being rather than about theology alone. The act of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, of associating partners'' with God, is thereby raised to a higher register, and shown to imply not simply the affirmation of false deities but the attribution to God of a merely numerical oneness. To reduce His oneness to number is to confine it within the wholly relative category of determinate entities. Each such entity is a unit, and on this reckoning God would be no more than one among other similar beings''. But the Imam insists that God has no second: there is no other being in being but Him. To call Him one in the ordinary sense is itself imprecise, for it implies that a second could follow in sequence. He is better described as unique. No entity or unit that appears to exist can, in reality, be compared with His being, which is one in an infinitely all-encompassing sense and unique in an absolutely exclusive sense — a oneness that includes all reality and excludes all otherness. This radical idea — of a oneness that absolutely excludes any otherness'' in Being — means that created entities are so many false gods, precisely to the extent that their apparent oneness'' in existence is set beside God''s true oneness, or rather beside God''s one-and-onliness''.</p>
<p>In another sermon, Ali turns to divine immanence, focusing this time on the kind of awareness the human intellect can have of the divine Reality — which returns to the question of what vision of God is possible for a human being:</p>
<p>&quot;Praise be to God who is present in the hidden inwardness of things, and whose being is indicated by the signs of manifest things. The eye of the onlooker cannot behold Him, so the eye of one who does not see Him cannot deny His reality, nor can the heart of one who affirms His reality see Him. He is utmost in elevation, so no thing is more elevated than He; but He is also close in His nearness, so no thing is closer than He ... God has not made the intellects capable of defining His qualities, but He has also not veiled the intellects from essential knowledge of Him.&quot;</p>
<p>This passage says two things. On the one hand, human intellects cannot define, still less know, the divine attributes; on the other, essential knowledge of Him is attainable. The apparent contradiction resolves in another key dimension of dhikr — that of recollection, remembrance, recognition. What takes place is a re-collecting of fragments of a knowledge that already exists but has been forgotten. One recognizes what one has always known, and it is precisely for this reason that one can know it again. This does not mean the knowledge is made of parts to be reassembled, which would be empirical knowledge. The remembrance here is more like an awakening, an illumination that dawns as the clouds of inborn forgetfulness are dispelled.</p>
<p>This path to knowledge through remembrance recurs throughout the Imam''s sayings. In Sermon 1 of the Nahj, for instance, comes the phrase, &quot;Foremost in religion is knowledge of Him (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَّلُ الدِّينِ مَعْرِفَتُهُ</span>).&quot; The word awwal literally means the first, and can be understood as the starting-point, the foundation, and so the foremost element of religion. The word marifa means not merely knowledge but also carries the sense of recognition. The implication is plain: the basis of all religious orientation is man''s pre-existing knowledge of the divine Reality. One re-cognizes what one has always known at the deepest level of consciousness; one ac-knowledges what one already knows but had seemingly forgotten.</p>
<p>Further on in the same sermon, God sent to mankind &quot;His messengers, dispatching prophets in succession, in order to claim from them [His creatures] the fulfillment of the covenant of His creation; to remind them of His forgotten graces; to remonstrate with them through communication [of His revelation]; to unearth for them the buried treasures of the intellects ...&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَفَائِنُ الْعُقُولِ</span>).</p>
<p>The buried treasures'' can be identified with the knowledge of divine realities ingrained in man''s deepest nature — the realities to which humanity was made to bear witness at the dawn of creation: &quot;And when thy Lord brought forth from the children of Adam, from their loins, their seed, and made them testify of themselves, Am I not your Lord? They said, Yea, we testify. [This was] lest ye say on the Day of Resurrection, Truly, of this we were unaware&quot; (Al-Araf: 172).</p>
<p>These divine realities can also be understood by reference to the divine spirit breathed into man at the creation of the human soul: &quot;Then He fashioned [man] and breathed into him of His Spirit&quot; (As-Sajdah: 9); to the &quot;names&quot; taught by God to Adam: &quot;And He taught Adam the names, all of them ...&quot; (Al-Baqarah: 31); and to the fitra, the primordial nature of man, patterned upon the divine nature: &quot;... the nature of God (fitrat Allah), that according to which He created man&quot; (Ar-Rum: 30). The primordial nature of the human soul can thus be seen to hold the very element — knowledge of God — to which all of humanity is made to bear witness in the pact (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْمِيثَقَ</span>) named in the verse above. Imam Muhammad al-Baqir makes this identity between the fitra and the knowledge of God explicit, in a saying quoted by al-Khui as a commentary on that sentence of the first sermon. Al-Baqir was asked about the meaning of the Quranic words, &quot;the nature of God, that according to which He created man.&quot; He answered, &quot;He created them [such that their primordial nature was] in conformity with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, at the covenant regarding knowledge of Him, [when they testified] that He is indeed their Lord.&quot;</p>
<p>Knowledge of God, then, essentially defines human nature; and for this reason the human intellect cannot be veiled, as the Imam said, from &quot;essential knowledge of God.&quot; Yet although this knowledge is ingrained in human nature, forgetfulness (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَةٌ</span>) has become man''s second nature. In Quranic terms, the mere passage of time smothers the heart and so hardens it: &quot;Hath not the moment come for the believers, that their hearts be humbled to the remembrance of God, and to what hath been revealed of the truth; and that they not become as those who were given the Book aforetime. Then the term was prolonged for them, so their hearts were hardened; and many of them are evil-doers&quot; (Al-Hadid: 16).</p>
<p>The verse speaks both of this hardening of the heart over time — which can be identified with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَةٌ</span> of second nature — and of its opposite: the heart''s being present, here and now, to the dhikruLlah. To cure forgetfulness, more is needed than a simple factual reminder, some item of information, some piece of formal, conceptual, reasoned knowledge. No amount of empirical data, arriving only from without, can bring man to knowledge of God. Rather it is integral remembrance — a spiritual awareness that wells up from within and crystallizes on contact with revelation from without — that makes the innate knowledge of God present once again to heart and mind, the knowledge buried deep in the intellect. There is no common measure between this consciousness of God and ordinary empirical knowledge of things. The Imam expresses the gulf between the two kinds of knowledge in sayings such as, &quot;Praise be to God who is well-known (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْرُوف</span>) without being seen.&quot; A further wording of the same principle sharpens the paradox: &quot;By things their Creator manifests Himself to the intellects; and by things He is guarded from the sight of the eyes.&quot; This opens onto a difficult theme in the Imam''s teaching: the nature of the veil formed by created things, and how it is to be understood in the light of the infinite and inalienable reality of God. On one hand, all created things are a veil, in that they keep the divine Essence from being seen by outward vision. On the other, all things cannot but manifest the divine, bearing witness to its creativity and revealing something of its reality — plain to the intellect in its normative state, and dazzlingly evident to the heart illumined and purified by the remembrance of God.</p>
<p>The following aphorisms of Ibn Ataillah — one of the great masters of Sufi gnosis, or marifa — are wholly to the point here. They read as a series of reflections on the paradox the Imam expressed so tersely. Ibn Ataillah writes: &quot;That which shows you the existence of His omnipotence is that He veiled you from Himself by that which has no existence alongside of Him.&quot; In one respect God is &quot;guarded from the eyes,&quot; in the Imam''s words, and so veiled by &quot;things&quot;; but since God alone truly &quot;is,&quot; these &quot;things&quot; do not really exist, and so God is veiled by &quot;nothing.&quot; Through their very &quot;appearance&quot; — both their coming into existence and their &quot;apparent&quot; existence — it is &quot;by things&quot; that &quot;their Creator manifests Himself.&quot; The mystery of this self-manifestation of God by and through all things is pressed to the full by the following rhetorical questions of Ibn Ataillah:</p>
<p>How can it be conceived that something veils Him, since He is the one who manifests everything?</p>
<p>How can it be conceived that something veils Him, since were it not for Him the existence of everything would not be manifest?</p>
<p>It is a marvel how Being has been manifested in non-being, and how the contingent has been established alongside of Him who possesses the attribute of Eternity!</p>
<p>The import of all these sayings can be grasped within a paradox the Imam expressed tersely: &quot;The veils do not veil Him (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا تَحْجُبُهُ الْحُجُبُ</span>).&quot; The veils cannot help revealing what they are meant to conceal. They are not merely transparent to the reality they cannot hide; in and of themselves they reveal it rather than conceal it. This recalls the Imam''s famous saying, so often quoted by the Sufis: &quot;Were the veil to be removed, I would not increase in certitude&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لو کشف الغطاء ما ازدت یقینا</span>). The veils of creation reveal in the end what they at first conceal, yet only the eye of the heart, purified through the dhikr, perceives this revelation — the heart that now &quot;sees after being blind.&quot; The Imam continues, deepening the paradox further:</p>
<p>&quot;The veil between Him and His creatures is His very creation of them. [This veil is] for the sake of His prevention of that which is possible in their essences, and for the sake of making possible that which is prevented in regard to Him; and to distinguish the Creator from the created, and the limit from the limited, and the Lord from the vassal.&quot;</p>
<p>All of creation, every &quot;veil&quot; it forms, is thus nothing other than the divine dimension of al-Zahir: the Apparent, the Clear, the Evident, the Manifest. Yet creation is al-Zahir not in its own right, but by virtue of the divine reality of which it is a projection, a form of tajalli. God, as al-Zahir, projects something of His &quot;hidden treasure&quot; within the creation and as the creation. The world manifests the &quot;apparent&quot; aspect of God — that which &quot;appears&quot; from Him, that which He can manifest or bring into being — while still concealing the mystery of God: that which remains &quot;hidden,&quot; al-Batin, the supra-manifest source of being, the uncreated essence. The veil of creation is therefore nothing other than the self-revelation of God. Yet this holds only by virtue of what pierces the veil of its own apparent otherness — that divine creativity which creation cannot in fact conceal, even though in principle it has the character of a veil.</p>
<p>The veil formed by creation prevents what the essences of creatures make possible. What the essences make possible is the realization of inmost identity with the divine Reality — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> at its highest level — a possibility that follows from the fact that this same divine Reality is the ground of being of &quot;their essences.&quot; It is the divine Names, qualities, and attributes that &quot;fill the foundations of all things.&quot; At the foundations, or within the &quot;essences,&quot; there is but the one and only divine Reality. If the realization of this truth of identity is thus &quot;possible,&quot; what the veil &quot;prevents&quot; is the affirmation of that identity at the wrong level of being — the level of empirical existence — rather than at the deepest level, where the &quot;essences&quot; of things are nothing other than the divine qualities, and thus the divine Reality itself. To equate created things with God amounts to pure <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, the cardinal sin of &quot;association,&quot; and it is against this that a vision of divine oneness must guard. One must always &quot;distinguish the Creator from the created, and the limit from the limited, and the Lord from the vassal.&quot;</p>
<p>The veil of creation, in other words, acts as a barrier against pantheism — the reduction of the Creator''s infinite transcendence to the finite forms of creatures. Yet since the veil has no existence apart from the self-manifestation of the Real, it also, paradoxically, makes possible &quot;that which is prevented in regard to Him&quot;: made transparent to the divine Reality of which it is one expression, the veil cannot prevent the immanent reality of God from shining through and within all things. On one side stands essential identity, with no diminution of divine transcendence; on the other stands existential distinction, with no lessening of divine immanence. This returns to the founding sentence of the first sermon of the Nahj: &quot;He is with every thing, but not through association; and other than every thing, but not through separation.&quot; It recalls too a central theme in Sufi metaphysics: the dynamic relationship between tanzih — the declaration of divine incomparability and transcendence — and tashbih — the affirmation of similarity and, at the deepest level, of immanence. In the words of Ibn al-Arabi:</p>
<p>&quot;He is not declared incomparable in any manner that will remove Him from similarity, nor is He declared similar in any manner that would remove Him from incomparability. So do not declare Him nondelimited and thus delimited by being distinguished from delimitation! For if He is distinguished then He is delimited by His nondelimitation. And if He is delimited by His nondelimitation, then He is not He.&quot;</p>
<p>To return to the Imam''s powerfully evocative statement, &quot;The veils do not veil Him.&quot; Both tanzih and tashbih can become veils unless each is seen in the light of the other; both transcendence and immanence can become veils unless the two are held together in spiritual vision; and both the creation, as divine manifestation, and the uncreated Essence, above and beyond all manifestation, can become veils unless they are integrated in the spiritual vision proper to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, and so made transparent, each &quot;revealing&quot; the other. In the same way, a one-sided stress on God as &quot;the Apparent&quot; becomes a veil by blinding one to God as &quot;the Hidden&quot; — and the reverse holds too. For even the hidden aspect of God, taken in isolation, will, despite its non-manifest nature, form a conceptual veil that prevents the seeing of God''s &quot;evidence,&quot; His &quot;evident-ness,&quot; in the very heart of the created world. The &quot;veils&quot; are thus not only created things but also the limits built into every human conception of God, including the divine qualities and attributes. Hence the need for a radical negation, on the plane of human conception, of those divine attributes. Only on the basis of such an apophasis can consciousness begin to fathom the true nature of divine transcendence, and see through the veils that &quot;do not veil Him.&quot;</p>
<p>These perspectives on the nature of the veil are better understood as the evocation of a spiritual vision than as the logical statement of empirical observation. They are the fruits of contemplative synthesis, not the conclusions of rational analysis. Such a vision resolves apparent contradiction through an intuitive awareness of the unitive simplicity of reality; it is not the product of some tortuous mental effort to reconcile the complexities of existence. It is at once a vision reflected by the heart burnished by the remembrance of God, and itself a form of that very remembrance. What the rational faculty will always perceive as irreducible contradiction, the spirit of the intellect can grasp as a fruitful paradox.</p>
<p>This aspect of the Imam''s teaching finds vivid expression in the words of the famous Sufi Abu Said al-Kharraz. Asked how he came to know God, he replied: through the fact that He brings opposites together (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَمْعُهُ الضِّدَّيْنِ</span>). He then recited the Quranic verse, He is the First and the Last, and the Outward and the Inward.</p>
<p>It is spiritual intuition that gives the capacity to see as one, in the unifying vision of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, what appears on the surface as many. In sharpening the paradox on the conceptual plane, Ali is, in modern parlance, deconstructing the artifices of mental conception, and in doing so he opens the way to intuitive apprehension and spiritual contemplation. The very work of the intellect, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عقل</span> — a word whose root means to bind or restrain — is one of binding together. The realities the heart discloses through intuition and inspiration are drawn by it' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'dhikr: the polish of the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || ' into the organic unity of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> through an exercise in logic, a movement of negation and affirmation, of constriction and expansion.</p>
<p>It is only at these deeper levels of consciousness that the complementary process of reconstruction can take place: a re-cognition of the Real, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>, arising from the heart''s vision of God through the verities of faith (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقَائِقُ الْإِيمَان</span>). Only these haqaiq are truly on a level with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>. Only these realities, hidden within the depths of faith and brought to light in a heart polished by the remembrance of God, can do justice to the reality of God.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'dhikr: the polish of the heart';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The practice of the remembrance of God, dhikruLlah, is best approached first through the Quranic verses that name it as a devotional discipline:</p>
<p>And invoke the Name of thy Lord morning and evening (Al-Insan: 25).</p>
<p>And invoke the Name of thy Lord, devoting thyself to Him [or: it] with utter devotion (Al-Muzzammil: 8).</p>
<p>O ye who believe! Invoke God with much invocation (Al-Ahzab: 41).</p>
<p>Call upon thy Lord in humility and in secret (Al-Araf: 55).</p>
<p>And invoke thy Lord within thyself, in humility and awe, and beneath thy breath, in the morning and in the night (Al-Araf: 205).</p>
<p>All these verses point to an actual practice, one to be maintained in different modes: within thyself, in humility, in awe, in secret, beneath thy breath, and so on. Most importantly, the invocation is to be performed abundantly, dhikran kathiran. The Quranic chapter al-Muzzammil (The Enwrapped) recalls how intense and how long the devotions of the Prophet and the first Muslims were, mentioning the long night vigils these early Muslims kept: Truly, thy Lord knoweth that thou standest in prayer for almost two-thirds of the night, and a half of it, and a third of it, as doth a group from those with thee (Al-Muzzammil: 20). The opening of this Sura contains the verse instructing the Prophet to remember/invoke the Name of thy Lord, and devote thyself to Him with utter devotion (Al-Muzzammil: 8). The pronoun in the word ilayhi, to Him, can refer either to Him as thy Lord or to it as the Name of thy Lord, so that the operative meaning of the verse becomes: Invoke the Name of God and devote yourself to this Name with utter devotion. This recalls the explicit injunction, Glorify the Name of thy Lord, the Exalted (Al-Ala: 1). In devotional practice, then, devotion to the Name of the Absolute amounts to devotion to the Absolute itself. The Absolute in itself lies above and beyond all forms, including all verbal forms. Yet it is made accessible through the divine Name — a Name that no human language invented, but that God bestowed by disclosing Himself, revealing the sacrament of His own Name. Distraction from the remembrance or invocation of the Name is, equally, distraction from God Himself, as two verses make clear: The devil hath overcome them, causing them to forget the remembrance of God (Al-Mujadila: 19), and O ye who believe, let not your wealth nor your children distract you from the remembrance of God (Al-Munafiqun: 9).</p>
<p>This identification of devoting oneself to God with devoting oneself to His Name follows from a principle Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) states in a simple affirmation: He who loves a thing dedicates himself fervently to its invocation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ أَحَبَّ شَيْئًا لَهِجَ بِذِكْرِهِ</span>). The lover of God should therefore devote himself utterly to the invocation of the Name of God, giving himself to it in abundance, kathiran. The pairing of lahija, meaning both to be devoted to and to mention repeatedly, with dhikr, which indicates the constant verbal repetition of the invocation, also appears in the Dua Kumayl, there with an added explicit reference to the tongue: ... my tongue has devoted itself to thy invocation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا لَهِجَ بِهِ لِسَانِي مِنْ ذِكْرِكَ</span>). He implores God, Make my times in the night and the day inhabited by Thy remembrance, and make my tongue invoke Thee with utter dedication (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اجْعَلْ لِسَانِي بِذِكْرِكَ لَاهِجًا</span>). The invocation as a practice is confirmed in his words: O He whose Name is a remedy and whose invocation is the cure (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا مَنِ اسْمُهُ دَوَاءٌ وَذِكْرُهُ شِفَاءٌ</span>).</p>
<p>Here the Name of God is far more than a name among other names, a word or sound among other symbols: it is the remedy for man''s inborn forgetfulness, the spiritual amnesia, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَةٌ</span>, that has become his second nature. But to be effective, the Name must be actively invoked rather than merely contemplated, just as a medicine must be taken and not merely thought about if it is to exercise its healing power. As the Imam puts it in one of his sermons, describing the greatness of the Quran: It has made the dhikr incumbent upon your tongues. And the invocation is to be performed kathiran, much, in abundance. The following hadith of the Prophet shows that dhikr is something to be done, a practice, and cannot be reduced to a purely theoretical interpretation of its meanings:</p>
<p>Abd Allah b. Busr reported that a man said, O Messenger of Allah, the laws of Islam are too many for me, so tell me something that I may cling to. He replied, may God bless him and give him peace, Let your tongue never cease to be moist from invoking Allah.</p>
<p>The verses that follow the Quranic exhortation to invoke much deserve quotation: O ye who believe, invoke God with much invocation, and glorify Him early and late. He it is who blesseth you, as do His angels, so that He may bring you forth from darkness into light; and He is ever merciful to the believers'' (Al-Ahzab: 41-43).</p>
<p>There is a reciprocity here between the dhikr of the believers and the salat — the prayer, or blessing — of God upon the believers. The relationship between the human act of invocation and the divine act of blessing is more complex than it first appears. Exploring this relationship, and the ontological dimensions of the invocation, brings one close to the mystery expressed by the verse, Remember Me, I will remember you (Al-Baqarah: 152).</p>
<p>Within these same verses (Al-Ahzab: 41-43) lies a further relationship: that between invocation and illumination, between being brought from darkness into light. The Imam speaks of the way in which human invocation results in divine illumination, and his words may be read as a commentary on this dimension of those verses: Whoever invokes God, glorified be He, God enlivens his heart and illuminates his inner substance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لُبٌّ</span>). The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لُبٌّ</span> (pl. albab) here evokes the Quranic verse describing the ulul-albab, the possessors of substance, in which the dhikr again figures prominently: Truly in the creation of the heavens and the earth, and in the alternation between night and day, there are signs for those of substance, those who invoke God standing, sitting and reclining on their sides, and reflect upon the creation of the heavens and the earth ... (Ali Imran: 190-191).</p>
<p>Besides confirming that remembrance is a practice and a method, this passage points to the relationship between dhikr and fikr, between invocation and reflection or meditation. The practice of invocation polishes the inmost organ of awareness, and so deepens the capacity for meditation upon the signs of God in creation and within oneself. When the element of love is placed between invocation and meditation, for he who loves a thing dedicates himself fervently to its invocation, these three elements enter a virtuous circle in which each strengthens and deepens the other two. The more seriously one ponders the signs'' of God and appreciates their beauty, the more one comes to love God; the more one loves God, the more one invokes His Name; the more one invokes, the more keenly one perceives the sacred message of those signs'' in the universe. Thus fikr intensifies dhikr, and dhikr deepens fikr.</p>
<p>These verses (Ali Imran: 190-191) also bring out the supra-formal dimension of invocation. The dhikr is presented here as the quintessence of all religious activity, the spiritual act par excellence. Its modes are universal: standing, sitting, reclining, as the verse states, and within thyself, in humility, in awe, in secret, beneath thy breath, as the verses cited above make clear. These modes transcend the formal rules governing the fixed canonical prayers, which prescribe specific words, movements, and conditions. The dhikr, by contrast, is to be performed at all times, in all places, and in all postures; it is something to be woven into the texture of everyday life rather than imposed upon life as an external, formalistic practice. According to Imam Jafar al-Sadiq, invocation is the one practice that should be carried out in all circumstances and at all times, with no limit placed upon it: Everything has a limit, at which it terminates, except the dhikr Allah. God has made obligatory the religious duties (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَرَائِضُ</span>), so whoever fulfills them, that fulfillment constitutes their limit. For the month of Ramadan, whoever fasts therein, that constitutes its limit ... He goes on to mention other rites, then cites the Quranic verse: O ye who believe, invoke God with much invocation, and adds that his father, Imam Muhammad al-Baqir, never ceased invoking, even while eating and walking.</p>
<p>In this light it becomes easier to understand those whom the Quran describes as men whom neither trade nor merchandise diverts from the remembrance of God. The verse also illuminates why believers are described as those who are perpetually at prayer (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَىٰ صَلَاتِهِمْ دَآئِمُونَ</span>) (Al-Maarij: 23). Such people are not merely regular in performing the canonical prayer at its appointed times; for the men of substance, there is no moment when they are not at prayer, if one understands prayer as the remembrance of God. The Quran instructs mankind to establish the prayer for the sake of My remembrance (Ta-Ha: 14). The very purpose and goal of prayer, its spiritual value and substance, is thus the remembrance of God.</p>
<p>A saying of Ali bears directly on this: Continuous invocation is the food of the spirit and the key to prayer (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِفْتَاحُ الصَّلَاةِ</span>). This sheds further light on why the Quran says that prayer keepeth [one] away from lewdness and iniquity, and the remembrance of God is greater (Al-Ankabut: 45). The formal, canonical prayer is described here in negative or constraining terms: it is a preventative. Its formality is a ritual necessity, but it is also, unavoidably, an existential limitation, performed and operative at one time and not another, expressed in certain forms and not others. The remembrance of God is greater in that it is the positive, enlivening substance of prayer; it is what liberates consciousness from the verbal, mental, and existential limits by which all formal prayer is defined. Formal prayer is inescapably bounded by such limits, and so its baraka, its blessedness, can all too easily become confined, in one''s own experience, within those same limits — so that one feels close to God only during formal prayer, and not in everyday life outside the prayer-times. It is dhikr, in the sense of recollectedness and constant awareness of God, that allows prayer, or the consciousness shaped by prayer, to open outward into its intended essence: an awareness of the unconditional presence of God. Dhikr thus transforms a preventative measure against moral transgression into a positive apprehension of spiritual reality. One might say that the dhikruLlah is greater than the ritual or canonical prayer in the measure that spiritual awareness transcends moral conscience, or in the measure that essence transcends form. The lesser is not negated by the greater but is rather contained within it, enriched by it, and brought to a fullness and scope it could never attain on its own. In this light one can better understand at least some of the reasons why the Imam, following the Prophet and the Quran, presents dhikruLlah as the quintessence of worship and devotion.</p>
<p>What remains are the spiritual fruits of the remembrance of God, and in particular realization, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحْقِيق</span>: the process by which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>, the Real, is made real within oneself. This is closely related to the process the Imam earlier described as tawahhud, self-integration, the spiritual assimilation of oneness within oneself, as distinct from the simple theological affirmation of oneness, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, in its conventional sense. In keeping with the Imam''s characteristic use of paradox, the fruit of remembrance can be approached first by examining its very opposite: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَةٌ</span>, heedlessness, or sahw, forgetfulness and distraction. Here again the Imam''s sayings function implicitly as a commentary on Quranic verses, while opening onto a rich terrain of mystical speculation and spiritual contemplation concerning the ultimate consequences of the invocation and remembrance of God. The Quran is severe in its condemnation of those who pray ostentatiously and hypocritically, those who give the lie to religion: Woe to those who pray, those who are heedless of their prayer, those who make a show, and who withhold simple assistance (Al-Maun: 4-7). The Imam likewise warns against remembering God heedlessly or distractedly, and in describing its opposite, perfect or complete dhikr, he articulates a paradoxical return to a kind of forgetting that goes to the very heart of the Sufi principle of extinction in remembrance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْفَنَاءُ فِي الذِّكْرِ</span>):</p>
<p>Do not remember God absent-mindedly (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَاهِيًا</span>), nor forget Him in distraction; rather, remember Him with perfect remembrance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْرًا كَامِلًا</span>), a remembrance in which your heart and tongue are in harmony, and what you conceal conforms with what you reveal. But you will not remember Him according to the true reality of the remembrance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَقِيقَةُ الذِّكْرِ</span>) until you forget your own soul in your remembrance.</p>
<p>This saying offers a summary of the entire mystical path. What is implied is the whole sequence of spiritual stations'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَامَات</span>, sing. maqam) through which total sincerity is attained. Sufism is itself often described simply as sincerity, that is, sidq or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>. To be totally sincere in this sense is to orient oneself and one''s desires purely toward God, without any taint of impurity, where impurity means the presence, in one''s devotion, action, or intention, of any desire for something other than God. On one hand, the dhikr itself helps to realize sincerity by polishing the heart; on the other, it is sincerity that confers perfection or completion upon the spiritual efficacy of the dhikr. What matters at this stage of perfect dhikr is that the outward verbal form of the invocation must harmonize with heartfelt intention. There must be no contradiction between what the tongue articulates and what motivates the heart. Bringing heart and tongue into harmony means that the divine Reality invoked by the tongue reverberates in the heart, the very source of the soul''s fundamental dispositions, its virtues.</p>
<p>The divine qualities contained within the Name of the Absolute must, to some degree at least, be reflected in the heart and soul of the invoker as virtues — virtues that are summed up in the sincerity or purity of soul to which <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span> refers. When the soul is completely pure, sincerely and utterly at one with the highest ideals proclaimed by the tongue and the intellect, it becomes transparent to the spiritual qualities embedded within its own substance: the fitra, or primordial nature. These are the qualities brought to light, and brought to life, by the dhikr; they are the qualities that shine forth from the heart once it has been polished by the dhikr. In their absence, however, the purely technical act of invocation is heedless'' and without any effect. One who makes no effort to attain sincerity, who does not take up the greater jihad, is heedless of God, neglecting the moral qualities that accompany authentic remembrance and ignoring the moral conditions its efficacy requires.</p>
<p>Yet what is most remarkable in this saying of the Imam is his presentation of the ultimate stage of dhikr in terms of its apparent opposite. The reality of dhikr, its haqiqa, opens outward into the supreme haqiqa, divine Reality as such. This degree of dhikr requires the forgetting of the individual: the veil of the self must be cast aside if the spiritual vision of divine Reality is to be attained. There can be no marifa without self-effacement at the outset, and extinction (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>) in the end. The reality of remembering calls for the most radical forgetting.</p>
<p>In other words, a clear distinction — indeed a radical break — separates perfect dhikr from the reality of dhikr. To move from perfection to reality, from human perfection to divine Reality, is to make an ontological leap of consciousness. At the first level, the aim is to eliminate hypocrisy and to attain sincerity, integrity, and wholeness — in short, perfection on the plane of the human soul. Here the individual, as the agent of the dhikr, keeps his identity. The harmonious reverberation between hidden intent and outward act is what, at this stage, is called perfect dhikr. Yet it remains a dhikr belonging to the individual as agent. At the higher level, by contrast, the dhikr can no longer be defined in relation to the individual, for the individual has been forgotten — forgotten by himself. This can only mean that the center of consciousness has shifted from the invoker, al-dhakir, to the Invoked, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَذْكُورُ</span>. The haqiqa of the dhikr is thus one with the object of invocation, the Real itself, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>. Esoterically, then, the true agent of the invocation is God Himself. Man''s apparent invocation is no more than an outward appearance, an illusion that the very act of invocation extinguishes.</p>
<p>The question of agency returns here. A hadith qudsi bears upon it: &quot;My slave never ceases to draw near to Me through supererogatory acts (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَوَافِلُ</span>) until I love him.&quot; In his commentary on the Quranic verses about those whom the world does not distract from the remembrance of God, the Imam made clear that these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَوَافِلُ</span> center upon the practice of invocation. Invocation, then, can be understood as disclosing the reality of God''s ontological agency within all acts: God is the hearing and the seeing of the individual whom He loves as a result of his devotions. This account of the Divine penetrating the faculties of soul and body describes a universal reality, the true state of affairs — not any change of state, whether in God or in the individual. God does not &quot;change&quot; in order to enter into the acts and perceptions of one person rather than another.</p>
<p>His &quot;becoming&quot; one with the acts of perception must be understood as a reality &quot;becoming clear&quot; to the soul once it is unveiled, so that the soul sees through the illusory appearance of its own agency. Through the invocation, the soul is enabled to see the deeper meaning of two Quranic verses:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">As-Saffat: 96</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱللَّهُ خَلَقَكُمْ وَمَا تَعْمَلُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;God hath created you and what ye do&quot; (As-Saffat: 96)</p></blockquote>
<p>and:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Anfal: 17</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا رَمَيْتَ إِذْ رَمَيْتَ وَلَكِنَّ ٱللَّهَ رَمَىٰ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;thou didst not throw when thou threwest [the stones], but God threw&quot; (Al-Anfal: 17).</p></blockquote>
<p>The kind of &quot;vision&quot; the dhikr makes possible is illuminated by the very title of one of Ibn al-Arabi''s treatises, Kitab al-fana fil-mushahada (The Book of Extinction in Contemplation). The title seems to point directly at the Imam''s paradox of forgetting at the very heart of remembrance, for to be extinguished in contemplation is akin to being forgotten in invocation. At the opening of this treatise, Ibn al-Arabi writes that the divine Reality is too exalted to be witnessed by the creaturely eye. The vision of God is impossible so long as any &quot;trace of the condition of the creature in the eye of the witness&quot; remains. Only toward the end of the treatise is this seemingly insuperable barrier removed. Ibn al-Arabi resolves the problem by means of the Prophet''s definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>: &quot;It is to worship God as though you see Him, and if you see Him not, yet truly He sees you.&quot; The Arabic wording allows a pause to be placed in the middle of the phrase &quot;if you see Him not (in lam takun: tarahu),&quot; and that pause transforms the meaning entirely into: &quot;if you are not, you see Him.&quot;</p>
<p>This vision of God cannot, then, belong to the individual — &quot;forgotten&quot; in the Imam''s words, or non-existent according to Ibn al-Arabi. The vision of God can only be by God, not by man. The metaphysical underpinnings of this &quot;ontological transference,&quot; in relation to the divine Names, are taken up further on. The message of &quot;extinction in contemplation&quot; is reinforced by a dialogue Ibn al-Arabi narrates between himself and Moses in the sixth heaven, set within his account of his own visionary ascent through the heavens to the divine presence within his own soul.</p>
<p>He addresses Moses: &quot;You requested the vision (of God), while the Messenger of God (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُحَمَّد</span>) said that not one of you will see his Lord until he dies.&quot;</p>
<p>Moses replies: &quot;And it was just like that: when I asked Him for the vision, He answered me, so that I fell down stunned (Al-Araf: 143). Then I saw Him in my (state of) being stunned.&quot;</p>
<p>Ibn al-Arabi asks: &quot;While (you were) dead?&quot;</p>
<p>Moses: &quot;While (I was) dead. I did not see God until I had died.&quot;</p>
<p>Here is a supra-personal vision entirely congruent with Ali s unselfconscious remembrance. What both forms of spiritual assimilation imply is the &quot;ontological transference&quot; of agency. The true vision of God cannot take place so long as anything creaturely remains in the &quot;eye&quot; of the beholder. The spiritual principle of this dialogue — and even some of its language — is rendered in condensed form in a supplication attributed to the Imam. He pleads with God to make him &quot;one of those whom You have called and he has replied to You, upon whom You have glanced and who falls unconscious before Your majesty.&quot; The word saiqa (&quot;to fall unconscious,&quot; &quot;to be stunned&quot;) in this supplication is the same word used in the Quranic verse (Al-Araf: 143) for Moses'' state when God revealed Himself to the mountain. A single &quot;glance&quot; from the Real suffices to precipitate this undoing of self-awareness — the shadow cast, upon the plane of individual consciousness, by the blazing light of God''s self-disclosure, His tajalli.</p>
<p>This divine &quot;glance&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَحْظَة</span>) returns the discussion to Ibn al-Arabi''s evocative phrase &quot;extinction in contemplation.&quot; In his Tarjuman al-ashwaq, he refers to divine wisdom as a peacock that &quot;kills with her glances,&quot; and adds in his own commentary that this refers to &quot;the station&quot; of al-fana fil-mushahada (extinction in contemplation). Earlier in the same commentary, he refers to the &quot;murderous glances&quot; of this divine wisdom &quot;which accrues to a man in his hours of solitude, and which assaults him with such violence that he is unable to behold his personality, and which exercises dominion over him.&quot; His commentary on the following lines is also instructive, underlining the relationship between dhikr and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>: &quot;Wild is she, none can make her his friend; she has gotten in her solitary chamber (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْتُ خَلْوَتِهَا</span>), a mausoleum for remembrance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِکْر</span>).&quot; The commentary adds: &quot;because contemplation of the Essence is a passing away (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>). Her solitude is her looking on herself, for God says, Neither My earth nor My heaven contains Me, but I am contained by the heart of My believing servant, and since the heart which contains this essential wisdom of the race of Jesus is bare and empty of all attributes, it is like a desert and she is like a wild animal.&quot;</p>
<p>Realization through remembrance, then, strictly implies the extinction of individual consciousness, the perishing of egoistic self-awareness before the Absolute. This principle is far from merely speculative. Quite apart from the wealth of Sufi literature on the states of consciousness (ahwal, sing. hal) through which the mystic passes, there is the evidence of the Imam''s own immersion in such states. Abul-Darda, an eminent companion of the Prophet, reported that he once found Ali lying in one of the palm orchards of Medina, his body seemingly lifeless, as stiff as wood. He went to condole with Ali s family, but was told by his wife Fatima, &quot;My cousin has not died. Rather, in fear of God he has fainted. This condition overcomes him often.&quot; The &quot;fear&quot; in question is to be understood as &quot;awe.&quot; It relates to the heavenly aspect of khawf — heavenly because Paradise is said to be the fruit of realizing this kind of fear to perfection, as the Quran indicates: &quot;But whoso feareth (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَافَ</span>) the station of his Lord and restraineth his soul from caprice, verily, the Garden will be his abode&quot; (An-Naziat: 40-41). &quot;Fear of God,&quot; in the spiritual rather than the moralistic sense, does not so much terrify as beatify. It produces not merely quaking awe before the transcendent Absolute, but also an intimate absorption within the immanent divine presence. As is often quoted in Sufism: when one fears creatures one flees from them, but when one fears God one flees to Him:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">At-Tawbah: 118</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَّا مَلْجَأَ مِنَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَّآ إِلَيْهِ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;There is no refuge from God except [fleeing] to Him&quot; (At-Tawbah: 118).</p></blockquote>
<p>This report also shows that the following saying of the Imam is not mere extravagant rhetoric. It appears, rather, to allude to an authentic state of spiritual intoxication — a total re-absorption of consciousness within its divine source, and a description of the conditions leading up to the state that &quot;overcomes him often,&quot; as Fatima put it:</p>
<p>Truly, God has a drink for His friends (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوْلِيَائِهِ</span>). When they drink it, they are intoxicated (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَكَرَ</span>); and when they are intoxicated, they are enraptured (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرِبَ</span>); and when they are enraptured, they are blessed (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَابَ</span>); and when they are blessed, they dissolve (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَابَ</span>); and when they dissolve, they are free (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَصَ</span>); and when they are free, they devote themselves purely (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَصَ</span>); and when they devote themselves purely, they seek (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَلَبَ</span>); and when they seek, they find (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَجَدَ</span>); and when they find, they arrive (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَصَلَ</span>); and when they arrive, they are at one (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِتَّصَلَ</span>); there is no difference between them and their Beloved.</p>
<p>This saying of the Imam echoes that of the Prophet, who said he had states or &quot;moments&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَقْت</span>) that no &quot;angel brought close&quot; nor &quot;prophet sent forth&quot; could encompass — the implication being that neither angel nor prophet can subsist as such in the ineffable &quot;moments&quot; of absolute <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. This is not &quot;union&quot;; it is, rather, the realization of oneness. Union implies two entities becoming one, whereas God is, as the Imam put it, &quot;that which has no second.&quot; Even to call Him &quot;one&quot; falls short, since that word implies that a second might stand in sequence to Him. He is more truly named unique — a reality that admits no otherness. This ultimate realization of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> can be seen as the fruit of the invocation — the most potent of all intoxicating &quot;drinks,&quot; if prayer may be called a drink. And it leads to a careful consideration of the true agent of the invocation, the true &quot;drinker.&quot; What appears to be man''s invocation of God is transformed into the divine invocation of man. At the outset it seems that man &quot;drinks&quot; God through His Name; but the ultimate result of the invocation, unitive realization, reveals that it is in fact God who &quot;drinks into Himself&quot; the essence of human consciousness — an essence from which the impurity of self-consciousness has been extracted. The true nature of the dhikr, masked by the outward form of man''s invocation of God, is alluded to in another saying of the Imam: &quot;The invocation is not a formality of speech nor a way of thinking; rather, it comes forth firstly from the Invoked, and secondly from the invoker (awwal min <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَذْكُورُ</span> wa thanin min al-dhakir).&quot;</p>
<p>It is thus God Himself who performs the invocation, first and foremost; man''s invocation is but a shadow, a reflection, a consequence of this divine invocation. The many-sidedness of the Imam''s brief sentence opens onto a number of fruitful meditations upon the meaning, the origin, the end, and the very substance of the dhikr. Among these stands the Quranic verse cited earlier: &quot;Remember Me, I will remember you.&quot;</p>
<p>This perception of the reality of the invocation reveals its spiritual efficacy more clearly. The reality denoted by the term dhikr is no longer confined within the boundaries of the individual, still less definable in terms of his verbal or mental activity. In its primary, metaphysical significance, dhikr opens out into the realm of engendering — it implies the function of bringing into existence, the effusion of Being. The creative Word uttered by God — &quot;Be! (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُنْ</span>)&quot; — which brings the possibilities of being into existence, can be seen in this light as a form of dhikr: God utters the &quot;name&quot; of the possible thing, &quot;calling&quot; it from nonexistence into existence. This passage from nothingness to being is what renders the possible thing &quot;remembered,&quot; madhkur. Here again, the Imam''s sentence can be read both as a commentary on a Quranic verse and as an anticipation of the highly elaborate cosmogony associated with Ibn al-Arabi.</p>
<p>The Quran poses the following question:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Insan: 1</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هَلْ أَتَىٰ عَلَى ٱلْإِنسَنِ حِينٌۭ مِّنَ ٱلدَّهْرِ لَمْ يَكُن شَيْـًۭٔا مَّذْكُورًا</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Hath there come upon man any moment in time when he was not a thing remembered?&quot; (Al-Insan: 1).</p></blockquote>
<p>Man''s being a &quot;thing remembered&quot; is here tantamount to his coming into being, and his nothingness is equivalent to his being &quot;not remembered&quot; — that is, not yet called forth from the abyss of pre-existential hiddenness into the light of manifest existence. In this pre-existential state, the human being is, according to Ibn al-Arabi, &quot;known,&quot; malum, but only by God. He refers to this verse in the following lines of al-Futuhat al-makkiyya (The Meccan Illuminations):</p>
<p>God said of Himself that He is al-Badi, the Originator, that is, He created that which had no like or exemplar in all the degrees of being, for He knows, by His all-encompassing nature, every single thing that enters into the degrees of being. Therefore He says, as regards the creation of man, that he &quot;was not a thing remembered,&quot; for His is the remembrance, so a thing has being in the remembrance of the one who remembers it.</p>
<p>God''s &quot;remembrance&quot; of man thus precedes man''s remembrance of God — ontologically, not merely temporally. This yields another interpretation of the verse cited earlier: &quot;and the remembrance of God is greater&quot; — infinitely greater, that is, than man''s remembrance of Him. The true being of man resides in the &quot;remembrance&quot; God has of him. While man''s devotion to God is articulated through the utterance of the divine Name, God''s creation of man is articulated through His utterance of the creative Word. In Ibn al-Arabi''s cosmogony, the utterance of this Word is not simply an event in time; that Word never ceases to reverberate, furnishing each human soul with its divine substrate, support, or foundation — its mustanad ilahi. This Word &quot;breathes&quot; existence upon the immutable entity, al-ayn al-thabita, which, though inexistent in itself, determines the contours, form, and configuration of the being that will be manifested through it — just as a prism refracts the light passing through it, or as water takes on the color of its cup, an image used by Junayd and often quoted by Ibn al-Arabi.</p>
<p>The hadith al-haqiqa, attributed to Ali, contains the essence of the Akbari doctrine of the tajalli of the unique Light of God upon the differentiated &quot;receptacles,&quot; the loci of manifestation for that Light. The Imam''s disciple Kumayl questions him on the meaning of al-haqiqa. The response includes the following affirmations:</p>
<p>Al-haqiqa is the unveiling of the splendors of Majesty without any allusion; the effacement of what is falsely imagined (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَوْهُومُ</span>) together with the clarity of what is truly known (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَعْلُومُ</span>); the drawing of absolute unicity toward the quality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>; a light dawning from the morning of eternity, its traces shimmering upon the temples of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>.</p>
<p>Al-Kashani comments on this last image: &quot;That is, the manifestation of the Light of the one Essence upon the loci of manifestation of the qualities of the Real, the Essence, these being the loci of the entities of existents (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْيَانُ الْمَوْجُودَاتِ</span>), which the Imam calls temples of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, that is, the forms of the divine Names in the station of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>.&quot; This mustanad ilahi, or divine support of each individual soul, is therefore its particular Lord, the divine Name that engenders and sustains it. The soul is but the locus of manifestation, the mazhar, where God discloses Himself in a particular, unique mode.</p>
<p>The very first words of the khutba with which Ibn al-Arabi opens the Futuhat al-makkiyya are these: &quot;Praise be to Him who engendered things from nothing, then negated it; and established their being through the orientation toward them of His Words.&quot; When the divine Word turns toward a possible thing—this turning is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَجُّه</span>—the Word''s properties become manifest according to what that possible thing can receive. The possibility is then &quot;breathed&quot; into existence and becomes like a &quot;body,&quot; whose &quot;heart&quot; is the divine Name that brings it into being: &quot;every slave has a Name which is its Lord; so it becomes the body of that Name, which is its heart.&quot;</p>
<p>This comparison recalls the Imam''s image of the dhikr as a &quot;polish&quot; for hearts. The Name revealed from &quot;above&quot; is mirrored by a Name that resounds from &quot;within.&quot; This mutual reflection appears once the rust clouding the surface of the heart is removed by the polish of the dhikr. What is evoked here is a kind of union between two divine realities, and it brings to mind once more the phrase &quot;light upon light.&quot;</p>
<p>Earlier the Imam had spoken of the Names of God &quot;which have filled the foundations of all things,&quot; a phrase that anticipates Ibn al-Arabi''s teaching that the divine Names are the supports and underlying ground of all things. A question arises here, and its answers bring one nearer to grasping the powerful and mysterious efficacy of the dhikr. What is the difference between the divine Names understood as the metaphysical roots of all things and the particular Names of God revealed in the Quran? On one side, the whole of creation is the &quot;Book of God,&quot; and every phenomenon is one of God''s &quot;words.&quot; On the other, the revealed text holds specific words and Names, and it is these that are to be recited and invoked. Why?</p>
<p>One clear answer is given in the following verse:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Isra: 110</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلِ ٱدْعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ أَوِ ٱدْعُوا۟ ٱلرَّحْمَنَ ۖ أَيًّۭا مَّا تَدْعُوا۟ فَلَهُ ٱلْأَسْمَآءُ ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ۚ وَلَا تَجْهَرْ بِصَلَاتِكَ وَلَا تُخَافِتْ بِهَا وَٱبْتَغِ بَيْنَ ذَلِكَ سَبِيلًۭا</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Call upon Allah or call upon al-Rahman. By whatever Name you call upon Him, His are the most beautiful Names&quot; (Al-Isra: 110).</p></blockquote>
<p>The Quran itself commands believers to invoke these specific, revealed Names, and not others. These Names are therefore utterly unlike the names of the idolaters'' gods:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Najm: 23</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْ هِىَ إِلَّآ أَسْمَآءٌۭ سَمَّيْتُمُوهَآ أَنتُمْ وَآبَآؤُكُم مَّآ أَنزَلَ ٱللَّهُ بِهَا مِن سُلْطَنٍ ۚ إِن يَتَّبِعُونَ إِلَّا ٱلظَّنَّ وَمَا تَهْوَى ٱلْأَنفُسُ ۖ وَلَقَدْ جَآءَهُم مِّن رَّبِّهِمُ ٱلْهُدَىٰٓ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;They are but names that ye have named, ye and your fathers, for which God hath revealed no authority&quot; (An-Najm: 23).</p></blockquote>
<p>The names of idols are like the ordinary names given to things: arbitrary labels or signs with no real bond to the things themselves, and still less to the principles beneath those things. The Names of God, revealed by God, belong to another order. They are steeped in the very substance of the reality they name. For this reason they carry &quot;authority&quot; (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلْطَان</span>), and by virtue of that authority they have the power to transform and not merely to inform. They are, in other words, charged with a theurgic power, the capacity to render present to mind and heart the indwelling reality of God.</p>
<p>God''s presence cannot be taken away, since nothing that exists can lie &quot;outside&quot; His all-encompassing presence; yet that presence can still become veiled, at least from the side of the one who perceives. The heart can be veiled from God by the &quot;rust&quot; of forgetfulness that settles over it, so that the divine Reality comes to be hidden by the very phenomena that manifest it. The Imam raises the question of how it is that the Creator both manifests Himself &quot;by things&quot; and is hidden from sight &quot;by things.&quot;</p>
<p>This being so, two things are needed to open oneself to the divine presence within and beyond all things: a polishing of one''s own &quot;within&quot; through the dhikr, and an unveiling or revelation from that &quot;beyond.&quot; This second disclosure can be understood as a direct revelation of God within the indirect revelation of Him that is the whole created cosmos—a cosmos that reveals and veils the divine Reality at once. From one point of view the two revelations, cosmic and scriptural, are both manifestations of God; from another, the scriptural revelation is charged with a spiritual power that makes it incomparably more revealing than the created cosmos. As a spiritual method, the cosmos as manifested divine &quot;Word&quot; and Allah as a revealed Name of God share no common measure. The one is differentiated manifestation; the other, a unique revelation. The divine Name may also be called the quintessential manifestation, for it both sums up the entire realm of manifestation and transcends it.</p>
<p>In one respect the difference between cosmic manifestation and scriptural revelation is like the difference between shadow and light. Revelation is like a projection of the pure Light of God, and is therefore of one substance with the source from which it emanates; manifestation is like the shadow cast by that light. Both are projections of one and the same reality, yet this does not lessen how far apart light and darkness stand. In the words of Ibn al-Arabi, &quot;The very Being of the Real is a verified Light, the very existence of creation is a shadow following after.&quot;</p>
<p>There is a remarkable supplication attributed to Ali, the Dua al-Mashlul, the &quot;supplication of the lame man.&quot; It runs to one hundred and twenty-two verses, filled with a great many divine Names, attributes, and titles. Near its end comes a verse that may be taken as the key to the entire text: &quot;I ask Thee by every Name with which Thou hast named Thyself.&quot; It is because God &quot;names Himself&quot; that His Names hold their theurgic power, their sacramental substance, their mystical efficacy. The revealed substance of the Names is what accounts for these distinctive properties and makes them unlike any other names. This is why, as Sufi texts so commonly affirm, the Name is the Named (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلِاسْمُ هُوَ الْمُسَمَّى</span>).</p>
<p>These reflections on how the Names of God stand in relation to being do illuminate certain sides of the dhikr as a mystical practice, yet they fall short of explaining that leap of consciousness in the order of being, that extinction of self-awareness, to which the Imam points in the words, &quot;you will not remember Him according to the true reality of the remembrance until you forget your own soul in your remembrance.&quot; A question then follows: why and how does the dhikr bring about this extinction of consciousness, this effacement of individual being, especially when the very work of the dhikr seems to be the opposite—to awaken and bring on a heightened mode of consciousness and to deepen one''s quality of being?</p>
<blockquote class="quran aside editorial"><p class="tr"><strong>Editorial note (tradition-grounded).</strong> The &quot;spiritual amnesia&quot; this chapter names, ghafla, is worth pausing over, since the very remembrance it prescribes is defined against it. The Arabic root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غ</span>-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ف</span>-<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ل</span> describes an untended emptiness — land with no landmark, a mind with no attention — and ghafla is its inward form: a heedless forgetfulness of God, the exact opposite of the wakeful remembrance that dhikr demands. That opposition is why the chapter can treat distraction from the Name as distraction from God Himself, and why the invocation must be performed abundantly rather than merely contemplated. To invoke much, in this light, is to keep the field of the heart tended against the very emptiness the word describes.</p></blockquote>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'remembrance as the quintessence of worship';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The first response to that question begins with a limit. A person cannot, by his own effort, cross from remembrance into forgetfulness. Someone who is conscious, and who is concentrating on the act of invocation, cannot make himself unconscious of himself in the course of that invocation. The Imam''s phrasing is exact: the soul is to be forgotten in the remembrance. Any effort to will oneself into unconsciousness comes from a conscious will, and so it deepens self-consciousness rather than erasing it. The point, then, is not to force oneself to forget oneself while invoking God. It is rather to concentrate on the Name so completely that no room is left in one''s awareness — no space, whether mental or existential — for anything but the Named, the reality being invoked. A first degree of self-effacement can therefore be understood as a natural by-product of that total concentration.</p>
<p>The full completion of self-effacement in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>, however, comes as a divine grace. It exceeds all individual effort, even though the act of invocation is understood to draw this grace down, or to clear from the heart the rust that hides from a person his own nothingness before the sole reality of God, that which has no second. This part of the response can be gathered up in an esoteric reading of the Quranic words, ... thou wilt not forget, unless God willeth it so (Al-Ala: 6-7). Al-Kalabadhi, who wrote one of the most important early manuals of Sufism, opens his chapter on dhikr with words that echo the Imam''s: The haqiqa of the dhikr is this: that you forget everything other than the madhkur (the Invoked) in the dhikr. For He says: &quot;So remember thy Lord when thou hast forgotten&quot; (Al-Kahf: 24). That is, when you have forgotten what is not God, then you have remembered God.</p>
<p>The second response can be framed through the principle of inverse analogy. Human invocation is analogous to divine invocation: it is a kind of reflection of it. And like any reflected image, human invocation both shares in what it reflects and turns it upside down. In the first respect, the act of invocation brings the invoker a fuller consciousness and a fuller share in being. In the second respect, human invocation reaches its completion in what looks like the very opposite of this: the effacement of the individual in the object he invokes. Yet the opposition is only apparent. In reality this effacement is the fulfillment of that same growth in consciousness and being, though its final stage is a sudden leap — the negation of the very limits that make a person a separate individual. The individual is limited, and a limit is itself a kind of negation; so to negate this negation amounts to pure affirmation: the affirmation of unconditional being that lies beyond the individual. On the plane of the self, this negation of a negation can be seen as the reflection of the negation of nothingness that Ibn al-Arabi expresses in his line, Praise be to Him who engendered things from nothing, then negated it.</p>
<p>Here the Imam''s enigmatic saying returns: I am the death of the dead. It recalls the words Rumi places in the Imam''s mouth in these lines of his Mathnawi:</p>
<p>He said, I am wielding the sword for God''s sake, I am the servant of God, I am not under the command of the body.</p>
<p>I am the Lion of God, I am not the lion of my passion: my deed bears witness to my religion.</p>
<p>In war I am (manifesting the truth of) thou didst not throw when thou threwest: I am (but) as the sword, and the wielder is the (divine) Sun.</p>
<p>I have removed the baggage of self out of the way, I have deemed (what is) other than God to be non-existence.</p>
<p>I am a shadow, the Sun is my lord; I am the chamberlain, I am not the curtain (which prevents approach) to Him.</p>
<p>I am filled with the pearls of union, like a (jewelled) sword: in battle I make (men) living, not slain.</p>
<p>The cosmic unfolding of creation moves from God to man, and it requires the negation of a relative nothingness that is pregnant with the possibilities of being. Man''s realization of God moves the other way, and it requires the negation of that same nothingness'' once breathed into being by God. Ibn al-Arabi''s treatise Extinction in Contemplation names what is at stake: the extinction of that which never was ... and the subsistence of that which never ceased to be. The following Quranic verses are clearly implied: Everything thereon perisheth (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَانٍۢ</span>), and there subsisteth (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَبْقَىٰ</span>) only the Face of thy Lord, possessor of glory and bounty (Ar-Rahman: 26-27).</p>
<p>Yet everything that exists has two aspects, or two faces'': a divine face, which is the thing''s true being, and a created face, which is nothing. The Face of the Lord that alone subsists is therefore not only the divine Reality above and beyond all things, but that same reality within all things. This is the meaning of al-Ghazali''s esoteric commentary on the Quranic verse, Everything perisheth except His Face (Al-Qasas: 88). Al-Ghazali comments:</p>
<p>[it is] not that it perisheth at some particular moment, but rather it is forever perishing, since it cannot be conceived except as perishing. For each thing other than God is, when considered in and by itself, pure nothingness; and if considered from the face (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَجْهَهُ</span>) to which existence flows to it from the Prime Reality, it is viewed as existing, but not in itself, solely from the face which accompanies Him Who gives it existence. Therefore, the divine face is the sole thing in reality.</p>
<p>He then ties the mystical realization of this oneness to the invocation of God and to the forgetting of the self within that invocation, and so he gives a valuable commentary on what this total dissolution of self-consciousness means. The Sufis who verify this oneness through mystical taste (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَوْقٌ</span>), he writes, are drowned in oneness'', so that they become dumbfounded:</p>
<p>No capacity remained within them save to recall Allah; yea, not so much as the capacity to recall their own selves ... when this state prevails, it is called in relation to him who experiences it Extinction, nay Extinction of Extinction, for the soul has become extinct to itself, extinct to its own extinction; for it becomes unconscious of itself and unconscious of its own unconsciousness, since were it conscious of its own unconsciousness, it would be conscious of itself.</p>
<p>Relevant here too is a saying that al-Qushayri attributes to Abu Uthman, which throws light on another side of the unconsciousness'' reached through invocation: Whoever has not tasted the loneliness of unconsciousness will not find the food of the intimacy of remembrance. Here losing consciousness of oneself is called loneliness'', while becoming fully conscious of God through invocation yields intimacy. One cannot find true intimacy with the divine Reality except by stripping the self of its false intimacy — that self-satisfied, habitual self-centeredness which is the shadow cast by self-awareness. It is this awareness of self that, in the Imam''s statement, must be forgotten. Such higher forgetfulness can arise only on the ground of the remembrance of God; for only the source of Being, unconditional Consciousness, and eternal Life can extinguish, eclipse, or efface the relative existence, partial consciousness, and apparent life of the individual. This, the haqiqa of the dhikr, is put with perfect economy in the Imam''s own words: ... forget your own soul in your remembrance.</p>
<p>The state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>, the highest degree of this forgetting of oneself, is therefore a grace. The individual cannot attain it, however hard he strives. Yet this does not mean the invoker gives up effort. On the contrary, as the Sufi tradition attests again and again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span> is closely bound to the practice of dhikr, and is in fact its very climax. After this state, or hal, of extinction comes the station, or maqam, of subsistence, baqa: a return of consciousness to the self — a self now transfigured by the knowledge of reality that was impressed upon the soul in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span>. For it is only in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span> that the final vision of absolute oneness is reached, a vision that leaves no room for the duality of being which clings inescapably to individuality. To assert the individual is to assert its correlate, the Lord, and so to affirm duality.</p>
<p>Al-Kashani makes this point in his commentary on the Quranic verse, Remember Him as He hath guided you (Al-Baqarah: 198), where he traces the guided ascent through the degrees of the dhikr. The first is the dhikr of the tongue, which corresponds to the soul (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النَّفْس</span>). Then comes the dhikr of the heart (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْقَلْب</span>), which corresponds to the divine acts, those that make His graces manifest. Then the dhikr of the secret (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلسِّرُّ</span>), which corresponds to the vision of the divine acts together with the unveiling of the sciences of the manifestation of the divine qualities''. Then the dhikr of the spirit (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرُّوح</span>), corresponding to the contemplation of the lights of the manifestations of the divine qualities, with an apprehension of the light of the Essence. Then comes the dhikr of the hidden (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَفِيُّ</span>), which is the contemplation of the beauty of the Essence along with the subsistence of duality. And finally the dhikr of the Essence, which is essential vision, attained through the removal of duality.</p>
<p>The same point emerges from the counsel of Ibn Ataillah, which shows how the dhikr opens a person to the divine presence, and to the grace by which alone the individual can truly forget himself — that is, become unconscious of his own unconsciousness'', as al-Ghazali put it:</p>
<p>Do not abandon the invocation because you do not feel the Presence of God therein. For your forgetfulness of the invocation of Him is worse than your forgetfulness in the invocation of Him. Perhaps He will take you from an invocation with forgetfulness (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَةٌ</span>) to one with vigilance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَقَظَة</span>), and from one with vigilance to one with the Presence of God (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُضُور</span>), and from one with the Presence of God to one wherein everything but the Invoked (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمَذْكُورُ</span>) is absent. And that is not difficult for God.</p>
<p>In his commentary on the Hikam, Ibn Ajiba adds: So, in the measure that one is extinguished in the Name, one is extinguished in the Essence. This follows from the principle that the Name is the Named. In another work, a commentary on a poem by Shushtari on the divine Name Allah, he sets out this central tenet of the invocatory discipline in Sufism more plainly. The first line of the poem reads, speaking of the letters that make up the word Allah: The alif before two lams, then a ha: coolness of the eyes. Ibn Ajiba comments: The &quot;coolness of the eyes&quot;, this is joy, for tears of joy, as one knows by experience, are cool. The author means this: this Name is the joy of my heart, its happiness, its beatitude. He then quotes some lines from al-Hallaj on the letters of the Name Allah, adds a verse of his own, and follows with this comment: The Name is not other than the Named, for joy cannot come from anything but the essence of a thing, not from the name as such.</p>
<p>This sums up the doctrine that the Name and the Named are identical — the rationale for the Sufi practice of dhikruLlah. Yet a problem arises here. However closely the Imam''s statements on the dhikr may agree with these later Sufi expressions of the same underlying principles, this particular emphasis on the identity of the Name and the Named appears, on the surface, to conflict with many sayings of the Shii Imams that deny the Name is the Named. Imam Jafar al-Sadiq, for instance, is reported to have said:</p>
<p>The Name of Allah is other than Him. For everything to which the name of a thing is applicable is a creation, hence other than God. For whatever the tongue expresses, or is worked on by the hand, is a creation ... everything described is created, and the Creator of things cannot be described within the limits of a named object.</p>
<p>Allah has ninety-nine Names. If the Name were the Named, then every one of the Names would be a god. But Allah is the one meaning, or spiritual reality, mana, indicated by the Names, and all of them are other than Him.</p>
<p>This apparent contradiction can be resolved in two ways. First, one must weigh carefully what the word Name means in each of the two contexts: the context of doctrinal distinction, where the Absolute is held strictly apart from the relative, and the Essence from all forms; and the context of methodic concentration, where the Absolute is understood to be the reality of the very form through which it reveals itself. One can then turn to one of the most esoteric aspects of how the Shii Imams understood themselves: their references to their own selves as the most beautiful Names'' (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْأَسْمَآءُ ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ</span>).</p>
<p>As for the meaning of Name, Ibn al-Arabi offers a precise definition: [T]he Names have two connotations; the first connotation is God Himself who is what is named, the second that by which one Name is distinguished from another ... As being essentially the other, the Name is the Reality, while as being not the other, it is the imagined Reality. On the one hand, each Name is identical with every other Name, because each is essentially identical with the Named — the one Essence that is the source of all the Names. On the other hand, each Name has its own particular meaning and quality: it is the relationship through which the one Reality engages the created cosmos. In this second respect each Name is distinct from the others, and distinction implies limitation, and so relativity, and in the end a kind of illusion before the one and only Reality. It is in this sense that the Name takes on the character of imagined Reality. For Ibn al-Arabi, the Names have no separate existence of their own; they all return to the one and only Reality, that of the divine Essence. This dissolves the problem raised in the saying of Imam Jafar — the problem that different Names would imply different gods''.</p>
<p>A lexicon of Sufi terms, the Lataif al-ilam fi isharat ahl al-ilham (Subtle Disclosures in the Allusions of the Folk of Inspiration), attributed to al-Kashani, gives a definition of the divine Names that also helps to distinguish these two connotations. Under the term Names of the Divinity, the text reads:</p>
<p>These are, in the terminology of the Sufis, expressive of the manifest aspect of being, in respect of its determination within a specific meaning. For every divine Name is the manifest aspect of being, which, in turn, is identical to the Essence, but not as the Essence is in itself; rather, in respect of its specification and determination within a particular meaning, or what one might call a quality. For example, the Name al-Hayy (The Living) is a Name of manifest, determined being, but in respect of its specification and determination as a particular meaning, it is Life. So in regard to being itself, al-Hayy is identical to the Essence, while in regard to its determination as a particular meaning, it is other than the Essence. If you have understood what we have said, you will know what is meant by the saying: the Name is not identical to the Essence, the Named, neither is it other than It.</p>
<p>The Name and the Named differ in name and in thought, and in this respect the Name is not the Named. Yet the two are also spiritually assimilated, and in this respect the Name is the Named. A further distinction must then be drawn. The spoken form of the Name is one thing; the Name as an attribute of the Essence is another. Taken as an attribute, the Name is nothing but the divine quality it designates, and that quality is in turn identical to the Essence. Taken as the name of this Name, it is merely a spoken form. Qaysari, another leading commentator on Ibn al-Arabi, brings this distinction to light in the introduction to his commentary on the Fusus al-hikam.</p>
<p>The Essence receives a Name when it is considered together with some determined quality and in relation to one of Its theophanies. The Compassionate has an essence, which is compassion; The All-Conquering has an essence, which is all-conquering power. Such spoken Names are Names of Names, and this is how the saying is to be understood: the Name is identical to the Named.</p>
<p>In his monumental commentary on Qaysari''s introduction, Jalal al-Din Ashtiyani, a contemporary authority on Shii Irfan, adds an important observation after quoting this passage. It returns the discussion to two earlier threads: extinction through the invocation of the Name, and the point that the Imams themselves are the most beautiful Names''. The saints, Ashtiyani writes, often speak of the Names as one with the Named because they have in mind the referent (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِصْدَاقٌ</span>), which is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّفَة</span>, the divine attribute or quality: The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّفَات</span> are in one respect the specifications of the Essence, while in the station of unicity they are annihilated in the Essence, and are thus identical to the Essence.</p>
<p>This metaphysical account of how the Name, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّفَة</span>, and the Essence stand to one another mirrors, on the objective and universal plane, the path the soul travels inwardly and mystically when it is extinguished in and by the invocation of the Name. This extinction is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَنَاء</span> means: the perishing of egoistic consciousness before the Absolute. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّفَات</span> are annihilated in the Essence and are thereby absolutely and unconditionally one with it; in the same way, the soul extinguished by the Name reaches at a single stroke both its total nullification and its ultimate realization — no longer as an individual soul. This extinction on the plane of the individual soul amounts to realization on the plane of transcendence only by virtue of the divine theophany, the particular divine Name, the heart that forms the soul''s true being, its mustanad ilahi. Here the deepest reality of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> comes to light. The individual no longer affirms that there is no god but God, for the individual has been extinguished by the reality that there is no reality but the one and only Reality. God is that which has no second. Just as No one has truly said Allah but Allah, so too no one realizes God but God. God alone comes to know or be Himself, and this is one aspect of the purpose of creation, as the hadith qudsi indicates: I was a hidden treasure and I loved to be known.</p>
<p>Seen in this light, the saints (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْأَوْلِيَاءُ</span>) are perfectly self-realized theophanies, wholly effaced in that of which they are the theophanic manifestations; and their very effacement in the divine Reality is the ultimate source of their knowledge of it. This is all the more true of the Prophet himself, the prototype of all the saints and the perfect man, al-insan al-kamil. On this point Sabziwari, in his commentary on the divine Names, cites the saying of Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>), We are the most beautiful Names'': There is no doubt that they are His most exalted indicators and His greatest signs (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَائِمِهِ الْعُظْمَى وَآيَاتِهِ الْكُبْرَى</span>), as the Prophet said, God bless him and his family, &quot;Whoso sees me has seen the Truth (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَقّ</span>).&quot; He goes on to equate the spiritual station, or inner reality, of the Imams with that of the divine Names and attributes, while stressing that the divine Essence transcends all Names and attributes and remains forever ineffable, known only to itself. The Prophet, the Imam, the saint, the perfect man, thus manifest those aspects of the divine nature that can be perceived on the human plane. The more the divine Essence is exalted in transcendence above all Names and attributes, the more brilliantly those Names and attributes are reflected in the mirror that the perfect man constitutes. The unknowable transcendence of the hidden treasure is, in some measure, offset by the faithful reflection of its communicable aspects in the friend of God.</p>
<p>Identifying the Imam with the divine Name or attribute has implications worth weighing, for in one respect such an identification would appear to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> — the association of a creature, however exalted, with the transcendent Reality. The same charge appears to apply to the ecstatic utterances (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَطْحِيَّاتٌ</span>) of many of the most celebrated Sufis, such as the anal-Haqq (I am the Real) of al-Hallaj and the subhani (Glory to me) of Bayazid. Yet from a viewpoint shaped by the Imam''s own conception of the divine attributes, a very different picture emerges — one that, far from being tainted by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, in fact reaches the most profound application of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. The first sermon of the Nahj al-balagha contains these remarkable and highly important statements about the attributes of God:</p>
<p>The perfection of purification (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاص</span>) is to divest Him of all attributes (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّفَات</span>) because of the testimony of every attribute that it is other than the object of attribution, and because of the testimony of every such object that it is other than the attribute. So whoever ascribes an attribute to God, glorified be He, has conjoined Him with something else; and whoever so conjoins Him has made Him twofold; and whoever makes Him twofold has fragmented Him; and whoever thus fragments Him is ignorant of Him.</p>
<p>When the Imam says that pure devotion to God requires stripping God of all attributes, what is negated is not the divine attributes themselves. What is negated is any notion of them as separate, self-standing, or added-on realities — as things substantially other than the Essence of God, or set apart from it in their very being. The reality of God''s attributes is plainly affirmed in an earlier phrase of the same sermon: He whose attribute has no binding limitation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَّذِي لَيْسَ لِصِفَتِهِ حَدٌّ مَحْدُودٌ</span>). What the sermon denies is that the reality of any divine attribute can be confined within the bounds of human thought and language. No divine attribute can stand apart from the divine nature; that nature is infinite; and so every attribute is of infinite scope as well, beyond all the limits that formal thought and verbal definition presuppose.</p>
<p>The attribute or Name, then, is to be affirmed in one respect and negated in another: affirmed as not other than the Essence, negated insofar as it is regarded in itself, on its own account. This same metaphysical view of how the attribute or Name stands to the Essence can be applied, mystically, to how the individual soul stands to the divine Reality. On its own account the individual is nothing; but in respect of the divine ground beneath it — the divine Name, the mustanad ilahi that forms its essential reality — it is not other than the unique Real. As Ibn al-Arabi puts it, The final end and ultimate return of the gnostics is that the Real is identical with them, while they do not exist. This captures perfectly the twofold aspect of the realized sage''s soul: insofar as he is aware that he does not exist, he knows that the Real is his true identity. However paradoxical it seems, the mystic''s realization of absolute Reality goes hand in hand with his realization of his own non-existence. Ibn al-Arabi expresses these two aspects of one and the same realization in compressed form. He describes the summit of his spiritual ascent, his miraj, recounting that God removed from him his contingent dimension (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمْكَانٌ</span>), resulting in the attainment of the inner realities of all the Names''. He continues:</p>
<p>Thus I attained in this nocturnal journey the inner realities (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعَانِي</span>) of all the Names, and I saw them returning to One Subject (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُسَمَّى</span>) and One Entity (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَيْنٌ</span>): that Subject was what I witnessed and that Entity was my Being. For my voyage was only in myself and pointed to myself, and through this I came to know that I was a pure servant without a trace of lordship in me at all.</p>
<p>This passage helps to explain so many of the ecstatic utterances of the Sufis and the seemingly self-deifying statements of the Imams. Their personal servitude to God is not denied. On the contrary, it is fully realized through the knowledge of their true identity, for that identity as the Named is reached only when their individuality is effaced. Like the divine attributes, they are mustahlak, annihilated in the divine Essence, so that no individual appropriation of the glory of God is possible. When the Imam appears to speak of his own greatness, this is nothing but the greatness of God expressed through him as a mazhar, a locus for the theophanic manifestation of the divine — and expressed precisely in the measure of his own effacement in that greatness.</p>
<p>The same point is reinforced by what Attar recounts about Imam Jafar al-Sadiq in his Tadhkiratul-awliya. Someone accused him of being proud, and the Imam replied: I am not proud, for pride pertains to the divine greatness. Since I have gone beyond the pride of self, His pride has come and taken the place of my own pride. With pride of self present, there can be no divine greatness, but through the divine greatness, pride will be present. The divine qualities shine with greater brilliance, not less, through the prism of a soul made transparent to the divine Reality — a transparency that comes from the dissolving of all the clouds of egoism and passion.</p>
<p>A holy utterance bears on this: My slave draws near to Me through nothing I love more than that which I have made obligatory for him. My slave never ceases to draw near to Me through supererogatory acts (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَوَافِلُ</span>) until I love him. And when I love him, I am his hearing by which he hears, his sight by which he sees, his hand by which he grasps, and his foot by which he walks.</p>
<p>In the light of all this, the paradox this divine utterance contains can now be resolved. On the one hand, the thing most beloved to God appears to be the obligatory rites. On the other, it is the supererogatory rites that carry the slave near to God until He loves him — and that love seems the greater, since it issues in the divine penetration of the human faculties, the unitive realization proper to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>. The paradox dissolves once it is seen that God''s love for the obligatory rites is a love directed toward something apart from Himself, whereas His love for the slave — the pure slave effaced in his Lord — is infinitely greater, since it shares in God''s love for His own Self-manifestation, and so for Himself. In the beginning God loved to be known, and so He created the world. In knowing Himself through the effaced slave, the seeming other, His love to be known is realized through the saint whose greatest glory is precisely his own effacement, poverty, and emptiness of self.</p>
<p>It is in this sense that the following statement of the Imam is to be understood: Down from me torrents do pour; up to me no bird can soar. Torrents pour down from him because — and in the measure that — he is extinguished in the source of the torrent. The image recalls the Quranic description of the slaves of God who, unlike the righteous'', drink directly from the celestial spring of Kafur, identified in the end with the divine Essence: Truly, the righteous shall drink from a cup flavoured with Kafur, a spring wherefrom the slaves of God drink, making it gush forth abundantly (Al-Insan: 5-6). These slaves make the spring flow more abundantly because, in al-Kashani''s words, they are themselves the sources of this spring; there is no duality or otherness. Were it otherwise, it would not be the fountain of Kafur, because of the darkness of the veil of egoity (ana iyya) and duality.</p>
<p>The final degree of the dhikr, as al-Kashani defined it, is the dhikr of the Essence, which is essential vision, attained through the removal of duality. The dhikr polishes'' away not only the impurities of the heart and mind but, in the end, through the grace of the madhkur, every taint of duality — and above all that root supposition of duality, the individual as such. It removes all subtle or hidden <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, the partners'' in being that are non-existent before the One that has no second, and it establishes the spiritual principle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span>, so that the heart, transfigured by the knowledge of the sole Reality, comes to see the light of that Reality reflected in all the mirrors of creation. To realize this one and only Reality — to make it real in the fullest sense — is, by the same stroke, to realize the unreality, or merely apparent reality, of everything else.</p>
<p>At this level of realization a measure of perplexity inevitably attends the revelation of the divine Reality on every side. The more the divine Essence is exalted beyond all comprehension, the more unavoidable it becomes that creaturely consciousness must be utterly annihilated if that Essence is to be realized, if gnosis of it is to be reached. On the one hand, one knows'' God by knowing that His Essence is unknowable, utterly other, utterly transcendent — the essence of the Creator lying beyond the veil that creation itself forms. On the other hand, the very distinction between creature and Creator is effaced once the creature has forgotten itself, not merely as a notion but existentially, in a state of genuine contemplative extinction. It is then no longer a matter of one form of self-consciousness replacing another, but of the self being taken out of the reckoning altogether, leaving only pure consciousness as such — which cannot belong to any entity without being reduced to the relativity proper to that entity. Consciousness of the Absolute, that is, being conscious of the Absolute, is thus transmuted into absolute consciousness. The Real is realized not by another but through another, which is the source of the title al-arif biLlah, literally the knower through God rather than simply the knower of God. The Imam says, Know God through God. Knowledge of God at this deepest level is the Real knowing itself through the creature; and to say through implies an unobstructed passage, the creature being marked by perfect faqr — poverty, self-effacement, and, in the end, extinction.</p>
<p>Realization through remembrance, however, means not only &quot;forgetting&quot; everything in existence except God, but also discovering that in reality there is nothing in being but God. In the Imam''s image of the &quot;polish&quot; worked by remembrance, the heart — once blind and deaf — is made able to &quot;see&quot; and to &quot;hear.&quot; It sees the Face of God wherever it turns, and it hears the cosmic hymn of remembrance and praise that the whole of creation sings, for every creature proclaims the boundless creativity of its Creator. As the Quran says:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 115</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِلَّهِ ٱلْمَشْرِقُ وَٱلْمَغْرِبُ ۚ فَأَيْنَمَا تُوَلُّوا۟ فَثَمَّ وَجْهُ ٱللَّهِ ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ وَسِعٌ عَلِيمٌۭ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Wherever ye turn, there is the Face of God&quot; (Al-Baqarah: 115).</p></blockquote>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">An-Nur: 41</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُسَبِّحُ لَهُۥ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱلطَّيْرُ صَٓفَّتٍۢ ۖ كُلٌّۭ قَدْ عَلِمَ صَلَاتَهُۥ وَتَسْبِيحَهُۥ ۗ وَٱللَّهُ عَلِيمٌۢ بِمَا يَفْعَلُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;Do you not see that God is the one who is glorified by everything in the heavens and the earth, and the birds with wings outstretched; each one indeed knows its prayer and its glorification, and God is All-Aware of what they do&quot; (An-Nur: 41).</p></blockquote>
<p>A final reflection turns to a passage from the Imam''s famous and much-disputed Khutbat al-bayan, &quot;The Sermon of Clarifying Expression&quot;:</p>
<p>I am the Sign of the All-Powerful. I am the gnosis of the mysteries. I am the Threshold of Thresholds. I am the companion of the radiance of the divine Majesty. I am the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden. I am the Face of God. I am the mirror of God, the supreme Pen, the Tabula secreta. I am he who in the Gospel is called Elijah. I am he who is in possession of the secret of God''s Messenger.</p>
<p>This remarkable affirmation of the Imam''s inmost reality may seem shocking to the exoteric mentality, and is all too easily misread as a blasphemous self-deification of the kind associated with Hallaj. One key to understanding these declarations of &quot;his&quot; true nature correctly is the statement: &quot;I am the gnosis of the mysteries.&quot; Once this is grasped, every other statement falls naturally into place. For here the Imam is not identifying himself with God, but with the knowledge of God — and not with his own knowledge of God, since that would still be a relative knowledge, belonging to him as a relative being. Only knowledge itself — the knowledge God has of Himself, not the knowledge a human being has of God — can be fully identified with its object. The distinctions between knowledge, knower, and known dissolve only at this transcendent level, just as the distinction between knowledge and being ceases to operate there. Thus the knower is the known, when the knowledge in question is that consciousness which the divine Reality has of itself. Individual consciousness reaches this knowledge only on one condition: the utter extinction of every trace of individual, creaturely selfhood. To &quot;remember&quot; God, one must have &quot;forgotten&quot; oneself. To &quot;be&quot; the knowledge of God is, by a kind of metaphysical reverberation, to &quot;be&quot; everything that God is; for the consciousness that is wholly at one with the Absolute is nothing other than &quot;the radiance of the divine Majesty.&quot; Insofar as this consciousness is one with &quot;the gnosis of the mysteries&quot; — and these mysteries (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسْرَارٌ</span>) are all rooted in the ultimate mystery (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلسِّرُّ</span>), the one and only Essence of God — it is necessarily one with all the divine attributes. It is in this sense that the Imam can proclaim: &quot;I am the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden.&quot;</p>
<p>The same reasoning applies to the claim to be &quot;the Face of God&quot; (wajh Allah). It becomes intelligible only once one grasps that the Imam describes himself as the &quot;mirror&quot; of God. A mirror loses its identity as a mirror when attention rests entirely on the object reflected in it: the mirror''s own surface becomes, as it were, invisible, and only the object is seen. Conversely, when the mirror itself becomes the object of attention, the face reflected in it can no longer be seen. The mirror is thus identified with what it reflects — which in a certain sense is &quot;in&quot; the mirror — yet the mirror does not cease to be a mirror, and the Face does not cease to be wholly independent of it. The &quot;veil&quot; here is the mirror itself. To attend to the mirror as a mirror prevents one from seeing the object it reflects; but once attention rests wholly on the image the mirror holds, the mirror ceases to be a veil and instead reveals the object itself. Within this symbolism, forgetting the mirror answers to the effacement of the self, and fixing on the image within it answers to the remembrance of God.</p>
<p>Yet this leads to another paradox: the deepest effacement of individuality goes hand in hand with the most perfect expression of humanity. The &quot;friends of God,&quot; who know themselves to be nothing before the Absolute, are precisely those who most faithfully reflect the divine Names and qualities — qualities that belong not to them but to God. This is why the great saints and sages are also models of virtue, virtue whose true foundations are the &quot;most beautiful Names&quot; and attributes of God. Those divine attributes shine through them most clearly because the mirrors of their hearts have been polished by the remembrance of God, and the most stubborn stains — those left by egotism — have been removed. The saint''s creativity is raised to its highest pitch precisely because the divine qualities reflected by the heart are no longer blocked — on the inner side, by the individual coagulations that egotism generates, nor, on the outer side, by the cosmic coagulations of this lower world. For such saints are those whose &quot;hearts are in the Gardens [of Paradise] while their bodies are at work.&quot; Their hearts, bound and attached to that divine realm — the abodes and stations of paradise reaching all the way to the divine presence — are already &quot;within&quot; that transcendent realm; they already live more in that spiritual world than in this earthly one. And so, though they labor in this world with their bodies, their outward actions veil their inward transcendence of higher realities: they act, but they are not bound by the fruits of their action.</p>
<p>Such souls have not merely understood this principle; they have been seized by it, in their very existence, at the root of their being. It is the principle voiced in the Quranic verse:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-''Ankabut: 64</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا هَذِهِ ٱلْحَيَوٰةُ ٱلدُّنْيَآ إِلَّا لَهْوٌۭ وَلَعِبٌۭ ۚ وَإِنَّ ٱلدَّارَ ٱلْآخِرَةَ لَهِىَ ٱلْحَيَوَانُ ۚ لَوْ كَانُوا۟ يَعْلَمُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">&quot;And the life of this world is naught but sport and play; and truly, the abode of the Hereafter, that is true life&quot; (Al-Ankabut: 64).</p></blockquote>
<p>Their instincts are therefore always attuned to that higher reality. When Ali was struck by the poisoned sword of Ibn Muljam, the cry that broke from him was not one of pain but of triumph: &quot;I have triumphed, by the Lord of the Kaba!&quot; At the very moment of death there was in him nothing but the remembrance of God, and the Imam passed away, it is said, with his tongue still repeating the words la ilaha illaLlah. His death was thus completed exactly as his life had been lived, in accord with the wisdom of the Prophet, who, when asked which of all acts is the most meritorious, replied: &quot;That you die while your tongue is moistened with the invocation of God.&quot;</p>
<p>All the rites and practices bound up with the chivalric orders of Islam were traced, without exception, back to the Prophet''s son-in-law, Ali, who was thereby established as the supreme source of the virtues of futuwwa, as Muhammad Jafar Mahjub observes in his &quot;Chivalry and Early Persian Sufism.&quot; S. H. Nasr writes to similar effect that Ali, having received the &quot;truth and power&quot; of futuwwa from the Prophet, &quot;became the supreme source of futuwwa in Islam for both Sunnis and Shiites... The personality of Ali, at once sage and knight, contemplative and protector of laborers and craftsmen, has continued to dominate through the centuries over the horizon of futuwwa as it has over much of Sufism.&quot; Nasr also draws attention to the organic link between futuwwa and the guilds, the asnaf, singular sinf, of craftsmen and artists established throughout the length and breadth of the Muslim world.</p>
<p>It is often said that the Naqshabandi tariqa is the one exception to this rule, tracing its line of descent from the Prophet through the first caliph, Abu Bakr, rather than through Ali. But this affiliation with Abu Bakr belongs to only one of its three principal silsilas. Abd al-Majid b. Muhammad al-Khani, himself a Naqshabandi-Khalidi shaykh, calls the first chain (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السِّلْسِلَةُ الْأُولَى</span>) the &quot;golden chain,&quot; which begins with Ali and runs through the Shii Imams down to Ali b. Musa, then to Maruf al-Karkhi and the other masters. The second chain (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السِّلْسِلَةُ الثَّانِيَةُ</span>) likewise begins with Ali, running through Hasan al-Basri. Only the third chain, called al-silsilat al-siddiqiyya, runs from the Prophet to Abu Bakr (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصِّدِّيقُ</span>).</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'extinction and the name: the death of the saint';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The opening sermon of ⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نھج البلاغہ</span>⟫ — delivered by Maulana Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) as a ⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خطبۃ</span>⟫ — proclaims the oneness of God: a oneness that stands outside the category of numbers altogether. The sermon is built on paradox. The loftier the truth Maulana Ali means to reveal, the deeper the paradox he forms to reveal it. By negating the reach of human words and concepts, he affirms the Reality that no word and no concept can contain. That is the manner of his sermons. The eye cannot see Him, yet the mind cannot deny Him. He is farther than the farthest flight of imagination, yet nearer than the jugular vein. Nowhere is this plainer than in these opening lines.</p>
<p>The sermon opens: Praise be to God, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحَمْدُ للهِ الَّذَي لاَ يَبْلُغُ مِدْحَتَهُ القَائِلُونَ</span> — whose laudation those who speak cannot deliver. The paradox is immediate. Speech exists to carry a concept from one mind to another; here it is turned to demonstrate that God is so lofty and sublime that words can carry nothing of Him. Whose graces those who count cannot number, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلاِ يُحْصِي نَعْمَاءَهُ العَادُّونَ</span> — for counting belongs to things that can be enumerated, and His bounties surpass all enumeration. Whose rightful due those who strive cannot render, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولاَ يُؤَدِّي حَقَّهُ الُمجْتَهِدُونَ</span> — for however hard they strive, the praise and gratitude He is owed remains forever beyond their reach. He cannot be reached by far-reaching aspirations, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَّذِي لاَ يُدْركُهُ بُعْدُ الهِمَمِ</span>, nor fathomed by profound intuitions, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلاَ يَنَالُهُ غَوْصُ الفِطَنِ</span>.</p>
<p>He whose attribute admits no binding limitation, no settled description, no appointed time, no extended term — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَّذِي لَيْسَ لِصِفَتِهِ حَدٌّ مَحْدُودٌ، وَلاَ نَعْتٌ مَوْجُودٌ، وَلا وَقْتٌ مَعْدُودٌ</span> — so that His attribute cannot be confined within a definition and yet is affirmed as real, described in the very words of revelation even though no description can capture Him. Through His power He brought all created things into being; through His mercy He sent the comforting winds abroad; and with rocky pillars He entrenched His swaying earth.</p>
<p>Foremost in religion is knowledge of Him. The perfection of that knowledge is belief in Him; the perfection of belief is affirming His oneness; and the perfection of that affirmation is to purify one''s devotion to Him — the perfection of sincerity, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أخلاص</span> — which means rendering both one''s devotion and one''s very conception of God entirely pure. The perfection of that purification is to divest Him of all attributes (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْیُ الصِّفَۃِ</span>), because every attribute bears witness that it is other than the thing it is attributed to, and every such thing bears witness that it is other than the attribute.</p>
<p>So whoever ascribes an attribute to God, glorified be He, has conjoined Him with something else. Whoever conjoins Him has made Him twofold. Whoever makes Him twofold has fragmented Him. And whoever fragments Him is ignorant of Him. Whoever points to Him confines Him. Whoever confines Him counts Him. Whoever asks &quot;in what?&quot; encloses Him. And whoever asks &quot;upon what?&quot; isolates Him.</p>
<p>The very attributes by which He is named in revelation — that He is the All-Compassionate (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَٰن</span>), the All-Merciful (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رحیم</span>), the Almighty (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عزیز</span>) — are affirmed as real and yet cannot be defined, bounded, or described. He is merciful, but not as any mercy the human mind can picture; powerful, but not as any storm or flood or force that creation has known. Reason itself &quot;de-fines,&quot; drawing a boundary, a ⟪ar:<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حد</span>⟫, around whatever it grasps. In breaking that boundary, the sermon opens an awareness beyond the reach of speculative thought: by negating the limits of human words, it affirms the Absolute that has no limit. This is the very movement of the testimony of faith — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لا الہ</span>, &quot;no deity,&quot; which is the negation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفی</span>), and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الا اللہ</span>, &quot;except God,&quot; which is the affirmation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِثْبَات</span>). Each attribute is negated only insofar as the limited mind conceives it, and is thereby affirmed as nothing but God Himself: La Rahmana illaLlah, there is no All-Compassionate but God; La Raheema illaLlah, there is no All-Merciful but God. Thus is God glorified above all that the mind would attribute to Him: &quot;‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَنَ ٱللَّهِ عَمَّا يَصِفُونَ</span>,&quot; &quot;Glorified is God above all that they attribute to Him!&quot; (Surat al-Muminun, 23:91).</p>
<p>Being, but not by way of any becoming; existing, but not from having been non-existent; with every thing, but not through association; other than every thing, but not through separation; acting, but not through movements and instruments; seeing, even when nothing of His creation was to be seen; solitary, even when there was none whose intimacy might be sought or whose absence might be missed.</p>
<p>He brought forth creation, originating it from the very beginning, without pondering over any deliberation or benefiting from any previous experience, without initiating any movement or engaging in any unsettling exertion. He assigned to all things their span of time and harmonized their differences. He instilled within them their natural dispositions, impressing upon them their respective forms, knowing them before their genesis, encompassing their boundaries and their finalities, and aware of their configurations and their contours.</p>
<p>Then He, glorified be He, created a fissure in the atmospheres, cleaving apart directions and the uppermost levels of air, and caused water to flow therein — a current of surging waves swelling and swirling in overflowing abundance. He mounted it upon a tempestuous wind, a thunderous gale, then made that wind prevent the return of the water, enabling it to dominate the violence of its flow and aligning it with the boundaries of the water. The air beneath the wind spread wide open, while the water above flowed copiously.</p>
<p>Then He raised up another wind, making it sterile, fixing its course, intensifying its current, and distancing it from its source. He ordered it to agitate the swelling waters and to stir up oceanic waves. The wind churned up the waters as one churns milk in a milkbag, imparting to the water its own violence in the atmosphere, turning its foremost back upon its hindmost and its stillness into motion, so that its torrents surged and its swelling waves threw up foam. He then elevated that foam within a cleft air and a vast open atmosphere, and formed from it the seven heavens, making the lowest a suspended wave and the highest a protected shelter and an elevated ceiling, without any pillars to support them or any clinch to transfix them.</p>
<p>Then He adorned the heavens with the ornaments of the stars and the effulgence of the firmament, and made a radiant lamp — the sun — and an illuminating moon to follow an orbit in a revolving sphere, beneath a roof in motion and a firmament in flux.</p>
<p>Then He split up the highest heavens and filled them with angels of different dispositions:</p>
<ul>
<li>some prostrating and not bowing; others bowing and not standing upright;</li>
<li>some arrayed in ranks, not separating from one another;</li>
<li>others glorifying, never wearying, not overcome by sleepiness in the eyes, distraction in the mind, fatigue of the body, or the negligence of forgetfulness.</li>
</ul>
<p>Among them are custodians of His Revelation and conveyors of His Words to His Messengers, and those who come and go with His Decree and His Command. Among them are the guardians of His slaves and the wardens of the gates of His Gardens. And among them are those whose feet stand firmly on the lowest worlds, whose necks surpass the highest heaven, whose sides exceed all bounds, and whose shoulders are proportioned to the pillars of the Throne — their eyes cast down before it, enwrapping themselves beneath it in their wings. Between them and all others there is a barrier: veils of Glory and screens of Might. They do not imagine their Lord through formal representation, nor ascribe to Him created attributes. They do not confine Him within places, nor allude to Him through similitudes.</p>
<p>Then He, glorified be He, gathered up a quantity of dust — from some hard earth and some soft, some sweet and some salty — and poured water onto it until it was pure, then kneaded into it moisture until it held together. From this He fashioned a form with curves and joints, limbs and parts, compacting it until it held fast, solidifying it until it became as sounding clay, and assigning to it a measured duration and a known end. Then He breathed into it of His spirit, and it rose up as a human being, endowed with intellectual powers for reflection, thoughts by which to conduct himself, limbs to put to use, instruments at his disposal, and knowledge with which to discern between true and false, between different tastes and fragrances, and between different colors and kinds — a constitution compounded of different hues, unified forms, contrasting oppositions, and distinct admixtures of heat and cold, moisture and dryness.</p>
<p>Then God, glorified be He, demanded of the angels the fulfillment of what He had charged them with, and their compliance with His injunction, by prostrating in submission before Adam and humbling themselves before him. So He said, &quot;Prostrate to Adam,&quot; and they prostrated — all except Iblis. Passionate scorn deranged him and wretchedness overwhelmed him; he took pride in his constitution of fire and despised the creature made of clay. Then God granted him a respite, so that His wrath would be fully warranted, the affliction consummated, and the promise fulfilled. So He said, &quot;You are among those given respite until a day whose time is appointed.&quot;</p>
<p>Then He, glorified be He, settled Adam in an abode where He made his life comfortable and his domain secure, and warned him of Iblis and his enmity. But his enemy beguiled him, driven by envious desire for Adam''s immortal dwelling and his intimacy with the righteous. So Adam traded certainty for doubt and resolution for weakness, and replaced joy with dread and self-esteem with remorse. Then God accepted his repentance, enveloped him in a word of His mercy, and promised him a return to His Garden, before sending him down to the abode of tribulations and the begetting of progeny.</p>
<p>From Adam''s offspring God chose prophets, taking from them a pledge concerning the Revelation and entrusting them with the conveyance of His message, at a time when most of His creatures had strayed from their covenant with God — ignoring His due, setting up rivals alongside Him, while the demons distracted them from knowledge of Him and severed them from worship of Him. So He sent among them His messengers and dispatched prophets in succession, to demand of His creatures the fulfillment of their covenant, to remind them of His forgotten graces, to remonstrate with them through the communication of His Revelation, to unearth for them the buried treasures of the intellect, and to show them signs of His omnipotence: the outspread earth laid low beneath their feet, provisions that give them life, endings that mete out death, ailments hastening their decline, and a succession of events that flows over them.</p>
<p>God never left His creation without a prophet sent forth, a revealed scripture, a compelling proof, or a clear path. These messengers were not weakened by the scarcity of their numbers or by the multitude of those who denied them. Those who came earlier were told the names of those who would follow, and those who came later were informed of those who had gone before. In this way generations were born and ages elapsed, fathers passing away and sons succeeding them, until God sent forth Muhammad, Messenger of God, in fulfillment of His promise and in completion of the cycle of prophecy — he concerning whom a pledge had been taken from the prophets, known through his signs, of noble birth.</p>
<p>At that time the people of the earth were divided into different religious communities; caprice was rampant and pathways were in disorder, among them the path of those who likened God to His creation, distorted His name, or pointed to other than Him. God guided them through His Prophet out of their error and, through his presence, delivered them from their ignorance.</p>
<p>Then God chose for Muhammad the meeting with Him, and desired for him what was with Himself. He removed him with dignity from the abode of this lower world and drew him away from the realm of trial, taking him to Himself in honor. The Prophet left behind among them what the prophets leave for their communities, so that they would not be abandoned to wander without a clear path or a visible beacon: the scripture of their Lord, elucidating</p>
<ul>
<li>what is lawful and unlawful,</li>
<li>what is obligatory and what is recommended,</li>
<li>the abrogating verses and the abrogated,</li>
<li>the conditionally permissible and the unconditionally compulsory,</li>
<li>the specific and the general,</li>
<li>His admonitions and His analogies,</li>
<li>the undelimited and the delimited,</li>
<li>the unambiguous and the equivocal,</li>
</ul>
<p>making plain what is condensed and clarifying what is obscure.</p>
<p>In it are matters whose knowledge is binding upon God''s servants, and others whose ignorance is overlooked. There are injunctions affirmed as obligatory in the scripture yet known to have been abrogated by the Sunna, and actions made obligatory by the Sunna from which the scripture has nonetheless permitted abstention. There are duties incumbent upon a person at their prescribed time that cease to be incumbent once that time has passed. The scripture also draws a distinction between forbidden acts: the major sins, with which He has threatened punishment by His fire, and the minor ones, for which He has prepared His forgiveness. And it describes acts that are acceptable when performed to a modest degree and equally acceptable when performed to a higher degree, without either being obligatory.</p>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'the first sermon of the nahj al-balagha';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>The Imam wrote this letter to Malik al-Ashtar when he appointed him governor of Egypt and its regions, at a time when the rule of the previous governor, Muhammad b. Abi Bakr, had grown unstable. It is the longest of his letters, and the richest of them in both beauty of form and excellence of meaning.</p>
<p>In the Name of God, the Compassionate, the Merciful.</p>
<p>This is what the servant of God, Ali (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>), Commander of the Faithful, commands Malik b. al-Harith al-Ashtar in his mandate (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عہد</span>) of appointment, upon making him governor of Egypt: to collect its revenues, to fight its enemies, to seek the welfare of its people, and to bring prosperity to its lands.</p>
<p>He commands him to fear God, to place obedience to God before all else, and to hold fast to what He has commanded in His Book, both the obligatory acts and the recommended ones — for no one attains virtue except by following them, and no one becomes wretched except by denying and neglecting them. He commands him to assist God with his heart, his hand, and his tongue, for God, majestic is His name, has undertaken to grant victory to whoever assists Him and to exalt whoever glorifies Him. And he commands him to curb the passions of his soul and to hold it back when whim and caprice run high, for the soul incites to evil unless God shows mercy.</p>
<p>Know, Malik, that I am sending you to a land that has known governments before yours, some just and some unjust. The people will now weigh your conduct just as you once weighed the conduct of the rulers before you, and they will speak of you just as you spoke of them. The righteous are known only through what God causes to be said of them on the tongues of His servants. So let the treasure dearest to you be the treasure of good deeds. Master your desires, and hold yourself back from whatever is not lawful to you, for such restraint is the soul dealing fairly with itself, keeping it balanced between what it likes and what it dislikes.</p>
<p>Habituate your heart to mercy, love, and kindness toward your subjects. Do not stand over them like a ravening beast that counts them fair prey, for they are of two kinds: either your brother in religion or your equal in creation. Mistakes slip from them and faults befall them, whether by intention or by accident. So extend to them your forgiveness and your pardon, just as you would wish God to extend to you His forgiveness and pardon; for you stand above them, the one set in authority over you stands above you, and God stands above the one who appointed you. He has asked you to manage their affairs, and He is testing you through them.</p>
<p>Do not set yourself on a course of war with God, for you have no strength against His retribution and cannot do without His forgiveness and mercy. Never regret a pardon, and never rejoice in a punishment. Do not act in haste on any impulse when a way around it can still be found. Do not say, &quot;I have been given authority, so I command and am obeyed,&quot; for that breeds corruption in the heart, weakens religion, and draws misfortune near. If the authority you hold breeds in you pride or vanity, look to the greatness of God''s dominion above you and to His power over you — a power the like of which you do not hold even over yourself. This will settle your ambition, restrain your vehemence, and restore to you the good sense that had strayed. Beware of matching yourself with God in greatness or likening yourself to Him in might, for God humbles every tyrant and disgraces every braggart.</p>
<p>Be just with God and be just with people, granting them their due from yourself, from your close kin, and from those of your subjects for whom you feel fondness; for if you do not, you are an oppressor. Whoever oppresses the servants of God has God for his adversary, and not merely His servants; and whomever God opposes, He refutes his plea, and that man remains at war with God until he desists and repents. Nothing so surely hastens the withdrawal of God''s grace or the coming of His retribution as persistence in oppression, for God hears the cry of the oppressed and lies in wait for the oppressors.</p>
<p>Let the dearest of your concerns be those most balanced in truth, most complete in justice, and most inclusive of your subjects'' contentment; for the discontent of the common folk (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعَامَّةُ</span>) cancels the contentment of the elite (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْخَاصَّةُ</span>), while the discontent of the elite is absorbed within the contentment of the common folk. In times of ease no one is a heavier burden to a ruler than the elite, and in hardship none less helpful; none is more averse to fair treatment, more insistent in asking for favors, less grateful when given them, slower to accept an excuse when refused, or weaker in patience under the blows of fortune. It is the common folk of the community who are the pillar of religion, the mass of the Muslims, and the defense against enemies; so let your inclination and your leaning be toward them.</p>
<p>Let those among your subjects who most eagerly hunt out the faults of others be the furthest from you and the most despised in your eyes, for people do have faults, and the ruler is the one most bound to conceal them. Do not expose what is hidden from you; your duty is only to correct what has become plain, and God will judge whatever remains hidden. Veil people''s failings as much as you can, and God will veil from your subjects those failings of your own that you would wish kept concealed. Loosen from the people every knot of resentment, and cut away from your own heart the cord of every enmity. Turn a blind eye to whatever is unclear to you, and do not be quick to believe a slanderer, for a slanderer is a deceiver, however sincerely he may seem to give counsel.</p>
<p>Do not admit a miser into your counsel, for he will turn you away from generosity and frighten you with poverty; nor a coward, for he will make you feel too weak for your affairs; nor a greedy man, for he will dress up avidity as though it were justice. Miserliness, cowardice, and greed are separate traits, yet a single bad opinion of God unites them. The worst of your ministers is the one who served evil rulers before you and shared in their sins. Do not let such men into your inner circle, for they are the helpers of sinners and the brothers of tyrants. You can find good replacements for them among men of equal intelligence and capability who carry none of that burden of sin, who never helped a tyrant in his tyranny or a sinner in his sin. Such men will cost you the least, support you the best, incline most warmly toward you, and attach themselves least to anyone but you; so choose them as your intimates, in private and in public.</p>
<p>Among these, give the highest place to the one who most readily speaks the bitter truth to you and gives you the least encouragement in whatever you do that God dislikes for His friends, however sharply that strikes at your own desires. Attach yourself to people of piety and truthfulness, and train them not to flatter you or to gladden you with praise for something you never did, for lavish praise breeds conceit and carries a person toward vanity. Never set the good and the wicked on the same footing before you, for that would make the good think little of their goodness and let the wicked settle deeper into their wickedness; hold each of them to what he has bound himself to. Know that nothing so builds a ruler''s confidence in his subjects as his kindness toward them, his easing of their burdens, and his refusal to press them beyond what they can bear. So conduct yourself in a way that earns their trust, for that trust will spare you long trouble; the one most deserving of your good opinion is the one you have treated well, and the one most deserving of your distrust is the one you have treated badly.</p>
<p>Do not break any good tradition set by the leaders of this community, by which harmony was knit together and the subjects were set right; and do not bring in any new practice that harms the traditions already in place, for then the reward for the old traditions will go to the one who founded them, while the burden of breaking them will fall on you. Study much with the scholars (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَعُلَمَاءِ</span>) and hold much discourse with the sages (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحُكَمَاءِ</span>), so as to strengthen what keeps your lands sound and to uphold what those before you had already set right.</p>
<p>Know that the subjects are made up of several classes, no one of which can be sound without the others, and no one of which can do without the rest. Among them are the soldiers of God (junud Allah); the scribes (kuttab, sing. katib) who serve the common people and the elite; the judges who administer justice (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُضَاةُ الْعَدْلِ</span>); the officials who uphold fairness and secure the right of redress (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِفْقٌ</span>); those who pay the poll-tax (jizya, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جزیۃ</span>) and the land-tax (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَرَج</span>) from among the protected people (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَهْلُ الذِّمَّةِ</span>) and the Muslims; the merchants and artisans; and the lowest class, the needy and the destitute. To each of these God has assigned its share, and He has fixed its bounds and its duties in His Book or in the Sunna (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سنۃ</span>) of His Prophet, God bless him and his family, as a binding covenant (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عہد</span>) from Him, kept safe with us.</p>
<p>As for the soldiers, they are, by the grace of God, the fortresses of the subjects, the adornment of the governors, the strength of religion, and the pathways to security (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبُلُ الْأَمْنِ</span>). The subjects cannot sustain themselves except through the soldiers, and the soldiers in their turn cannot be sustained except through the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَرَج</span>, the revenue God brings forth for them; by it they gain the strength to wage war on their enemies, on it they depend for their own welfare, and through it they meet their needs. Neither of these two classes can be maintained without the third: the judges, the administrators, and the scribes, who uphold every contract, gather and order all interests and benefits, and are entrusted with the affairs of state, particular and general alike. None of these can function without the merchants and artisans, who gather the goods that are needed, set up the markets, and by their own labor supply what the work of others could not procure. Last comes the lowest class, the needy and the destitute, whose relief and support is an obligation owed to them. For every one of these classes there is abundance with God, and every one has upon the governor a right measured by what its welfare requires. The governor cannot fully discharge the duties God has laid upon him except through earnest striving and seeking God''s help (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الِاهْتِمَامُ وَالِاسْتِعَانَةُ</span>), training himself to hold fast to what is right, and bearing patiently whatever comes his way, be it easy or hard.</p>
<p>Appoint as commander of your soldiers the man you feel most deeply to be the most sincere toward God, the Prophet, and your Imam, the purest of heart and the most excellent in forbearance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِلْم</span>): one who is slow to anger, glad to pardon, kind to the weak, and firm with the strong; neither driven by violence nor held back by weakness. Hold fast to men of noble descent, from pious families of established name and repute, and to those known for their bravery, their courage, their generosity (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّخَاءُ</span>), and their tolerance (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّمَاحَةُ</span>), for they are a group formed by nobility and a party made of honor. Then oversee their affairs as parents oversee their child. Let no act by which you strengthen them seem too great in your eyes, and do not belittle any kindness, however slight, that you have promised them, for such kindness is a summoner (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَاعِيَةٌ</span>) calling them to give you good counsel and to place their trust in you. Do not neglect the close inspection of their affairs for the sake of matters that seem weightier, for there are small acts of kindness that benefit them greatly, and there are weightier matters that cannot be set aside.</p>
<p>Let the most favored of your commanders be the one most generous to the people and most open-handed with his bounty, so that they and their dependants have ample means. Let their concern be gathered into a single resolve: to fight the enemy. Your kindness toward them will incline their hearts toward you.</p>
<p>The greatest source of joy for governors is the establishment of justice in the land and the flow of love from their subjects. That love will not appear unless the subjects'' hearts are secure, and their counsel will not be sound unless the governor watches over those charged with their welfare and makes the burden of his rule light and easy to bear, so that they no longer anxiously hope for it to end. Raise their hopes, praise them continually, and recount the brave deeds of those who have been brave, for abundant praise of noble acts rouses the courageous and spurs on the sluggish, if God wills. Acknowledge the courageous deed of every man; do not credit one man''s deed to another, and do not fall short in granting anyone his due. Do not let a man''s nobility lead you to reckon a small accomplishment great, nor a man''s low station lead you to reckon a great one small. Refer to God and His Messenger any matter that weighs heavily upon you or is unclear to you, for God the Exalted has said to a people whom He loved to guide: O ye who believe, obey God and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you. And if ye dispute with one another over anything, then refer it to God and the Messenger (An-Nisa: 59). To refer to God is to follow what is clear and unequivocal in His Book; to refer to the Messenger is to follow that part of his Sunna which unites rather than that which divides.</p>
<p>For the settlement of disputes among the people, choose the one you hold to be the most excellent of your subjects: one whom complex matters do not confound and whom quarrelling litigants do not enrage; who does not persist in error, and does not shrink from returning to the truth once he perceives it; whose soul is not drawn toward greed; who is not content with a shallow understanding but probes to the depths; who is most cautious before ambiguities, most firm in holding to the proofs, least wearied by litigants returning again and again, most patient in uncovering the true state of affairs, and most resolute once the right judgment is plain; whom praise does not make vain and whom enticement does not sway. Such people are indeed rare.</p>
<p>Then examine carefully and often the carrying-out of the judge''s verdicts, and be generous in what you pay him, so that want is removed and his need to seek help from others is lessened. Give him a rank of nearness to yourself that none of your other close companions might hope to attain, so that he is safe in your presence and shielded from the attacks of other influential men. Scrutinize all of this with a piercing scrutiny, for this religion had been held captive in the hands of evil people, abused by them for their own caprice and exploited by them for the sake of this world.</p>
<p>Then look into the affairs of your administrators. Employ them only after testing them, and do not appoint anyone out of partiality or favouritism, for these two are a wellspring of every kind of injustice and treachery. Seek out men of proven experience and modesty, from righteous households and families of precedence in Islam, for they are the most noble in character, the most rooted in dignity, the least inclined toward greed, and the most mindful of how all things end. Then provide them with a generous livelihood, for this gives them the strength to keep themselves in good order, frees them from reaching into the funds in their charge, and stands as an argument against them should they disobey your orders or betray your trust. Examine their actions carefully by appointing over them supervisors known for their sincerity and reliability, for your quiet watching of their affairs will move them to keep faith with their trust and to be gentle with the subjects. Keep watch over your assistants as well: if any one of them stretches out his hand toward a treacherous deed, and the reports of your watchers agree upon it so that you hold it sufficient evidence, then lay corporal punishment upon him, take him to account for what his act has brought about, set him in a place of disgrace, brand him with the charge of treachery, and gird him with the shame of the accusation.</p>
<p>Look also into the matter of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَرَج</span> land-tax, in such a way that those subject to it are kept in a proper state of well-being, for it is from their welfare, and from the sound collection of the tax itself, that the good of all others is drawn. There can be no welfare of the rest except through them, since all without exception depend upon the revenues of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَرَج</span> and upon those who pay it. Let your concern for the cultivation of the land outweigh your concern for collecting the tax, for no tax will be gathered where there is no cultivation. Whoever exacts the tax without cultivating the land ruins the land and destroys the people, and his rule will not endure for long. So if the people complain of a heavy burden, or of some blight, or of the cutting off of irrigation, or of a lack of rain, or of a change in the condition of the soil from flooding or drought, then lighten their burden in the measure that you hope will set their situation right. Do not let any relief you grant them weigh heavily upon you, for it is an investment that will return to you in the prosperity of your land, the adornment of your rule, the finest praise you reap from them, and the pride you take in dispensing justice among them. You may then rely upon an increase in their capacity as a result of what you invested in them when you gave them respite, and you may trust them, having accustomed them to your justice and your kindness.</p>
<p>It is possible that a situation might arise in which you depend upon them, and they would then bear this responsibility gladly, for a prosperous land can sustain whatever burden you place upon it. The devastation of the land comes about only through the destitution of its inhabitants, and the destitution of its inhabitants comes about only when the desire to amass wealth rules the souls of the governors, when they have doubts about what endures, and when they profit little from exemplary teachings.</p>
<p>Now consider with care the situation of your scribes. Appoint the best of them to manage your affairs. Assign those letters containing the most sensitive strategies and secrets to the scribe in whom you find the most comprehensive moral qualities: one who will not be emboldened by his elevated status to contradict you in public; one who is not negligent in delivering to you the correspondence of your officials and properly despatching your replies; one who takes and gives on your behalf with care. He must not weaken any agreement contracted for you, nor be incapable of repudiating what has been contracted to your detriment. He must not be ignorant of the value of his own capabilities, for one who is unaware of his own worth will be even more ignorant of the worth of others. Do not make your selection solely on the basis of your own instinct, confidence, and trust, for men know how to present themselves to rulers through affectation and good service that conceals neither sincerity nor fidelity. Rather, test them by what they did in the service of good people who came before you, and appoint the one whose virtue left the deepest impression upon the common people and whose reputation for integrity is most widely known. This will be proof of your sincerity toward God and toward the one on whose behalf you have been appointed to this office, for you will be putting the right man in the right place, and in so doing enacting justice.</p>
<p>Appoint for each of your affairs a head officer among your officials, one who will not be daunted by the magnitude of affairs nor scattered by their multiplicity. Any fault of your scribes which you have overlooked will be ascribed to you.</p>
<p>Then attend to the merchants and artisans, treating them well and urging the same on others, those of fixed abode, those who travel with their goods, and those who earn their livelihood with their hands. They are sources of benefit and the means by which conveniences are obtained. Through them, goods are procured from distant and remote places, brought by land and sea, from plains and mountains, and from places where men neither settle nor even dare to venture. They live in a state of reconciliation (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِلْمٌ</span>), so no calamity need be feared from them, and in a state of peace (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صُلْحٌ</span>), so no disturbance need be expected. Examine their affairs attentively, both those close to you and those in every corner of your land. Yet be aware that, despite what has been said, many of them harbor a despicable stinginess, a repugnant greed, a propensity to hoard goods, and a tendency to fix prices arbitrarily. All of this brings loss to the people and is a source of shame for governors. So prohibit hoarding, for the Messenger of God <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> prohibited it. Let trading be carried out with propriety and fairness, at prices that harm neither party, buyer nor seller. If any one of them succumbs to the temptation to hoard after you have prohibited it, inflict upon him an exemplary but not excessive punishment.</p>
<p>Then, O God, O God, pay particular attention to the lowest class: those who have no wherewithal, the destitute, the needy, the afflicted, the disabled. Within this class are those who beg and those whose wretchedness calls out to be alleviated but who do not beg. Be mindful of God in regard to their rights, for He has entrusted those rights to your care. Assign to them a portion from your public treasury (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَيْتُ الْمَالِ</span>) and a portion of the produce of what is taken as booty by the Muslims in every region, for those who are furthest away have the same rights as those nearest. Upholding the right of each of them is incumbent upon you. Do not let any haughtiness on your part cause you to neglect them, for you will not be pardoned even the slightest shortcoming toward them on account of attending to some seemingly more important matter. Do not turn your concern away from them or assume a contemptuous attitude toward them. Keep a watchful eye over the affairs of those who cannot gain access to you and who are disdained by men of high standing. Appoint from among those you trust a God-fearing and humble person to bring their affairs to your attention. Treat these people in a manner such that God may excuse you on the day you meet Him, for they are more in need of justice from you than any others among your subjects. Offer your excuse to God in respect of fulfilling each one''s right. Assume responsibility for the orphans and the elderly who have no resources and yet cannot bring themselves to beg. This is onerous for governors, and the fulfillment of all rights is onerous, but God makes it light for those who aspire to the Hereafter, who restrain their souls in patience, and trust in the truth of what God has promised them.</p>
<p>Apportion a part of your time to those who have special needs, making yourself free to attend to them personally, sitting with them in a public assembly in all due humility before God, your Creator. Keep your soldiers, guards, and officers away from them so that they may speak to you without inhibition, for I heard the Messenger of God <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span> say, on more than one occasion: A nation in which the rights of the weak are not wrested in an uninhibited manner from the strong will never be blessed. Bear patiently any coarseness or inarticulate expression they might show; do not display irritation or disdain toward them, and God will thereby extend His compassion to you in its fullest measure and make the reward for your obedience to Him binding. Give whatever you give with beneficence, and withhold, if this be unavoidable, with grace and apology.</p>
<p>There are certain affairs which you must handle personally, among them replying to your officers when your scribes are incapable of doing so, and issuing responses to the requests of people when they are brought to you in person because your aides find themselves at a loss on their account.</p>
<p>Perform each day the task proper to it, for to each day belongs a particular task. Set apart the finest portion of your available time, and the greatest share thereof, for your soul and for what is between you and God, even though all times and all actions within them are for God, provided the intention underlying them is good and your subjects derive security as a consequence. Let your observance of those duties (faraid, sing. farida) relating exclusively to God be the special means by which you purify your religion for Him. Give unto God of your vital energy in your nights and your days, and perform fully that by which you draw near to God, doing so perfectly, without becoming dull or deficient, taking your body to its limits.</p>
<p>When you lead the people in prayer, do so without repelling them or squandering it, for among them are people with infirmities or special needs. Indeed, I asked the Messenger of God <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ﷺ</span>, when he sent me as his representative to Yemen, How should I lead them in prayer? He replied: Lead them according to the prayer performed by the weakest among them, and be merciful to the believers.</p>
<p>In addition to all of this, do not prolong any period of absence from your subjects, for the isolation of governors from those they govern creates a kind of constriction and breeds deficiency in awareness of their affairs. Such isolation cuts rulers off from acquiring knowledge of things hidden from them, so that what is great comes to appear small and what is small, great; the beautiful appears ugly and the ugly, beautiful; the true becomes mixed with the false. The governor is but a human being: he cannot know what people conceal from him, and truth bears no visible mark by which sincere expressions may be distinguished from false ones.</p>
<p>You can only be one of two kinds of men: either a man who gives himself generously for the sake of the truth, in which case, why seclude yourself, thereby preventing yourself from bestowing a necessary right or performing an honorable deed? Or else you are a man afflicted with an unwillingness to give of himself, in which case, how quickly people will refrain from making requests of you, despairing of your generosity! And this, despite the fact that fulfilling most of the needs people bring to you is not burdensome, whether they be complaints against injustice or demands for fairness in transactions.</p>
<p>The governor has favorites and intimate companions, and among them some are prone to presumptuousness, arrogance, and unfairness in their dealings. Cut this out at the root by eliminating the causes of these vices, and by granting no landed estates to anyone in your entourage or circle of friends. Give them no reason to hope for such a grant; should they receive one, it would harm those living nearby in their access to water and in any shared undertaking, the burden of which would inevitably fall on the neighbors while the profit went to those holding the estate, not to you. What would fall to you is only the blame (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَيْبٌ</span>) for it, in this world and the next. Impose what is right upon all to whom it applies, whether they are close to you or distant. Be steadfast and vigilant in this, regardless of how it weighs upon your intimates and favorites. Always seek the end that this principle leads to, however heavily the path may lie upon you, for its outcome is praiseworthy.</p>
<p>If any of your subjects suspect you of an injustice, present your case openly so that the suspicion is cleared away from you by plain explanation. Such conduct is a discipline for yourself, a kindness to your subjects, and a means of pleading your cause that will help you keep them upright in adherence to the truth.</p>
<p>Never turn away from a call to peace with your enemy when God''s pleasure lies in it, for in peace there is rest for your soldiers, relief from your cares, and security for your lands. Yet once you have made peace, remain fully on your guard against your enemy, for he may have drawn close only in order to catch you off guard; so take every precaution, and hold even your own favorable opinion of him under suspicion. Yet if you and your enemy enter into a solemn agreement, or he receives from you a covenant of protection (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِمَّةٌ</span>), then keep your agreement faithfully and honor your obligation of protection with complete trustworthiness. Make your very life a shield for what you have promised, for among all the obligations God has laid down there is none on which people stand more firmly united, despite all the differences of their inclinations and the variety of their opinions, than the honoring of pledges. Even the polytheists observed it among themselves, quite apart from their dealings with the Muslims, so greatly did they fear the consequences of treachery. Do not betray your covenant of protection, do not break your promise, and do not deal treacherously with your enemy, for none dares defy God but the ignorant and wretched (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلٌ شَقِيٌّ</span>). God has made His pledge and His protection a sanctuary of security, which by His mercy He has spread over His servants, a refuge in whose inviolable strength they find rest, and toward whose sheltering power they hasten. Let there be no corruption within it, no treachery, and no deception. Do not enter into any agreement that leaves room for evasion, and do not resort to twisting words once an agreement has been confirmed and made binding. Do not let any hardship that befalls you drive you to break, without right, an agreement God has made binding upon you; for patience under a difficulty whose relief and good outcome you await is far better than a treachery whose consequence you dread, that a reckoning from God (tilba) should close in upon you from which you will find no release in this world or the next.</p>
<p>Beware of shedding blood unlawfully, for nothing so swiftly invites retribution, weighs more heavily in its consequence, or is more apt to end a blessing and cut short a span of life, than bloodshed without right. On the Day of Resurrection, God, glorified be He, will begin His judgment among His servants with the blood they have shed. Do not seek to strengthen your authority through unlawful bloodshed, for that in truth weakens and undermines it; indeed, it brings it down and takes it away entirely.</p>
<p>You have no excuse before God, nor before me, if you kill anyone deliberately, for that demands capital punishment. And if you are overtaken by an error, and your whip, your sword, or your hand goes too far in inflicting a penalty, for even a single blow, and whatever exceeds it, can bring about death, do not let the pride of your authority hold you back from paying the relatives of the slain what is rightfully theirs.</p>
<p>Beware of self-admiration, of leaning on whatever pleases you in yourself, and of loving to be flattered, for these are among the surest openings Satan finds to efface the good works (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">احسان</span>) of the virtuous (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المُحسنین</span>). Beware of making your subjects feel indebted to you for the good you do them, of inflating your own deeds, and of promising what you then fail to fulfill; for laying people under obligation destroys the good itself, self-praise extinguishes the light of truth, and breaking a promise earns you the hatred of God and of men. God the Exalted has said:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">As-Saff: 3</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَبُرَ مَقْتًا عِندَ ٱللَّهِ أَن تَقُولُوا۟ مَا لَا تَفْعَلُونَ</span></p><p class="tr">It is indeed hateful to God that you say that which you do not do (As-Saff: 3).</p></blockquote>
<p>Beware of rushing into matters before their time has come, and of letting them slip when the moment to act has arrived; of stubbornness when they grow tangled, and of slackness once they have become clear. Put every matter in its proper place, and attend to every task at its proper time. Beware of taking for yourself what belongs equally to all, and of feigning blindness to what is urgent and plain to every eye, for it will be taken from you for the benefit of others. Before long the coverings will be lifted from all affairs, and justice will be sought from you on behalf of the wronged. So master the heat of your pride, the sharpness of your temper, the force of your hand, and the edge of your tongue; guard against all of this by holding back sudden impulse and delaying the blow until your anger has subsided and you have regained your self-command. You will never achieve this mastery over yourself, however, unless you increase your remembrance of your return to your Lord.</p>
<p>It is incumbent upon you to remember the just governance and the noble custom that have come before you, the practice of the Prophet, and the obligations set forth in the Book of God. Follow faithfully what you have seen us do in these matters, and strive with all your soul to act upon what has been enjoined upon you in this mandate, by which the plea has been made complete against you, so that you will have no excuse when your soul races toward its caprice.</p>
<p>I ask God, by the vastness of His mercy and the greatness of His power to grant every desire, that He bestow upon me and upon you the success that wins His pleasure, that we may present a clear justification before Him and before His creatures, earn the fairest praise among His servants, and leave behind the most beautiful traces in the land, with blessing made complete and honor ever increased; and that He seal my life and yours with felicity (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلسَّعَادَةُ</span>) and martyrdom (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلشَّهَادَةُ</span>). Truly:</p>
<blockquote class="quran k-quran"><span class="q-band q-band--quran"><span class="q-orn" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="q-kind">Al-Baqarah: 156</span></span><p class="ar is-quranic" dir="rtl" lang="ar"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِينَ إِذَآ أَصَبَتْهُم مُّصِيبَةٌۭ قَالُوٓا۟ إِنَّا لِلَّهِ وَإِنَّآ إِلَيْهِ رَجِعُونَ</span></p></blockquote>
<p>Those who, when a misfortune befalls them, say: Truly unto God we belong, and unto Him we are returning (Al-Baqarah: 156).</p>
<p>And peace be upon the Messenger of God and upon his good and pure progeny.</p>
<blockquote class="aside editorial"><p><strong>Editorial note (tradition-grounded).</strong> When the letter tells Malik to hold himself back from &quot;whatever is not lawful to you,&quot; the word behind <em>lawful</em> is <em>halal</em>, whose root (h-l-l) means to untie or loosen a knot: the permitted is what has been unknotted — loosed from prohibition. The wider tradition preserves this sense, and it throws light on the letter''s own image a few lines later, where the governor is told to &quot;loosen from the people every knot of resentment&quot; and cut the cord of enmity from his own heart. A just ruler, on this reading, works as the law itself works — not by binding tighter but by untying: releasing what may be released, unknotting grievance where grievance can be unknotted, and leaving bound only what must remain forbidden.</p></blockquote>' WHERE slug = 'spiritual-ethos' AND anchor_key = 'the letter to malik al-ashtar';
